P.transport["npc:agathelos-the-raging"] = {
  hash = "45101d",
  fullHash = "144ea9",
  text = "Agathelos the Raging. Deep within Razorfen Kraul, Agathelos the Raging is held among the thorned tunnels. The quilboar revere Agamaggan as their god and progenitor, and Agathelos’s great size carries spiritual meaning. His armor and fury make him one of the most dangerous beasts beneath the southern Barrens. Agamaggan, the titanic boar, is god and progenitor to the quilboar. So among the Razorfen tribes, a beast as large as Agathelos carries deep spiritual meaning. He stands roughly twice as tall as a Barrens thunder lizard. The quilboar keep Agathelos from roaming freely through the Kraul. Several guards maintain a field before the short, dead-end tunnel where he is penned. Intruders who break through that barrier face a raging beast, made more fearsome still by his fury.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_agathelos-the-raging-7ec14892492078f7aa2f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.1142857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:aggem-thorncurse"] = {
  hash = "520730",
  fullHash = "520730",
  text = "Aggem Thorncurse. In Razorfen Kraul, Aggem Thorncurse bears the title Death's Head Prophet. When threatened, he calls a boar spirit to his aid. Aggem guards part of Razorfen Kraul, where the Death's Head tribe dwells. His shamanic power lets him call a boar spirit into battle. The spirit reflects the quilboar's reverence for Agamaggan.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_aggem-thorncurse-2e4e2d6221bcadc4959b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 22.0734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:aku-mai"] = {
  hash = "ef0c18",
  fullHash = "ef0c18",
  text = "Aku'mai. At Blackfathom Deeps, Aku'mai haunts the sunken temple of Elune along the Zoram Strand. An Old God's corruption tainted its sacred Moon Well, and Aku'mai rose from the waters as the Old God's servant. The Twilight's Hammer sacrifices innocents to Aku'mai for power. Blackfathom Deeps was once a great temple of Elune. Then an Old God's corruption tainted its sacred Moon Well, and Aku'mai rose from the waters. The gargantuan hydra serves the Old God. Now Aku'mai lairs in the drowned ruins of the night elves' temple, poisoning the waters along the Zoram Strand with its malice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_aku-mai-cf195293fc3df076644e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.5567347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:arcanist-doan"] = {
  hash = "4aad1b",
  fullHash = "4aad1b",
  text = "Arcanist Doan. Within the Scarlet Monastery, Arcanist Doan guards the Athenaeum and its archives. A master human mage, he brings deadly arcane skill to the Scarlet Crusade. The Scarlet Crusade trusts martial fury and holy fervor. Doan believes arcane power is vital to driving the Scourge from Lordaeron. Deep in the Monastery's library, he keeps its lore and guards the Scarlet Key. That key secures Crusade sanctuaries across the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_arcanist-doan-270494d301f913b3462b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 30.3804082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:baron-aquanis"] = {
  hash = "9f9f9b",
  fullHash = "9f9f9b",
  text = "Baron Aquanis. In the flooded Moonshrine Ruins of Blackfathom Deeps, Baron Aquanis rises from the depths when the Fathom Stone is disturbed. The Twilight's Hammer cultists occupy the ruins, and the Earthen Ring suspects the baron has been corrupted. Beneath the flooded halls of the Moonshrine Ruins lies the Fathom Stone, surrounded by Twilight's Hammer cultists. A fathom core rests within it. Read properly, the device reveals the history of nearby elemental activity. Je'neu Sancrea of the Earthen Ring sends Horde adventurers to recover it. Disturb the stone, and Baron Aquanis rises from the depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_baron-aquanis-104abebea0c6c8e65b5c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.0457143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:baron-silverlaine"] = {
  hash = "8c96b4",
  fullHash = "7404d7",
  text = "Baron Silverlaine. In the Great Hall above Pyrewood Village, Baron Silverlaine's spirit still lingers. He ruled Silverlaine Keep until Archmage Arugal unleashed worgen upon the region, and they slaughtered him. Baron Silverlaine ruled a noble estate in the southern hills of Silverpine. His fortress guarded the lands around it and kept them safe. Then Archmage Arugal summoned worgen to fight the rampaging Scourge and unleashed them upon the region. The feral beasts could not be controlled. They stormed the stronghold, overwhelmed its guards, and butchered the baron. Baron Silverlaine remains bound to the keep's ancient Great Hall. His restless ghost wanders among the wrecked dining tables where he once feasted. The cursed lord attacks intruders who enter his former home.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_baron-silverlaine-f22a8114256e2d2bb017/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.3004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bazil-thredd"] = {
  hash = "2861ed",
  fullHash = "152fd5",
  text = "Bazil Thredd. In the eastern cell block, Bazil Thredd leads the Defias inmates in revolt. Edwin VanCleef's lieutenant has been held in the Stormwind Stockade since the Stonemasons' riots. Bazil Thredd stood beside Edwin VanCleef when the Stonemasons Guild rose against Stormwind's nobility. The guild had been denied payment for rebuilding the city, and VanCleef rejected the government's attempts to appease them. Open revolt followed.\n\nThredd served as his chief lieutenant and most trusted assistant. The city guard captured Thredd in the clashes that followed. VanCleef and the surviving masons fled to Westfall and formed the Defias Brotherhood. Thredd was sent to the Stormwind Stockade. A prison riot has overwhelmed the guards and opened the cells. Now the Stockade is an armed fortress beneath the Mage Quarter. Thredd commands the imprisoned Defias bandits, insurgents, and allied cutthroats from a post in the eastern wing. An ogre named Hamhock guards him. For years Thredd stayed silent behind bars, seemingly forsaken. Now Warden Thelwater wants him dead, hoping to break the rebellion's leadership before the chaos reaches the city streets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bazil-thredd-5ccda273a0fe47d1edb1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.6130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bazzalan"] = {
  hash = "4ba4c4",
  fullHash = "b5a735",
  text = "Bazzalan. Beneath Orgrimmar, Bazzalan directs the Searing Blade cult's work in Ragefire Chasm. He shares its leadership with Jergosh the Invoker. Bazzalan commands the Searing Blade's field forces in the lava caverns beneath Orgrimmar. While Thrall works to establish the Orcs' new home across Durotar, the satyr rogue moves through the winding caverns with twin blades, swift and lethal. From a volcanic ledge above the central chamber, Bazzalan watches over the cultist guards. Below him, Jergosh the Invoker conducts demonic rituals. Like all satyrs, Bazzalan's deepest loyalty belongs to demonic masters. He uses poison, quick strikes, and stealth against Horde scouts who venture near the cult's staging ground.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bazzalan-082d01cfad6275ef0b23/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.4122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:blind-hunter"] = {
  hash = "c80801",
  fullHash = "43819d",
  text = "Blind Hunter. In the deepest reaches of Razorfen Kraul, the Blind Hunter roosts beyond the common bats. This pale albino bat prowls the quilboar redoubt, far from the cavern entrances. Razorfen Kraul winds through briars and dark caverns below ground. Common bats roost nearer the surface, where faint light reaches from outside. The Blind Hunter keeps to the furthest, darkest alcoves of the tangle. Its pale hide and milky gaze may come from generations spent apart from natural sunlight. The Blind Hunter is thought to have lost most or all of its ordinary sight. Scholars and scouts believe it tracks prey in the gloom with keen hearing and echolocation. It appears rarely, hunting only now and then among the dense thickets. For cavern explorers, a sighting of this phantom beast is rare.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_blind-hunter-ae2e4d7d9c514b604c6f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.2146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bloodmage-thalnos"] = {
  hash = "ac4634",
  fullHash = "cc0e01",
  text = "Bloodmage Thalnos. In the Scarlet Monastery's Graveyard, Bloodmage Thalnos haunts the tombs, hungry for vengeance. The plague of undeath claimed him, and he remains deep within the Crusade's holy grounds. No record tells who Thalnos was in life. The plague of undeath claimed him, and he rose as a withered skeleton. Still, he clings to fiery magic and arcane mastery. He cries out for vengeance and more souls. From the deepest corner of the monastery's courtyard tombs, Thalnos commands apparitions and shambling undead minions. He attacks anyone who disturbs his domain. Beneath the Crusade's sacred halls, his presence is a bitter contradiction. He embodies the horror they swore to eradicate.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bloodmage-thalnos-e1275abbf0094425684e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.557551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:bruegal-ironknuckle"] = {
  hash = "300765",
  fullHash = "d9e771",
  text = "Bruegal Ironknuckle. In the Stormwind Stockade, Bruegal Ironknuckle prowls the halls during an open revolt. The dangerous dwarf is a powerful brawler, and anyone trying to restore order may face his fists. Bruegal Ironknuckle prowls the Stormwind Stockade during its open revolt. The dwarf is known for his brutal strength in a brawl. Many of the city's most volatile cutthroats have seized the underground cellblocks, and Bruegal is among them. He leaves his cell and roams the halls, ready to bash anyone trying to quell the uprising. Bruegal Ironknuckle is among the rogues and outlaws held deep beneath Stormwind. The Defias Brotherhood and its ringleaders direct much of the revolt, while inmates like Bruegal show that the prison holds criminals from across the eastern realms. No record here tells what crimes brought him there. His fierce resilience makes him elusive and one of the deadliest lone inmates in the corridors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_bruegal-ironknuckle-25ce18be20a9532645c4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.4318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:captain-greenskin"] = {
  hash = "2cef2b",
  fullHash = "3f3e66",
  text = "Captain Greenskin. In the Deadmines cove, Captain Greenskin readies a warship for Edwin VanCleef. The goblin pirate directs its crew and construction for the Defias Brotherhood, preparing the ironclad juggernaut to sail against Stormwind. Captain Greenskin paces the main deck of the juggernaut under construction in the Deadmines cove. A seasoned goblin mariner, he joined Edwin VanCleef's inner circle to turn an underground cavern into a drydock for a warship. Captain Greenskin commands cutthroats, not stonemasons or bandits. His crew includes Mr. Smite, his fearsome tauren first mate, and Cookie, the murloc ship's cook. Together, they hold the gangplanks and upper deck, guarding the way to VanCleef's private cabin atop the bridge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_captain-greenskin-c096799857054c7934f9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.0016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:charlga-razorflank"] = {
  hash = "94d02e",
  fullHash = "94d02e",
  text = "Charlga Razorflank. In Razorfen Kraul, Charlga Razorflank leads the Razorfen quilboar. They drove the tauren from the southern Barrens, claiming to fight for their fallen god, Agamaggan. Charlga Razorflank rules from the tangled brambles of Razorfen Kraul. Her people honor her as a master geomancer, and none among the quilboar can equal her experimental magic. She bound her fractious kin into a deadly force. With primal earth and giant thorns grown from the remains of Agamaggan, the fallen demigod, Charlga shaped the Kraul into a fortress few can enter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_charlga-razorflank-b70c8daa648e9012ac84/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.362449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:commander-springvale"] = {
  hash = "e44ff4",
  fullHash = "085e7a",
  text = "Commander Springvale. At Shadowfang Keep, Commander Springvale's spirit still guards the halls where he fell. Once a devoted paladin and commander, he was slain with his men by Arugal's worgen and now haunts the castle. Commander Springvale led the garrison at Shadowfang Keep in Silverpine Forest. Before the keep fell into ruin, he was an honorable paladin devoted to the Holy Light. After Lordaeron fell to the Scourge, he kept watch over the fortress through those troubled days. Archmage Arugal summoned worgen from beyond this world to strengthen Dalaran's failing ranks against the undead Scourge. The wild beasts soon escaped his control and descended on Shadowfang Keep. They slaughtered Baron Silverlaine and his entire guard. Springvale died in the halls he had sworn to protect, his service cut short. Death did not release Springvale. His ghost haunts the keep's council room alongside spectral soldiers. He still wields paladin powers against intruders, including Holy Light and Hammer of Justice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_commander-springvale-abf9bd241bcca39fd789/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.5338776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:cookie"] = {
  hash = "59d851",
  fullHash = "9a967c",
  text = "Cookie. Beneath the juggernaut in the Deadmines, Cookie tends the crew's cauldrons. A murloc in the Defias Brotherhood, he cooks under Captain Greenskin and Edwin VanCleef. In the underground dry dock, his post is the only one a visitor may pass by. Cookie cooks beneath the decks of the juggernaut at Ironclad Cove. No human outrider or goblin engineer takes the duty; the solitary murloc does. No one knows how he came to hold the post. The crew may doubt a murloc's cooking, but pirates rarely choose their meals with care. In Moonbrook's underworld, a meal is a meal. Cookie patrols the waterways and shores around the pirate flagship. He fights with cooking utensils and throws acidic brews. Though apart from typical murloc tribes, he defends his cauldron with savage force, hurling caustic mixtures at intruders in the docks. His station lies away from the main gangplanks, so many who storm the cove pass his kitchen by. Cookie is the only overseer aboard the Defias juggernaut who can be bypassed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_cookie-c69099b11736d3804979/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.4628571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dark-iron-ambassador"] = {
  hash = "e11f56",
  fullHash = "e11f56",
  text = "Dark Iron Ambassador. Deep in Gnomeregan, the Dark Iron Ambassador moves among the ruined halls as an envoy of his clan. Why the clan has come to the fallen gnome capital remains unclear. In Gnomeregan’s irradiated halls, the Dark Iron Ambassador speaks for his clan. The purpose of his mission is unknown.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dark-iron-ambassador-212f1a151a8832707c72/1-0000.mp3", duration = 21.2636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dextren-ward"] = {
  hash = "7bb011",
  fullHash = "49880a",
  text = "Dextren Ward. In the Stormwind Stockade, Dextren Ward remains a prisoner during a bloody uprising. Before his capture, he dug up bodies at Raven Hill Cemetery in Duskwood and sold them to the necromancer Morbent Fel. Darkshire's town council has marked him for death. Before chains bound him beneath Stormwind, Dextren Ward robbed graves in Duskwood for profit. At Raven Hill Cemetery, he dug up the newly buried dead. He sold the bodies to local practitioners of dark arts. Morbent Fel, a necromancer, was Ward's main customer. His rituals plagued Duskwood and swelled the undead ranks. By supplying him with bodies, Ward helped spread terror through Darkshire.\n\nStormwind authorities eventually captured Ward and brought him to the Stockade. The people of Duskwood demanded justice for his crimes. Councilman Millstipe called for Ward's hand as proof. A rebellion swept through the Stockade, and Ward was freed from his cell in the chaos. Armed and vicious, he seized control of his corner of the prison. Now he uses fear to keep intruders away. Alliance forces seeking to restore order count him among their priority targets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dextren-ward-6b444bbe7a3276643c56/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.4416327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:edwin-vancleef"] = {
  hash = "480462",
  fullHash = "3cf326",
  text = "Edwin VanCleef. In the Deadmines, Edwin VanCleef commands the Defias Brotherhood. He once led the Stonemasons who rebuilt Stormwind City after the Second War. The House of Nobles denied the workers their promised pay, and VanCleef now seeks vengeance against the kingdom he helped restore. Edwin VanCleef led the Stonemasons Guild in rebuilding Stormwind City after the First War. A peerless craftsman and idealist, he mastered architecture and directed the work on the capital's grand ramparts and towering cathedrals. His childhood friend Mathias Shaw taught him stealth, subterfuge, thievery, and bladecraft. VanCleef became a deadly operative as well as a master architect. After years of labor, the Stonemasons finished rebuilding Stormwind. The House of Nobles had run up great debts through military campaigns and refused the guild its promised pay. VanCleef demanded restitution, but the nobles dissolved the guild.\n\nHe led the workers in a riot and out of the city. In the chaos, his lieutenant and most trusted assistant, Bazil Thredd, was captured and left in the Stockade. In Westfall, VanCleef gathered the exiled workers and allied outcasts beneath the Defias Brotherhood's banner.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_edwin-vancleef-4c0bf57991713de0a1db/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.0685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:electrocutioner-6000"] = {
  hash = "c648ee",
  fullHash = "beffc7",
  text = "Electrocutioner 6000. In Gnomeregan's Launch Bay, Electrocutioner 6000 guards the upper platform. The spider tank fights for Mekgineer Thermaplugg and the irradiated leper gnomes who follow him. After Gnomeregan fell, Mekgineer Thermaplugg and the irradiated leper gnomes who follow him took control of the city's war machines. Electrocutioner 6000 guards the Launch Bay's upper platform. It strikes intruders with high-voltage current. Electrocutioner 6000 guards the Workshop Key, which opens Gnomeregan's back door into its workshops. Bring down the machine, and the key gives you a quicker route into the city's depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_electrocutioner-6000-00f0f87f5d5de0e190cd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.9004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:fenrus-the-devourer"] = {
  hash = "20a690",
  fullHash = "a6d01d",
  text = "Fenrus the Devourer. In Shadowfang Keep, Fenrus the Devourer guards Archmage Arugal's upper sanctum. The enormous worg is the archmage's pet, swollen by unnatural magic. Anyone seeking Arugal's study must face beast and master. Fenrus the Devourer prowls outside Archmage Arugal's private chambers, deep within Shadowfang Keep. The worg is no rampart worgen. Swollen to enormous size, Fenrus stalks among the shelves, scrolls, and arcane tools in Arugal's study. The archmage keeps the beast as both pet and guard against anyone who comes seeking his head. Dark magic has saturated Shadowfang Keep since Baron Silverlaine's downfall, and Fenrus bears its mark. Archmage Arugal or his unleashed worgen warped the native predator through arcane or shadowy experiments. Now Fenrus is a ravenous juggernaut, so feral and bound by magic that no hunter can charm or tame him.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_fenrus-the-devourer-903adb66aaf29aacc213/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.9322449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gelihast"] = {
  hash = "b12b85",
  fullHash = "64ac62",
  text = "Gelihast. Within Blackfathom Deeps, Gelihast guards an altar in the flooded Pool of Ask'ar. His Blindlight murlocs gather around him in the sunken temple. Deep beneath the Zoram Strand, Gelihast has made his lair in Blackfathom Deeps. Most murlocs keep to the coastal shallows, but he strikes intruders with twin swords. He moves too quickly for many to react, and casters who come within his reach rarely survive. Gelihast rules the Pool of Ask'ar with his Blindlight brood. At the first sign of trouble, they swarm to his side. Beyond his lair stands an altar. Those who defeat him and touch it receive the Blessing of Blackfathom.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gelihast-6f480ccb5c05437ef74c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.6726531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:ghamoo-ra-classic"] = {
  hash = "502e9a",
  fullHash = "13f054",
  text = "Ghamoo-ra. In Blackfathom Deeps, Ghamoo-ra guards a small island in the Pool of Ask'ar. The ancient giant turtle tramples anyone who draws near. Blackfathom Deeps was once a night elven temple to Elune. Now flooded, its ruins belong to naga, satyr, and the nihilistic Twilight's Hammer cult. Ghamoo-ra dwells among them, his enormous, thick shell turning aside all but the strongest blows. Lesser turtles gather around Ghamoo-ra on a small island in the Pool of Ask'ar, yet they leave travelers alone. Ghamoo-ra attacks anyone who comes near. Beneath his trampling bulk, they are crushed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_ghamoo-ra-classic-3626649e3a717ecd0505/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.6016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gilnid"] = {
  hash = "9b4adb",
  fullHash = "9b4adb",
  text = "Gilnid. Deep in the Deadmines, Gilnid directs the Defias Brotherhood's goblin forges. Known as the Smelter, he oversees the work that turns raw ore into refined metal and munitions for Edwin VanCleef's hidden ironclad juggernaut. His foundry produces the heavy cannons the Brotherhood needs to strike Stormwind. Deep in the Deadmines, the Defias Brotherhood relies on the foundry for weapons of war. Gilnid, VanCleef's head smelter, commands goblin laborers, tinkers, and smiths there. Taller than most of his kin, he drives them through smoke and slag, barking orders and allowing no breaks. Their work turns raw iron ore into heavy plating and deadly ordnance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gilnid-1737ff1cee5a4f6214dd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.1020408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:hamhock"] = {
  hash = "3cd297",
  fullHash = "e40e55",
  text = "Hamhock. In the Stormwind Stockade, Hamhock remains among the rioting prisoners. The massive two-headed ogre is one of the prison’s most brutal inmates. Hamhock is a towering two-headed ogre held in the Stormwind Stockade. The prison was built for petty thieves, murderers, and political dissidents. So his size makes him a fearsome presence among the cells. When prisoners seized control, Hamhock remained amid the disorder and crushed anyone who entered his cellblock. No record tells how Hamhock was captured or why he was locked among Stormwind’s thieves and dissidents. Whatever brought him to the Stockade, he is now one of its most dangerous inmates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_hamhock-ac75e5ac957d5b182e0f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.6130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:herod"] = {
  hash = "645865",
  fullHash = "742f25",
  text = "Herod. In the Scarlet Monastery's Armory, Herod trains the Crusade's melee fighters. A renowned champion, he commands the Armory and wages the Crusade's holy war with fierce skill and zeal. Alliance dissidents and the Horde's rulers in Undercity seek his death to weaken the order. Herod commands the Armory within the Scarlet Monastery. Unlike the spellcasters and paladins in the Cathedral, he relies on physical strength. Clad in mail and bearing a massive two-handed axe, he trains cohorts of Scarlet Trainees. He teaches them to fight with fury and destroy anyone beyond the Crusade's cause. Herod oversees the weapons and combat training of the Crusade's front-line warriors. Raleigh the Devout, an ex-Crusader disillusioned with the order's madness, lives in Southshore and calls for Herod's death to break its military leadership. In the Undercity, the dreadlord Varimathras also seeks Herod's execution. He wants the dangerous human stronghold cleared from the border of Forsaken territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_herod-3b8d6904c433d10ec796/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.3395918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:high-inquisitor-fairbanks"] = {
  hash = "f63e74",
  fullHash = "f63e74",
  text = "High Inquisitor Fairbanks. In the Scarlet Crusade, High Inquisitor Fairbanks's fate remains bound to the order's dark descent. Before Lordaeron fell, he was a revered priest and a trusted advisor to Highlord Alexandros Mograine. Before Lordaeron fell, Fairbanks was a respected priest of the Knights of the Silver Hand. Highlord Alexandros Mograine trusted him above all others. Mograine bore the Ashbringer. Fairbanks stood beside Mograine, High General Abbendis and Grand Inquisitor Isillien when they founded the Scarlet Crusade, an order sworn to destroy the Scourge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_high-inquisitor-fairbanks-a9eeaeb5b843209f5c63/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.4408163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:houndmaster-loksey"] = {
  hash = "6ab590",
  fullHash = "e31d7a",
  text = "Houndmaster Loksey. In the Scarlet Monastery's Library wing, Houndmaster Loksey keeps the Crusade's war dogs ready. He breeds and trains them to hunt undead, intruders, and those the Crusade judges heretics. His harsh discipline has made the Monastery's beasts feared across Tirisfal Glades. Houndmaster Loksey trains vicious dogs to track and attack. The Scarlet Crusade sends them to sniff out plague-tainted corpses, uncover hidden infiltrators, and run down survivors fleeing the zealots' wrath. Loksey commands the hounds guarding the Library wing. They stand ready to tear intruders limb from limb. Loksey's brother, Huntsman Leopold, serves the Scarlet Crusade as its envoy to the Argent Dawn at Light's Hope Chapel. In private, Leopold thinks Loksey can barely manage his hounds, let alone handle high-stakes diplomacy. Yet Loksey remains a key officer in the Monastery's inner hierarchy.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_houndmaster-loksey-e11b98644c0faffb193d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.4620408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:interrogator-vishas"] = {
  hash = "18ace5",
  fullHash = "18ace5",
  text = "Interrogator Vishas. Deep in the Scarlet Monastery, Interrogator Vishas has captives at his mercy. The Scarlet Crusade’s master of torment takes pleasure in breaking prisoners and testing mortal flesh. In the Scarlet Monastery’s crypts and burial grounds, Interrogator Vishas tends the Crusade’s captives. Outside, zealots march in gleaming armor beneath banners of purity. Inside, Vishas reveals the cruelty behind their righteous fury.\n\nInterrogation serves the Crusade’s need for intelligence, and Vishas prizes it as a craft. He revels in the agony of Forsaken captives, captured Scourge, and living travelers branded heretics.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_interrogator-vishas-71cbb21997fc5a2f99ee/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.3444898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jergosh-the-invoker"] = {
  hash = "0b521c",
  fullHash = "0b521c",
  text = "Jergosh the Invoker. Beneath Orgrimmar, Jergosh the Invoker leads a cult against the Horde. In Ragefire Chasm, he turns to demonic arts while Thrall guides the orcs toward honor and shamanism. Jergosh gathers the Searing Blade beneath Orgrimmar. Thrall leads the orcs toward honor and shamanism, but Jergosh turns to demonic power. In Ragefire Chasm, he draws followers into a sect aligned with the remnants of the Shadow Council. From beneath the Horde capital, the cult works against the path the new Horde has chosen.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jergosh-the-invoker-bab39c6fffd12341ce7e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.6791837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:kam-deepfury"] = {
  hash = "c934aa",
  fullHash = "d085b1",
  text = "Kam Deepfury. In Stormwind's Stockade, Kam Deepfury waits apart from the Defias rebels. Captured after arranging the blast that shattered the Thandol Span far to the north, the Dark Iron dwarf is held far from his kin. Kam Deepfury is a Dark Iron dwarf held deep in the Stormwind Stockade. Most prisoners there are disgruntled Stonemasons or Defias rebels, but he is neither. Captured after an operation crippled vital northern infrastructure, he remains cut off from his kin beneath Blackrock Mountain. Like the rebels sharing the corridors, he is hostile to the Alliance. Alliance intelligence at Dun Modr, near the Wetlands' northern border, named Kam Deepfury as the chief architect of the blast that shattered the Thandol Span. The explosion severed one of the massive stone bridges between the Wetlands and the Arathi Highlands. It crippled a vital trade and military route for Ironforge and the Alliance. It also killed a kin of Longbraid the Grim, the dwarf commander.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_kam-deepfury-5c393df808cdbd92b320/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.3804082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lady-anacondra"] = {
  hash = "bce7b5",
  fullHash = "34bec2",
  text = "Lady Anacondra. Deep in the Wailing Caverns, Lady Anacondra holds to the Druids of the Fang. She followed Naralex in his effort to heal the Barrens, but his communion with the Emerald Dream became a nightmare. It shattered her mind and turned her devotion toward remaking the land in the dream’s image. Lady Anacondra followed Naralex into the caverns as he sought the Barrens’ subterranean springs through the Emerald Dream. Then dark visions seized his mind, and the nightmare spread from the dreaming realm to his disciples. Her sanity broke. Her hope for the land became a cold, reptilian obsession. Lady Anacondra guards the Wailing Caverns’ first chambers. She and the other Fanglords have cast aside the Cenarion Circle’s teachings and embraced the viper’s vicious ways. She wields lightning and nature magic, and keeps Naralex in his twisted sleep with slumbering trances. Above, the nightmare’s corruption still threatens the Barrens.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lady-anacondra-02854d87035bb7d54b22/1-0000.mp3", duration = 56.7118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lady-sarevess"] = {
  hash = "44831b",
  fullHash = "04b0f2",
  text = "Lady Sarevess. In Blackfathom Deeps, Lady Sarevess works dark sorceries among the flooded ruins. Lady Sarevess performs rituals in a flooded grotto within the Pool of Ask'ar. The Twilight's Hammer cultists haunt the sunken temple as well. They pursue their own worship deeper in the cavern. Lady Sarevess commands elemental currents and crackling lightning. Loyal naga sentinels and summoned water elementals guard her sanctuary. She fights intruders who threaten her dark work.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lady-sarevess-f9a01b6703f49946797d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.1902041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-cobrahn"] = {
  hash = "b6cfd1",
  fullHash = "f8a065",
  text = "Lord Cobrahn. Deep in the Wailing Caverns, Lord Cobrahn commands the corrupted Druids of the Fang. He was a night elf druid in Naralex's order, until the nightmare in the cavern springs claimed him. Now he guards the twisted labyrinth in a deadly serpent guise. Lord Cobrahn once served Naralex in his druid order. When Naralex entered the Wailing Caverns beneath the Barrens, he hoped to draw upon the underground springs and restore life to the arid wastes. Cobrahn went with him. But Naralex's communion with the Emerald Dream decayed into a twisting nightmare, and Cobrahn's mind shattered with those of his brothers. The nightmare warped Cobrahn's shapeshifting into a predatory reptilian form unlike the totemic beasts of Cenarion tradition. In battle, he takes the guise of a deadly serpent. Venomous deviate pythons flank him, and a vicious claw marks his right hand. Cobrahn rules his part of the grottos and violently purges anyone who tries to break the nightmare's hold on the caverns.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-cobrahn-5c1e2b96a4e7f2ae96c3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.7910204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-pythas"] = {
  hash = "274a76",
  fullHash = "87d92e",
  text = "Lord Pythas. Deep in the Wailing Caverns, Lord Pythas guards Naralex's slumber. Once a night elf warrior in Naralex's personal guard, he now leads the corrupted Druids of the Fang. His skill in battle and serpent magic bar the way to his master. Pythas joined Naralex's personal guard when the druid's expedition entered the Barrens' dry wastes. A seasoned night elf warrior, he studied druidism to help Naralex restore life to the arid realm. Yet discipline and valor could not shield him from the corruption hidden in the caverns' depths. Dark nightmares seized Naralex's connection to the Emerald Dream. Pythas and his fellow acolytes fell under their sway. He forsook his old loyalty and became a leader of the Druids of the Fang.\n\nSome serpent lords favor spells or beast forms. Pythas wields nature magic alongside deadly combat skill. Now he prowls the winding caverns and guards the nightmare's hold on the spring.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-pythas-ba3ca408f58f9ada9b69/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.2383673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lord-serpentis"] = {
  hash = "36c6db",
  fullHash = "3c4abc",
  text = "Lord Serpentis. Deep in the Wailing Caverns, Lord Serpentis leads the Druids of the Fang. Once a mighty druid in Naralex's order, he fell to madness when Naralex's dream soured in the cavern springs. Now he helps command the serpent-worshipping sect near the corrupted Naralex, who lies slumbering. Before the cavern springs darkened, Serpentis held great power and prestige as a druid. He belonged to the noble order led by Naralex. Together, they hoped to heal the Barrens with the springs and the Emerald Dream. But Naralex's vision became a corrupting nightmare, and its rising madness took hold of Serpentis and the other druids. Naralex's twisted dreams spread corruption through his followers. Serpentis and the others became the Druids of the Fang, abandoning Cenarion balance and the totem animals of old. Now they seek to remake the Barrens in the image of their twisted dreams instead of healing it. Serpentis shares leadership with Lady Anacondra, Lord Cobrahn and Lord Pythas.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lord-serpentis-c552b84e9b45d15714ff/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.0595918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:lorgus-jett"] = {
  hash = "6dfe0b",
  fullHash = "6dfe0b",
  text = "Lorgus Jett. In Blackfathom Deeps, Lorgus Jett leads the Twilight's Hammer through flooded ruins. The Horde has marked him as a target. Among the Twilight's Hammer in Blackfathom Deeps, Lorgus Jett is a human. Many cultists there are orcs or tauren, but Jett ranges from the upper Moonshrine Ruins to the tunnels beyond Gelihast, the murloc defiler. He directs the cult's rituals and violent ambitions in the ancient night elf ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_lorgus-jett-f52c415aca9b855b01f0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 28.395102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:miner-johnson"] = {
  hash = "809436",
  fullHash = "a94424",
  text = "Miner Johnson. Deep in the Deadmines, Miner Johnson oversees the digging as a seasoned miner. He works ahead of the heavy machinery, though he appears only now and then. Miner Johnson digs with the laborers beneath the Deadmines, where they work on Edwin VanCleef's hidden fortress. Before the shredders in the Mast Room, he waits in an alcove to the left. He is stronger and more aggressive than the other miners. Since he wanders the tunnels, he can be hard to find. Beneath Moonbrook, the Defias Brotherhood needs ore for weapons, reinforced shafts, and mechanical contraptions. Johnson and the other miners dig the mineral seams beneath Westfall. Anyone who enters his work area finds that he guards it fiercely.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_miner-johnson-040a524fcfb3c451e757/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6955102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:mr-smite"] = {
  hash = "7b17d5",
  fullHash = "d08b82",
  text = "Mr. Smite. At Ironclad Cove, Mr. Smite guards the Defias ship and its crew. The tauren serves Captain Greenskin as first mate and watches the docks where Edwin VanCleef's dreadnaught is being fitted out. Intruders meet his great strength and a changing array of weapons. At Ironclad Cove, Mr. Smite keeps order on the docks as first mate to the goblin Captain Greenskin. Most of the Defias Brotherhood are disaffected humans and goblin tinkerers, but Smite is a battle-hardened tauren from far beyond Kalimdor's plains. He commands a rowdy pirate crew as they fit out Edwin VanCleef's secret dreadnaught. No one may disrupt their work. Mr. Smite is a showman and a versatile weaponmaster, not just a fighter of brute force. He toys with challengers and draws out the fight, leaving them a false hope of victory. When pressed, he stamps the ground hard enough to stun his foes, then searches a nearby chest for another weapon. First comes a quick dueling blade, then twin cleaving reavers, and at last a huge two-handed warhammer.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_mr-smite-0829f40e5e721fcfe812/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.6195918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:mutanus-the-devourer"] = {
  hash = "937dbe",
  fullHash = "937dbe",
  text = "Mutanus the Devourer. In the Wailing Caverns, Mutanus the Devourer haunts Naralex's sleeping mind as a pale, monstrous murloc. The creature emerged from the Emerald Nightmare, and must fall before Naralex can awaken. Naralex, a night elf druid, sought to restore the Barrens by joining its underground springs to the Emerald Dream. Instead, the Emerald Nightmare entered his thoughts. Trapped in an agonizing vision, Naralex's fears took shape through the corruption as an immense, albino murloc.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_mutanus-the-devourer-dbbaacae8a8cb6c35669/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.9216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:naralex"] = {
  hash = "526094",
  fullHash = "cb9e1d",
  text = "Naralex. Beneath the Barrens, Naralex sleeps while his dream of green turns to nightmare. The night elf druid sought to restore the parched land through the Emerald Dream, but corruption overtook his slumber and twisted his followers into venomous fiends. Naralex came to the Barrens with devoted disciples, hoping to restore the forest that covered the plains before the Great Sundering. Beneath the savannah, he found underground springs and rushing geysers. He named the caverns for the howling wind and steam that moaned through the rock. He believed a deep trance could channel the Emerald Dream into those waters, carrying life to the surface and making the wasteland bloom. Deep in the caverns, Naralex entered meditation and reached toward the Emerald Dream. Corruption ensnared his mind instead of the peaceful wild nature he sought. The Emerald Nightmare seized the sleeping druid and made him a conduit for dark energies to enter the living world. Springs turned foul, fungal blooms mutated, and the caverns' beasts became ravenous horrors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_naralex-2b3d704bb5000ca6f3a5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.4628571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:oggleflint"] = {
  hash = "6e56f3",
  fullHash = "c876bb",
  text = "Oggleflint. In Ragefire Chasm beneath Orgrimmar, Oggleflint leads his trogg tribe toward the surface. Near the cavern entrance, he commands his pack as they threaten the Horde capital above. Deep beneath Orgrimmar, Oggleflint and his troggs climbed from fiery tunnels into Ragefire Chasm. Among the molten rock, they carved out a primitive domain and defend it with brutal force. The troggs have little advanced civilization. Oggleflint rules his pack through sheer strength. Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem sent diplomats into the chasm to make peace with the troggs. The troggs turned on them and killed them. Maur Grimtotem, one of the envoys, lies dead in the caverns under Oggleflint’s watch. Near the cavern entrance, Oggleflint waits to strike at outsiders who venture into the dark. Oggleflint’s tribe lives directly beneath Thrall’s capital, a hazard the Horde cannot tolerate. The troggs creep closer to the surface. Their volatile nature threatens to carry them into the Drag and Cleft of Shadow if left undisturbed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_oggleflint-509ebb12e86901292d84/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.2016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:old-serra-kis"] = {
  hash = "4722af",
  fullHash = "6a3a65",
  text = "Old Serra'kis. In the flooded depths beneath the Moonshrine Sanctum, Old Serra'kis waits in Blackfathom Deeps. The enormous threshadon hides in a submerged cave, deadly to anyone who seeks him there. The Twilight's Hammer has made its home in the sunken Moonshrine Ruins. Beneath the Moonshrine Sanctum, dark waters hide older and wilder things. Old Serra'kis, the largest threshadon in the sunken temple, lurks in the Forgotten Pool. Few would think to look in that flooded cave beneath the ruins. You must dive beneath the temple floor to reach Old Serra'kis in the flooded cavern. No air pockets break the water, and the cave is as deadly as the beast within. Cornered in the murk, Serra'kis shows an unnatural resilience. He drains life from his attackers with shadow energy and sustains his own vitality in the darkness.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_old-serra-kis-326fbc2a94c708eb177a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.7077551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:overlord-ramtusk"] = {
  hash = "a42068",
  fullHash = "fcf89c",
  text = "Overlord Ramtusk. Deep in Razorfen Kraul, Overlord Ramtusk rallies the quilboar warriors for battle. He calls for victory and for Agamaggan, and his fall breaks the defenders’ martial strength. Overlord Ramtusk commands the quilboar stronghold deep in Razorfen Kraul, in the southern Barrens. He enters battle crying out to Agamaggan, the boar demigod, and rules the Kraul’s fighters with unyielding authority. Ramtusk directs the quilboar warriors through the Kraul’s winding brambles. He keeps tight discipline among the bristled fighters who defend their tribe’s sanctum. Elite marksmen flank him as he fights on the front line, bellowing rallying shouts and unleashing slams that can break opposing battle lines in seconds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_overlord-ramtusk-9371e52418aed117f8b8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.0016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:rhahk-zor"] = {
  hash = "93df5a",
  fullHash = "25eb2c",
  text = "Rhahk'Zor. In the Deadmines, Rhahk'Zor drives the work forward by force. As head foreman, he keeps Defias laborers and miners in line while they build Edwin VanCleef's underground fortress. The Defias Brotherhood has made an underground fortress of Moonbrook's abandoned gold mines. Rhahk'Zor was appointed head foreman to direct the work. He oversees the crews and laborers as they carve tunnels and build VanCleef's secret projects. Rhahk'Zor keeps order with his strength and an immense stone-headed hammer. Defias diggers and goblin engineers know the towering foreman may strike a crushing blow if they defy him or fall behind schedule.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_rhahk-zor-286e05aa41fc5284f263/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.2359184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:scarlet-commander-mograine"] = {
  hash = "b6dd99",
  fullHash = "b6dd99",
  text = "Scarlet Commander Mograine. Within the Scarlet Monastery, Scarlet Commander Mograine guards the Cathedral with the Scarlet Crusade. A zealous paladin, he faces efforts by the Horde and Alliance to disrupt the Crusade's threat to Tirisfal Glades and the lands around it. At the heart of the Scarlet Monastery, Scarlet Commander Mograine leads the Scarlet Crusade garrison in the Crusader's Chapel. A devoted paladin, he strikes with holy power and shields himself. Those the Crusade judges corrupted or unclean meet his stunning blows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_scarlet-commander-mograine-38ed8ae13ad935e569cb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.8693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:sneed"] = {
  hash = "c4901b",
  fullHash = "ff7fd7",
  text = "Sneed. Deep in the Deadmines, Sneed directs the Defias Brotherhood’s timber work. From the Mast Room, he pilots a heavy shredder of his own design, cutting lumber for Edwin VanCleef’s secret juggernaut. The Defias Brotherhood draws most of its members from human stonemasons and laborers. Yet Edwin VanCleef brought Sneed in for his engineering skill and ruthless efficiency. A warship needs skilled trades, and Sneed directs the timber work in the Mast Room beside the underground rail network. There, wood is felled, cut, and shaped for VanCleef’s colossal dreadnaught. Sneed cares about costs and quotas, whatever the consequences. From a custom shredder fitted with oversized buzz saws, he directs the work. Its blades turn raw trunks into shipbuilding timber in seconds. When intruders corner him, Sneed uses the machine’s armor to grind them down. If the shredder fails, he scrambles from the wreckage and fights barehanded.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_sneed-03187303f843b8477323/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.5518367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:sneed-s-shredder"] = {
  hash = "a850c3",
  fullHash = "c9d866",
  text = "Sneed's Shredder. Deep in the Deadmines, Sneed pilots a lumber machine for Edwin VanCleef's shipwrights. Its blades cut timber and meet intruders with armored force. In the Deadmines foundry, goblin shredders cut timber for Edwin VanCleef's hidden juggernaut. They also turn their armored force against intruders. Sneed oversees the lumber works and pilots this modified machine himself. Its oversized buzzsaw is a weapon of defense. Sneed rides above the spinning blades in an open cockpit, behind heavy iron plating. The shredder's clatter can scatter his foes. When the machine breaks down in a burst of sparks, Sneed leaps from the wreckage and fights on.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_sneed-s-shredder-7280a153658bdea831d5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.762449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:taragaman-the-hungerer"] = {
  hash = "36ed05",
  fullHash = "36ed05",
  text = "Taragaman the Hungerer. Beneath Orgrimmar, Taragaman the Hungerer prowls Ragefire Chasm and threatens the city above. The Horde seeks his death to end the demonic taint below its capital. Taragaman the Hungerer prowls among Ragefire Chasm's magma pools, threatening Orgrimmar. Scouts and young warriors sent into the dark caverns see the demonic felguard as the Searing Blade cult's leader. With infernal strength, he bends the weak-willed and corrupts the passages beneath Thrall's fortress.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_taragaman-the-hungerer-37dcea7777d614985d42/1-0000.mp3", duration = 30.3020408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:targorr-the-dread"] = {
  hash = "042214",
  fullHash = "e48dd3",
  text = "Targorr the Dread. In the Stormwind Stockade, Targorr the Dread waits among rioting convicts. Corruption and neglect delayed his execution, and the Defias uprising opened a way out. Before his capture, Targorr the Dread served Warlord Gath'Ilzogg as the Blackrock clan's supreme executioner. From the occupied ruins in Redridge, he tormented Stormwind guards and militiamen defending the borderlands. His cruelty surpassed even the Dark Horde's violence, and few names were more reviled across the kingdom. Alliance forces captured Targorr alive and brought him in chains to the capital. They locked him in the high-security Stockade, but Stormwind's courts stalled over his execution. Graft, endless paperwork, and neglect kept the courts from delivering the death sentence he had earned. So a bloodthirsty war criminal remained in a cell beneath the heart of the city. When the Defias Brotherhood broke prisoners out by force, Targorr seized his chance. He escaped his cell and joined the Defias rioters, turning his cellblock into a slaughterhouse. Warden Thelwater has lost control of the prison. Lakeshire still bears the wounds Targorr dealt its defenders, and seeks adventurers willing to enter the riot and bring back the butcher's head.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_targorr-the-dread-7d05a5f318f800dccaa0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.0873469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:twilight-lord-kelris"] = {
  hash = "5235c2",
  fullHash = "d3a33c",
  text = "Twilight Lord Kelris. At Blackfathom Deeps, Twilight Lord Kelris leads the Twilight's Hammer from the sunken ruins. The orc warlock holds high rank in the cult and directs its rites in the Moonshrine Sanctum. There, his followers worship Aku'mai and seek a reign of cosmic chaos. Twilight Lord Kelris directs the cultists who seized the sunken ruins of Blackfathom Deeps. For years, he slipped past watchful eyes in Darnassus and evaded the Argent Dawn. Whenever ancient malice stirred across Kalimdor, Kelris was suspected of being behind it. He pledged himself to the Old Gods, not to warlords or clans. He seeks to unmake mortal civilization and return Azeroth to primordial chaos. In the Moonshrine Sanctum, Kelris oversees the cult's worship of Aku'mai, a vicious three-headed hydra who serves an Old God. Allied with the naga, the Twilight's Hammer occupies the drowned temple. Kelris leads the cult's rites and sacrifices innocents to Aku'mai in exchange for power.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_twilight-lord-kelris-a845b47870061413696f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.0334694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:verdan-the-everliving"] = {
  hash = "678b3c",
  fullHash = "678b3c",
  text = "Verdan the Everliving. In the Wailing Caverns, Verdan the Everliving lurks in deep tunnels and attacks those who enter. He dwells near the sleeping druid Naralex and the Druids of the Fang. Verdan is the largest bog beast in the overgrown depths of the Wailing Caverns. He is an elemental of foliage, silt, and mire, haunting its flooded hollows. Nothing is known of him from before corruption reached the caverns.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_verdan-the-everliving-3c438479867d182dbf9e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 24.8946939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:viscous-fallout"] = {
  hash = "8e4d32",
  fullHash = "b9c35e",
  text = "Viscous Fallout. In Gnomeregan's lower passages, Viscous Fallout drifts through the toxic runoff. Radiation flooded Gnomeregan after Mekgineer Thermaplugg advised a treacherous plan to wipe out the invading troggs. The fallout filled the city's lower levels with toxic slime and contamination. Viscous Fallout is a water elemental, its substance thick with the city's toxic waste. Its origins before the fall are unknown. Viscous Fallout drifts through Gnomeregan's irradiated lower passages as a living pool of toxic sludge. It spews volleys of toxin, then lashes out at anything in its path. Those who enter the city to cleanse its tainted depths must destroy the creature.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_viscous-fallout-bb5da815afe223ddfda1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9044898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-narache"] = {
  hash = "24823f",
  fullHash = "9dd626",
  text = "Camp Narache. Camp Narache is where young tauren begin their training, high on Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. It is the ancestral home of the Hawkwind tribe. Up here, above the plains, the old traditions are kept. But the camp must always stand guard, against the quilboar and the centaur. The camp stands at the northern edge of the mesa, a village of great hide lodges and pavilions. There is a tent for wounded braves, and a lodge for speaking with the ancestors. At its center, the tribal elders and trainers stand in a circle. Among them is the venerable Greatmother Hawkwind. She still guides the young, as they take up their place as defenders of the Earth Mother. The sheer cliffs of the mesa make a natural fortress, yet the camp knows little peace. Bristleback quilboar press in from Brambleblade Ravine, and centaur scouts linger in the distance. Here young hunters, shaman, druids and warriors prove their mettle. They cull the angry wildlife, guard the village well and turn back the quilboar raiders. Then they walk the great path down, into the wider valleys of Mulgore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-narache-ae2a0812a79c6193bf61/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.5191837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:coldridge-valley"] = {
  hash = "30046b",
  fullHash = "c7b8a0",
  text = "Coldridge Valley. Coldridge Valley is where young dwarves and gnome refugees train to serve Ironforge. Snow lies deep over this sheltered vale, high in the southwest of Dun Morogh. Sheer cliffs close it in. Only one road leads out to the wider mountains, and that is Coldridge Pass. The recruits gather at Anvilmar, the valley's training hall. There they learn to fight, to move unseen and to wield sorcery. Veteran dwarves teach them, side by side with gnome scholars who have lost their home. Out on the snowy valley floor roam boars and hungry winter wolves. They test whether a novice can survive the frontier. But this valley is not at peace. Frostmane trolls camp here, at Frostmane Hovel and beyond. They say the land was theirs by ancestral right, and they skirmish with the mountaineers over it. And Rockjaw troggs have overrun Coldridge Pass. It is the tunnel to Kharanos, and while it is blocked, nothing moves freely to Ironforge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_coldridge-valley-a1eff74262ac9a4c28b8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.8130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:deathknell"] = {
  hash = "c4c1f2",
  fullHash = "ad41f1",
  text = "Deathknell. Deathknell is where the newly freed dead first wake, in a hidden valley of southwestern Tirisfal. They have broken from the Lich King's grasp, and here they join the Forsaken. But mindless Scourge, zealots and wild beasts beset the village. For anyone who would claim a place in Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom, it is a harsh first trial. The valley lies behind jagged hills, just north of the Silverpine border. Elite Deathguards keep the narrow gorge out of the valley sealed. Most towns in Tirisfal kept their human names from fallen Lordaeron. This one did not. The undead who claimed it named it Deathknell.\n\nThe town itself is split in two. Its northern half is still overrun by mindless zombies and feral ghouls, the village forge with it. The Forsaken cling to the southern grounds, around the old church. There the caretakers take in each new arrival, and bring them into the Banshee Queen's service. Survival here is never certain. To the northwest, monstrous spiders have taken Night Web's Hollow, a gold mine the young Forsaken nation needs. To the southeast, the Scarlet Crusade keeps a camp, and watches the valley without rest. Its couriers run to the Crusade's strongholds in the east, and its scouts probe Deathknell's defenses. And in the northern ruins, the caretakers must still fight off the mindless dead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_deathknell-bfb4f35b5079551e45f7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.0734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northshire-abbey"] = {
  hash = "8ed36b",
  fullHash = "94095a",
  text = "Northshire Abbey. Northshire Abbey trains the kingdom's newest defenders, in the foothills of Northshire Valley. It is the spiritual heart of the Brotherhood of Northshire. And for those who answer Stormwind's call, few schools in the land are finer. It has been built twice. Centuries ago, clerics came south from Lordaeron to spread the Holy Light. They founded the Holy Order of Northshire Clerics, and they built its abbey. It soon became part of the life of the kingdom, caring for its sick. For years it tended the young Guardian Medivh, while he lay in his long coma.\n\nThen the First War came. Brigands and the Horde struck at the abbey again and again, until it lay in ruins. In the Second War, the Alliance of Lordaeron freed the region. Afterward, Archbishop Alonsus Faol helped to raise the abbey anew. And he refounded its protectors as the Brotherhood of Northshire. Today the abbey takes in volunteers, militia recruits and novices, all of them hoping to defend Stormwind. In its halls, instructors teach the martial, arcane and holy arts. Scouts and rogues train out in the yards. And the Knights of the Silver Hand keep watch over it all.\n\nBut the peace here is fragile. Kobolds have overrun the nearby mines, and wolves prowl the orchards. Agents of the Defias Brotherhood stalk the valley roads. Not even the abbey's archives are safe from them. They steal its ancient scrolls and sacred librams.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northshire-abbey-1b059f13426047b0bcb7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.8538776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sen-jin-village"] = {
  hash = "940fe4",
  fullHash = "f2cc0f",
  text = "Sen'jin Village. Sen'jin Village shelters the Darkspear on Durotar's southern shore. Vol'jin led them there after Zalazane seized the Echo Isles, and gave the refuge his fallen father's name. Across the shallows, their lost home waits. The Darkspear survivors have made their refuge on Durotar's shore. After Zalazane betrayed the tribe and seized the Echo Isles, Chieftain Vol'jin led them across the strait to the mainland. They raised crude huts beneath coastal cliffs, with the water and their lost home in view.\n\nThe village bears the name of Sen'jin, Vol'jin's father. Murlocs captured and sacrificed him during the Third War. Though the Darkspear are closely allied with Thrall's Horde, exile remains their burden. The lost isles lie just across the shallows. Preparation and defense shape life in Sen'jin Village. Quilboar raids, aggressive sea beasts, and Zalazane's hexes from across the sea threaten the villagers. Herbal remedies and potent alchemical spirits help sustain them.\n\nVol'jin coordinates the tribe's wider defense from the Horde capital. Meanwhile, local shamans, hunters, and scouts gather recruits arriving from the Valley of Trials. The Darkspear seek resources, guard Durotar's southern flank, and plan Zalazane's downfall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sen-jin-village-8ede9daba2ea235de100/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.6995918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowglen"] = {
  hash = "3ce49c",
  fullHash = "17db64",
  text = "Shadowglen. Shadowglen is where young night elves begin their training, beneath the boughs of Teldrassil. The whole vale gathers around Aldrassil, a great hollow tree. Inside it, elders, priestesses and mentors guide the young. Only then do they travel farther into the world. Aldrassil is a vast barrow-tree, and it rises in a vale of eternal dusk. The glen lies under the northeastern boughs of the World Tree, and high ridges ring it round. A single narrow pass leads south, toward Starbreeze Village and Dolanaar. With only one way out, the glen stays sheltered and at peace. Yet danger reaches even here. In the far northwest lies Shadowthread Cave, and its venomous spiders threaten the glade. So the young defenders drive them back, along with the vermin and the wild beasts. Then they venture on, deeper among Teldrassil's boughs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowglen-0519953862d962970401/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.0661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-trials"] = {
  hash = "c2cc94",
  fullHash = "c2cc94",
  text = "Valley of Trials. The Valley of Trials, in the south of Durotar, is where young orcs and Darkspear trolls are put to the test. Chieftains and trainers watch over them from The Den, a shelter cut into the cliffs. Out in the sunlit gulch, the recruits hunt scorpids and cull the angry boars. They keep the lazy peons in line as well, while they gather the valley's scarce timber. The trial is hard. Only those who prove their mettle earn the right to leave as soldiers of the Horde, ready to defend their people. Those who fail are left beneath the desert sun.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-trials-8f4f690f41ddb142ec14/1-0000.mp3", duration = 34.3771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:alliance-of-lordaeron"] = {
  hash = "536daa",
  fullHash = "536daa",
  text = "Alliance of Lordaeron. Across the Eastern Kingdoms, the Alliance of Lordaeron once held the Old Horde at bay. Seven human kingdoms joined with Khaz Modan dwarves, Wildhammer gryphon riders, gnomes, and high elves in the Second War, under Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and King Terenas Menethil II. Political fractures and the Scourge broke the coalition during the Third War, yet its legacy endures in today’s Alliance. After Stormwind fell in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar led its battered refugees across the Great Sea to Capital City. There he pleaded their cause before King Terenas Menethil II. The king called the human rulers to council, though old rivalries and border tensions divided them. The Horde’s threat outweighed those disputes. The seven human nations chose Lothar as Supreme Commander and bound themselves in a desperate pact. The Alliance faced brutal orcish warriors, dark warlocks, and monstrous red dragons. Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Lothar formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, raising holy paladins such as Uther the Lightbringer to counter the Horde’s dark magic. Fighting spread across the Hinterlands, and the siege of Aerie Peak was broken. Then Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his gryphon riders to the skies.\n\nSoon after, the Bronzebeard dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes were freed. They joined the high elven rangers under Alleria Windrunner. King Perenolde of Alterac betrayed the coalition, but the Alliance drove the Horde back to Blackrock Spire and broke its dominion. Victory left bitterness in place of lasting peace. Disputes flared over the taxes needed to keep the orc internment camps and rebuild Stormwind. Gilneas saw little gain in remaining, built the Greymane Wall, and cut ties. Stromgarde departed over disputes about territory and prisoners, while Quel'Thalas withdrew behind its enchanted runestones. King Terenas tried to hold together the coalition that remained.\n\nDuring the Third War, the Cult of the Damned and the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron and dismantled it. Arthas Menethil’s betrayal tore out the kingdom’s heart and ended the golden age of the northern realm.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_alliance-of-lordaeron-a5d797cb6cd2913c31a4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 138.083265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:alterac-kingdom"] = {
  hash = "59bc44",
  fullHash = "59bc44",
  text = "Alterac (kingdom). In the years after the Second War, Alterac lies broken and its capital stands abandoned in the snow. Once one of seven human kingdoms in the Alterac Mountains, it fell when King Aiden Perenolde secretly aided the Old Horde. Now dispossessed nobles and outlaws fight beneath the Syndicate's banner. Alterac began as an Arathorian city-state after the Troll Wars, thousands of years ago. Its settlers held mountain passes and trading outposts. When the empire fractured, they formed an independent realm. Rugged Alterac bordered the prosperous lands of Lordaeron and Stromgarde. Its mountain fighters were tough, yet friction along the border with Thoras Trollbane's Stromgarde left the kingdom isolated long before the Dark Portal opened. Sir Anduin Lothar forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, and King Aiden Perenolde joined with deep reluctance. As Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde swept north, Perenolde believed humanity was doomed. So he secretly made a pact with the Warchief.\n\nIn return for Alterac's safety, Perenolde opened fortified mountain passes, plotted against Alliance forces, and sent soldiers and sailors to help the Horde besiege Lordaeron. The betrayal let the orcs reach Capital City far ahead of schedule. When the truth came to light, it shook the Alliance to its core. Stromgarde's forces marched into the mountains, exposed Perenolde's treason, and broke Alterac's military. King Terenas Menethil II placed the fallen nation under martial law. For years, Alliance diplomats argued over who should inherit the throne. After the kingdom collapsed, King Perenolde died in disgrace.\n\nHis son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, gathered the bitter, displaced nobles and formed the Syndicate. In orange cloth from their fallen homeland, its bandits and mercenaries now fight ruthlessly to reclaim their ancestral holdings across Hillsbrad and Alterac.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_alterac-kingdom-b98053fd07984e260cb7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 122.122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:anduin-lothar"] = {
  hash = "a0d9bb",
  fullHash = "a0d9bb",
  text = "Anduin Lothar. In Stormwind's royal court, Anduin Lothar grew up beside Prince Llane Wrynn and Medivh. Last of the ancient Arathi bloodline, he led humanity through the First War and united the northern nations in the Second War as Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron. His campaign ended at Blackrock Mountain. Anduin Lothar grew up in Stormwind's royal court, heir to the bloodline of the Arathi kings. He shared his youth with Prince Llane Wrynn and Medivh. Together, the three ventured recklessly into the wilderness and skirmished with Gurubashi trolls in Stranglethorn Vale. Lothar rose through Stormwind's martial ranks and joined the King's Honor Guard. He also earned renown as an Armsman of the Brotherhood of the Horse. On Lordaeron's shores, Lothar brought King Terenas Menethil II grim news of the Horde. His Arathi lineage and standing as a diplomat helped unite the human realms, Ironforge dwarves, and high elves as the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Named Supreme Commander, he led the campaign that drove the Horde south to the foot of Blackrock Mountain.\n\nNear the end of the Second War, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer slew Lothar in a clash there. His death did not break the Alliance. His lieutenant Turalyon took command and led the charge that broke the Horde forever.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_anduin-lothar-3ef794409c7f5bc388f8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.397551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:argent-dawn"] = {
  hash = "5853e4",
  fullHash = "5853e4",
  text = "Argent Dawn. Across Azeroth, the Argent Dawn holds its ground against the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Survivors of the Silver Hand formed the order after rejecting the Scarlet Crusade's zealotry. They welcome champions of any race or faction who honor the Light, from Light's Hope Chapel to outposts across Kalimdor. After Highlord Alexandros Mograine fell and Lordaeron collapsed, the Knights of the Silver Hand split over what to do. Grief and paranoia drove many survivors into the xenophobic Scarlet Crusade.\n\nLord Maxwell Tyrosus and other veterans saw that their former comrades had forsaken the true tenets of the Holy Light. They would neither yield to madness nor abandon the dead kingdom. So they formed the Argent Dawn, ready to defend Azeroth beside any fighter from the Alliance or the Horde. Light's Hope Chapel, in the blighted Eastern Plaguelands, is the Argent Dawn's chief stronghold. From there, the order directs its war against Kel'Thuzad's necromancers and skeletal legions. Its forward camps include Chillwind Camp and a camp outside the Bulwark in Tirisfal Glades.\n\nArgent officers give adventurers enchanted commissions and pay bounties for Scourgestones taken from fallen undead. The Dawn has fewer fighters than the Scarlet Crusade, yet perseverance, unity, and discipline keep it holding the line. Lordaeron remains the Argent Dawn's main battleground, but its watch reaches across Kalimdor and the world's capitals. The order keeps diplomatic presences in Stormwind City, Darnassus, and the goblin trade town of Everlook. In Darkshore and Ashenvale, operatives such as Delgren the Purifier contest demonic cults. Scouts in Desolace and the flooded ruins of Blackfathom Deeps investigate threats involving the Shadow Council and the Twilight's Hammer cult.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_argent-dawn-aed4651737438b302fab/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.74449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:arthas-menethil"] = {
  hash = "bfc34c",
  fullHash = "bfc34c",
  text = "Arthas Menethil. As plague spreads through Lordaeron, Arthas Menethil leaves his place as its golden prince to hunt those behind it. A paladin of the Silver Hand, he claims Frostmourne in the frozen north, loses his soul, kills King Terenas, and turns against his homeland as the Scourge's champion. Arthas grew up heir to King Terenas Menethil II, expected to uphold the ideals of the Alliance of Lordaeron. Muradin Bronzebeard trained him in combat, while Uther the Lightbringer guided him into the Order of the Silver Hand. High and low alike looked to him as humanity's future. Bold, skilled, and fiercely protective of his people, he fought Blackrock clan orc incursions and forest troll warbands. Beneath that bright name lived pride and a deep fear of failure.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_arthas-menethil-12ef8c04aec015d12816/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.5428571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:black-dragonflight"] = {
  hash = "208d08",
  fullHash = "208d08",
  text = "Black dragonflight. From the shadows, the Black dragonflight wages war. The titans charged its dragons to guard Azeroth's deep places, but madness and treachery turned them from that duty. Neltharion became Deathwing, and his surviving brood plots against mortal kingdoms. The titans charged Neltharion and the Black dragonflight with guarding Azeroth's deep caverns and raising its mountains. For ages, they guarded the earth beside the other flights. Their duty brought them near the Old Gods' sunken prisons. Over centuries, dark whispers from those prisons broke Neltharion's mind and turned his brood toward ruin. In the ancient War of the Ancients, Neltharion betrayed the other dragons and became Deathwing the Destroyer. Since then, the Black flight has warred against the red, blue, green, and bronze flights. The other dragons see its cruelty as a profanation of life. Now Black dragons wield magma, black magic, and brimstone to scorch the lands they once tended. Misery and strife spread across mortal realms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_black-dragonflight-27d69a6022c913175c03/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.2089796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:blackrock-clan"] = {
  hash = "55fabc",
  fullHash = "55fabc",
  text = "Blackrock clan. In Blackrock Mountain, veterans of the Blackrock clan now stand divided between Thrall's New Horde and the Dark Horde. Strict ranks, heavy armor, and skill at the forge once made the clan a force in the First and Second Wars. From Draenor came the Blackrock orcs, whose strength lay in strict ranks, heavy armor, and skill at the forge. Under Chieftain Blackhand, they formed the brutal core of the Old Horde's invasion of Azeroth and laid siege to Stormwind.\n\nOrgrim Doomhammer killed Blackhand to free the Horde from Gul'dan's direct control. Blackrock leaders then commanded both the clan and the Horde through the hard campaigns of the Second War. After the Horde's defeat at Blackrock Mountain, the clan broke apart. Many warriors went to Alliance internment camps, then Thrall freed them and welcomed them into his reformed New Horde.\n\nOthers would not yield. They gathered behind Blackhand's sons, Rend and Maim, and withdrew to the heights of Blackrock Spire. There they formed the hostile Dark Horde with remnants of the Dragonmaw and other rogue clans. From his throne in Blackrock Spire, Rend Blackhand claims the title of true Warchief. The Dark Horde keeps a tense truce with the Dark Iron dwarves in the molten depths below, even as it fights both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde.\n\nClan remnants tainted by demons have also been seen in Lordaeron. There they seek to revive dark sacrificial rites.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_blackrock-clan-75ed58e5d9151847c056/1-0000.mp3", duration = 92.9436735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:bronzebeard-clan"] = {
  hash = "7e9dea",
  fullHash = "7e9dea",
  text = "Bronzebeard clan. In Ironforge, King Magni Bronzebeard leads his clan and its kingdom. The Bronzebeards won the War of the Three Hammers, then forged close ties with humanity and the gnomes. Now they anchor the Alliance. High King Modimus Anvilmar’s death opened old rivalries within Ironforge. For years, dwarven society had flourished under his rule, but when he died of old age about two hundred and thirty years ago, those rivalries became civil war. Thane Madoran Bronzebeard commanded Ironforge’s traditional garrison and largest standing army. He drove the Wildhammer and Dark Iron clans from beneath the mountain.\n\nSorcerer-Thane Thaurissan attacked to reclaim Khaz Modan, and Madoran led his clan and army in a decisive campaign. The Bronzebeards shattered the Dark Iron host and secured their supremacy over Ironforge. The Bronzebeards and human kingdoms first met nearly two thousand years ago. Their shared skill in metalcraft, trade, and battle built mutual respect. Then the Orcish Horde swept across the continent in the Second War, and King Magni Bronzebeard brought his realm into the Alliance of Lordaeron.\n\nThe Bronzebeards withstood brutal sieges inside their subterranean fortress. Their engineers, gunners, and demolitionists supplied vital firepower and arms, helping turn the tide of war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_bronzebeard-clan-e3ec14b2849acf245ec9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.6669388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:burning-blade-clan"] = {
  hash = "6dcf2f",
  fullHash = "6dcf2f",
  text = "Burning Blade clan. Across Durotar and the Barrens, the Burning Blade threatens the young Horde from within. Once renowned for its blademasters, the clan broke under demonic corruption. Now most of its scattered members serve the Shadow Council, while a few honorable blademasters stand with Warchief Thrall. The Burning Blade once fought beside clans such as the Warsong on Draenor. Its blademasters were famed for deadly skill. After drinking Mannoroth’s blood and crossing the Dark Portal into Azeroth during the First War, the clan’s minds broke into chaos.\n\nBy the Second War, it had lost all order and raged like a force of nature. These nomadic fighters charged without regard for survival, so ogres held them back until battle called. Near the Dark Portal, the Alliance battered and routed them. Across Kalimdor, Burning Blade covens trouble newly arrived orcs and Darkspear trolls from their first days in the Valley of Trials. Warlocks dwell in Thunder Ridge and keep a major hideout in Skull Rock, near Orgrimmar’s eastern gates. The cultists gather around dark relics such as the Demon Seed and harvest souls for demonic rites. In Durotar and the Barrens, they threaten the young Horde from within.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_burning-blade-clan-5958ed94ec4eb1c08cf6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.1991837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:burning-legion"] = {
  hash = "77efc9",
  fullHash = "77efc9",
  text = "Burning Legion. Across creation, the Burning Legion remains an infinite host bent on scouring all life. Led by the fallen titan Sargeras, its demons shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, corrupted the orcs, and brought the horrors of the Third War to Azeroth. Sargeras once served the titans, hunting demons across the cosmos and locking them away. Ages of battle convinced him that creation was flawed beyond repair and must burn. He freed the demons he had imprisoned and bent them to his will with fel magic. The corrupted eredar brought intellect to their chaos, and Archimonde and Kil'jaeden became his chief lieutenants. Together they formed an unstoppable engine of cosmic annihilation. Ten thousand years ago, the Highborne's reckless sorceries drew the Burning Legion's gaze to Azeroth. Through the Well of Eternity, Sargeras whispered to Queen Azshara and her councilor Xavius, promising them a chance to reshape the world as a paradise.\n\nThe portal unleashed Mannoroth and Archimonde, with swarms of doomguard, felhounds, and flaming infernals. Their war ruined Kalimdor's civilization before night elves, dragonflights, and demigods brought down the gateway. Its catastrophic implosion tore the supercontinent apart in the Great Sundering, scattering ruined lands across vast new seas. The Legion was cast back into the Twisting Nether, yet Azeroth remained in its sights. Kil'jaeden arranged proxy cataclysms from afar. The deceiver bound the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood pacts, and the Horde charged through the Dark Portal to weaken mortal defenses.\n\nWhen that failed, the Legion forged the Lich King and unleashed the undead Scourge across Lordaeron, paving the way for Archimonde's return in the Third War. Archimonde fell at Mount Hyjal, but Legion remnants still linger across Felwood, the Blasted Lands, and hidden cults throughout the world.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_burning-legion-50a56f018ecd5d73f918/1-0000.mp3", duration = 122.592653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cenarion-circle"] = {
  hash = "91aaaf",
  fullHash = "91aaaf",
  text = "Cenarion Circle. Across Azeroth, the Cenarion Circle guards nature’s balance. Founded by Malfurion Stormrage under Cenarius, it keeps its sacred haven in Moonglade and tends the Emerald Dream and blighted lands. Once made up of night elves, it now welcomes tauren druids to defend the living world. Thousands of years ago, Cenarius taught Malfurion Stormrage, the first mortal druid. After the Great Sundering, Malfurion gathered those who wished to learn nature’s ways. The order made its home in Moonglade’s sacred vale. Ever since, the Circle has watched over Azeroth’s wild places. The Cenarion Circle is rooted in night elf history, yet answers neither to Darnassus nor to the Sisterhood of Elune. Its heart is Moonglade, where the peaceful town of Nighthaven keeps an ancient truce. Weapons stay sheathed there, beyond the reach of mortal wars.\n\nAfter the Third War, Malfurion and Hamuul Runetotem, a tauren elder, became friends and came to respect one another. The Circle honored the tauren’s reverence for the Earth Mother and welcomed Hamuul into its ranks. Through him, druidism returned to his people after thousands of years.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cenarion-circle-3ddb5470a10b36bfe833/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.2138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cenarius"] = {
  hash = "a105df",
  fullHash = "a105df",
  text = "Cenarius. Across Kalimdor, Cenarius guards the wild places and teaches the night elves druidism. The ancient demigod, son of Elune and Malorne, guided mortal druids for thousands of years before falling in battle during the Third War. Born to Elune, goddess of the moon, and Malorne, the great white stag demigod, Cenarius was bound to the mortal world and placed in Malorne's care. Ysera the Dreamer took him as a foster son and guided him along the infinite paths of the Emerald Dream. Cenarius held power over living wood and earth. He fathered the Keepers of the Grove and the dryads, binding his bloodline to Kalimdor's wild places. Millennia ago, Malfurion Stormrage, his twin Illidan, and Tyrande Whisperwind sought the legendary demigod. Cenarius saw Malfurion's exceptional heart and trained him as the first mortal druid. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne unleashed the Burning Legion in the War of the Ancients, Cenarius gathered Kalimdor's wild demigods to fight beside the mortal defenders. For ten thousand years after the Great Sundering, Cenarius watched over Kalimdor and nurtured the Cenarion Circle with Malfurion. During the Third War, the Warsong clan began cutting timber in Ashenvale. Angered by the ruin of sacred woodlands, Cenarius drove the invaders back.\n\nThen Grom Hellscream and his warriors took Mannoroth's demonic blood. Filled with demonic fury, the corrupted orcs overwhelmed Cenarius and struck him down. Nature's defenders were left in deep sorrow.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cenarius-f1968023aec4a295a02b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.8661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:church-of-the-holy-light"] = {
  hash = "00c69e",
  fullHash = "00c69e",
  text = "Church of the Holy Light. After Lordaeron's fall, the Church of the Holy Light gathers in Stormwind City's Cathedral of Light. Its clergy guide human souls through compassion, justice, and devotion. Among the humans of Arathor, faith in the Holy Light took root more than a thousand years ago. The faithful learned to channel it to heal injuries and purge affliction. They were taught to give charity and seek righteousness, believing the Light dwells within all thinking beings. In time, these beliefs became an organized faith. Lordaeron became its heartland, with revered sanctuaries in Stratholme, Andorhal, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel. In the First War, the Brotherhood of Northshire went to battle, healing defenders who fell against the invading Orcish Horde. The clerics had no weapons or heavy armor, and suffered terrible losses to the orcs. During the Second War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol met with Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar.\n\nTogether they formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, holy warriors who could wield holy wrath and endure physical combat. Paladins and priests became the Alliance of Lordaeron's moral and martial bulwark, preserving humanity from annihilation. The Third War brought sorrow to the faithful. As the Plague of Undeath spread across Lordaeron, the Church aided the sick and tried to contain the contamination. But the kingdom collapsed from within, and the clergy could not stem the disaster.\n\nLordaeron is now a wilderness haunted by the Scourge, so the Church moved its seat south. Under Archbishop Benedictus, its clergy rebuilt their hierarchy in Stormwind's newly constructed Cathedral of Light. There they offer solace, spiritual guidance, and military purpose to the battered survivors of the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_church-of-the-holy-light-7031322727ba130ef0ea/1-0000.mp3", duration = 110.733061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:cult-of-the-damned"] = {
  hash = "336e74",
  fullHash = "336e74",
  text = "Cult of the Damned. Across Lordaeron, the Cult of the Damned still serves the Lich King. Before the Third War, its poisoned grain spread the Scourge and helped bring down humanity's greatest kingdom. Now cultists tend plague cauldrons and necromantic academies in the Plaguelands, while living agents infiltrate the cities of the living. Kel'Thuzad founded the cult after hearing the Lich King's whispers from the Frozen Throne. Exiled from the Kirin Tor, the archmage returned to Lordaeron with great wealth and forbidden arts. To the desperate poor, he promised equality and freedom from misery. To wealthy landowners such as House of Barov, he promised eternal life and lasting power over their domains. Both were drawn into a death cult meant to betray their homeland from within. Beneath Caer Darrow, cultists practiced necromancy in secret. They built abominations and perfected the Plague of Undeath brought from Northrend. Then they contaminated grain stores in Andorhal, Lordaeron's breadbasket.\n\nFlour traveled along merchant routes to homes across the Eastweald. Within days, commoners sickened and died, then rose as thralls under the Lich King's command. When Prince Arthas killed Kel'Thuzad in Andorhal, the cult had already spread too far to stop the kingdom's fall. The Cult of the Damned still threatens northern Lordaeron under the resurrected archlich Kel'Thuzad. Living cultists operate from the ruins of Stratholme and Scholomance. They perform dark rites, tend plague cauldrons among ruined farms, and try to kill Scarlet Crusaders and Forsaken. Their agents pass unnoticed through settlements, poison wells, disrupt supplies, and recruit others for the Scourge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_cult-of-the-damned-9b9580c0a7ff6172dcc6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 105.743673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-iron-clan"] = {
  hash = "eaad33",
  fullHash = "eaad33",
  text = "Dark Iron clan. In Blackrock Mountain, the Dark Iron clan serves Ragnaros under Emperor Dagran Thaurissan. Driven from Ironforge in the War of the Three Hammers, they called the Firelord from beneath the earth and fell beneath his rule. The War of the Three Hammers began when High King Modimus Anvilmar died. Three dwarven factions had shared Ironforge, but the Bronzebeards defeated both rivals in the civil war. They drove out the Wildhammers and Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan's Dark Irons.\n\nThaurissan swore vengeance and founded a settlement in the Redridge Mountains. His sorceress wife, Modgud, led an army against Grim Batol. Both assaults failed, and the united Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies marched on Thaurissan's stronghold. Desperate, he tried to free an apocalyptic being beneath the earth. His ritual broke through the world's core and released Ragnaros the Firelord. Ragnaros's return shattered the peaks around him and formed Blackrock Mountain's blazing caldera. The Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes lie beside it. The cataclysm killed Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan, and the surviving Dark Irons became the Firelord's servants.\n\nOver the centuries, they built Shadowforge City in the mountain's lower chambers. Their artisans and sorcerers mastered stone golems, dark sorceries, and steam-driven siege engines. Under the watch of flamewakers and elemental overlords, they made weapons from the molten elements around them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-iron-clan-20dedc0e0265796bd3cc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.7681633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-iron-dwarf"] = {
  hash = "b158a4",
  fullHash = "b158a4",
  text = "Dark Iron dwarf. Beneath Blackrock Mountain, Dark Iron dwarves live under Ragnaros's rule. Banished from Ironforge after the War of the Three Hammers, they have built a society shaped by fire and tyranny. Dark Iron dwarves have dark gray or soot-black skin and eyes like glowing embers. Their Bronzebeard and Wildhammer cousins look different. Their people split during the War of the Three Hammers, when Sorcerer-thane Thaurissan tried to conquer Ironforge. Defeated, he led them south into the Redridge Mountains.\n\nThere, he sought dark powers to avenge his losses and released Ragnaros the Firelord from the Elemental Plane. The summoning shattered the surrounding peaks into the volcanic waste of Blackrock Mountain. It bound the Dark Iron to fiery servitude. Life in Shadowforge, deep beneath the mountain, is brutal. The Dark Irons were once ambitious and cunning, but generations beneath Ragnaros's oppressive aura have warped much of their culture. Cruelty, paranoia, and tyranny now shape it.\n\nDark Iron law gives one sentence for infractions: trial by combat in the Ring of the Law. Poisoning and political intrigue run so deep that Dark Iron travelers bring their own rations rather than trust food from strangers. Not all Dark Iron dwarves serve Emperor Dagran Thaurissan and the cult of Ragnaros. Dissident factions have broken away to follow their own ends. The best known is the Thorium Brotherhood, a guild of master smiths based at Thorium Point in Searing Gorge. They left the main clan and hire outsiders to fight their former masters.\n\nOther Dark Irons work as mercenaries, spies, or trade engineers alongside neutral groups such as the Venture Company.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-iron-dwarf-c66049475ff516714bc6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 109.139592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dark-riders"] = {
  hash = "e24d56",
  fullHash = "e24d56",
  text = "Dark Riders. In Duskwood, the Dark Riders hunt magical relics and terrorize the land. Hooded and merciless, they strike from hiding and leave slaughter behind. Little is known of the Dark Riders' beginnings. Witnesses speak of hooded horsemen with harsh, shrill voices. One was seen using a small gem to receive orders from a master it called only its “Lord.” Across the kingdom, they hunt objects of magical power. Anyone who stands between them and their prize is cut down.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dark-riders-281e73645daee108ede1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.1306122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:darkspear-tribe"] = {
  hash = "a7d75e",
  fullHash = "a7d75e",
  text = "Darkspear tribe. Across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, the Darkspear tribe follows Thrall’s Horde. Driven from Stranglethorn Vale and rescued from a sea witch in the Great Sea, the jungle trolls follow Shadow Hunter Vol’jin from Sen’jin Village. They now struggle to reclaim the Echo Isles from Zalazane. Once part of the broken Gurubashi Empire in Stranglethorn Vale, the Darkspear were smaller and less ruthless than their neighbors. For centuries, they endured famine and fought the brutal Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter tribes. Driven out to sea, they settled on remote islands, where murlocs, Kul Tiran sailors, and a naga sea witch threatened them.\n\nTheir elder chieftain, Sen’jin, was captured and slain in dark rites beneath the sea. Before he died, he foretold that the orc Warchief Thrall would lead the Darkspear to a new destiny. Thrall’s fleet rescued the survivors. They pledged loyalty to the new Horde and sailed west to Kalimdor. The Darkspear have adapted quickly to the Horde’s values of honor, unlike their cannibalistic jungle kin. They have enclaves in Malaka’jin in the Stonetalon Mountains, Shadowprey Village in Desolace, and Zoram’gar Outpost in Ashenvale. In Orgrimmar, they make their spiritual home in the Valley of Spirits, where shadow hunters and witch doctors commune with the loa.\n\nTheir scouts, bat riders, and powerful hexes are respected. Yet old traditions run deep, and some ancient rites their allies frown upon are still practiced quietly in the dark.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_darkspear-tribe-de737d6b7eb9ca711c8a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 94.72, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:dragonmaw-clan"] = {
  hash = "a6b977",
  fullHash = "a6b977",
  text = "Dragonmaw clan. Across Khaz Modan, Dragonmaw remnants still lurk in wetlands and mountains. During the Second War, they became infamous for subduing and riding red dragons. After Alexstrasza was freed, the scattered orcs remained hostile to both the Alliance and Thrall's New Horde. The Dragonmaw crossed the Dark Portal from Draenor with the Old Horde. On Azeroth, they turned their skill toward subduing its winged terrors. Black dragon Deathwing whispered to Zuluhed the Whacked, chieftain of the Dragonmaw, and led him to an ancient golden disk called the Dragon Soul. Zuluhed gave it to Nekros Skullcrusher, his greatest warlock. Nekros drew on its fel taint and named it the Demon Soul. With its agonizing power, he ensnared Alexstrasza the Life-Binder.\n\nThe Dragonmaw fortified Grim Batol, an abandoned dwarven bastion, and chained the Dragonqueen in darkness. During the Second War, they forced her red dragon brood to serve the Old Horde as aerial dreadnoughts. The Dragonmaw reign ended when the Demon Soul was destroyed, freeing Alexstrasza and her flight from psychic torment. Alexstrasza devoured Nekros Skullcrusher, and her vengeful flight laid waste to Grim Batol. The clan's strength broke.\n\nRouted and terrified, survivors scattered into the Wetlands' marshy bogs and the jagged peaks around Grim Batol. The Dragonmaw who remain on Azeroth are desperate, feral renegades. They fiercely guard their dwindling camps against dwarven patrols and vengeful dragons, and remain hostile to both the Alliance and Thrall's New Horde.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_dragonmaw-clan-a4a303a2ba7fc6487988/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.041633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:elune"] = {
  hash = "56c595",
  fullHash = "56c595",
  text = "Elune. Across Azeroth, Elune is chiefly worshipped by the night elves as their protector and mother of Cenarius. The larger moon, the White Lady, is hers, and her gifts reach from healing and peace to lunar fury. Tauren know her as Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother. Elune is one of the few true deities known across Azeroth, bound to the night sky and the larger moon, the White Lady. Though she has never appeared in bodily form, night elven artists portray her as a radiant kaldorei with alabaster skin, silver hair, and eyes of pure moonlight. Ancient kaldorei legends say she rested in the depths of the Well of Eternity by day, whispering wisdom to the first night elves. Night elven life, both sacred and civic, centers on devotion to Elune through the Sisterhood of Elune. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind leads this ancient order. The Sisters call on Elune to mend even lethal wounds, and turn her radiant wrath upon their foes with searing starlight.\n\nElune and the Great White Stag, Malorne, brought forth Cenarius. Through him, Elune's heavenly mysteries are bound to the druidic traditions that tend Kalimdor's wildlands. Elune is known for peace, harmony, and restoration, yet battle has its place in her worship. As the Night Warrior, she is a fierce patron of battle. She gathers the spirits of fallen heroes and sets them in the heavens, where they ride as stars.\n\nThe nomadic tauren call Elune Mu'sha, the radiant left eye of the Earth Mother. She lights the night and guides wandering hunters beside her fiery twin, An'she the sun.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_elune-e035c7c2ebdebdc85356/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.814694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:emerald-dream"] = {
  hash = "f060a0",
  fullHash = "f060a0",
  text = "Emerald Dream. Druids and green dragons keep watch over the Emerald Dream, where Azeroth’s wild nature runs free. Its lands shift with ancient rhythms, and druids enter to seek communion with the cycle of life. The Emerald Dream shows Azeroth as it might have been without intelligent beings clearing forests or raising cities. Though it lies beyond the physical world, it remains bound to Azeroth’s heartbeat. Shaped during the ancient ordering of the world, it holds forests, plains, and rivers in a wilderness that never rests in one form. Time and distance lose their usual meaning there. A day in the waking world can feel like decades, while landmarks shift before mortal eyes. After the ordering of the world, the titans entrusted the Emerald Dream to Ysera the Dreamer, great Aspect of the green dragonflight. She guides Azeroth’s natural rhythms and evolution, dwelling in an unending trance. Through enchanted dreamsight, she perceives both the waking world and its spirit reflection. Green dragons guard the rare natural portals between the physical world and the Dream, watching the boundary between sleep and waking. For mortal druids, the Emerald Dream is the wellspring of their craft. Through nature magic, they send their spirits into the Dream while their bodies rest in barrow dens, sometimes for years or centuries. There they commune with ancient nature spirits and the Wild Gods, seeking insight into the Great Cycle of life and death. Skilled dreamwalkers move beyond physical laws in their dreamforms. They can cross vast distances or pass through solid barriers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_emerald-dream-e856f4d733eac108c3ae/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.333061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:explorers-league"] = {
  hash = "6f5369",
  fullHash = "6f5369",
  text = "Explorers' League. Across Azeroth, the Explorers' League seeks the lost beginnings of the dwarves. King Magni Bronzebeard helped found it, and his brother Brann now leads its work from Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. The League searches far beyond dwarven lands, where its digs often stir conflict. Dwarves in Khaz Modan once devoted their strength to mining, stonework, and engineering. Ancient ruins then pointed to a forgotten bond between dwarves and the mythical titans who created them.\n\nKing Magni Bronzebeard wanted to learn his people's origins, so he turned much of dwarven industry toward archaeology. With his brother Brann, Magni founded the Explorers' Guild, now called the Explorers' League. Its members seek relics, lost cities, and signs of their ancient ancestry. The League directs its work from Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. High Explorer Muninn Magellas handles daily affairs while Brann Bronzebeard explores distant lands. Prospectors oversee broad digsites across Khaz Modan and beyond. At Ironband's Excavation in Loch Modan and Whelgar's Excavation in the Wetlands, they uncover vast fossil beds and ancient buildings. Displaced wildlife, troggs, and rival factions seeking buried relics often besiege the prospectors. Beyond dwarven lands, the League's expeditions have met fierce resistance. In Kalimdor, blasting at the Bael Modan dig in the southern Barrens drove out the native Stonespire tauren. The work stirred their anger and roused local earth spirits. In the Badlands, League prospectors openly clash with the Dark Iron clan over the vast titan vaults of Uldaman.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_explorers-league-6130ec140dc379e0a4a8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.672653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:first-war"] = {
  hash = "fa5e02",
  fullHash = "fa5e02",
  text = "First War. The First War drove Stormwind's survivors north after the kingdom fell. The five-year conflict began when the Orcish Horde crossed the Dark Portal and ended with King Llane Wrynn I dead and Stormwind in ruins. For years after the Gnoll War, the Knights of Stormwind and the Clerics of Northshire Abbey guarded a peaceful kingdom. Then Guardian Medivh, secretly possessed by the demon lord Sargeras, contacted the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Together, they forged the Dark Portal in the Black Morass.\n\nDriven by demon blood and the slow death of their homeworld, the Orcish Horde crossed into Azeroth in force. Some dismissed the first reports as vengeful spirits or strange offshoots of trolls. Sir Anduin Lothar's scouting patrols soon confirmed a disciplined, bloodthirsty army had arrived. Warchief Blackhand led the Horde openly, while Gul'dan's Shadow Council guided it from the shadows. The Horde overran the kingdom's southern reaches. An attempt to push into Stranglethorn Vale against the Gurubashi trolls failed, yet the campaign across human lands brought ruin.\n\nThe Horde conquered until only Elwynn Forest remained under human control. Blackhand then launched a direct assault on Stormwind City. Sir Anduin Lothar repelled it with a surprise flanking attack by sea, saving the capital from destruction for a time.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_first-war-cc05127f9466d2e36d6e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.4130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:forsaken"] = {
  hash = "20f86c",
  fullHash = "20f86c",
  text = "Forsaken. Across Azeroth, intelligent undead called the Forsaken live beneath Lordaeron's ruined capital in the Undercity. They broke from the Scourge after the Third War under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. Though mistrusted, they hold a wary alliance with the Horde. After the Third War, the Lich King's hold weakened. Thousands of undead in Lordaeron's blighted ruins regained their minds and memories, and recalled the slaughter they had been forced to commit. The living hunted them, though they had once been citizens of Lordaeron.\n\nLady Sylvanas Windrunner, Quel'Thalas's fallen ranger-general, had become an unholy banshee. She gathered the wandering undead and claimed the catacombs beneath Capital City. There she named their new nation the Forsaken. Enemies surrounded the Forsaken, above all the Scarlet Crusade and the Scourge. Lady Sylvanas sent emissaries in search of allies. Across the Great Sea, tauren elders urged compassion, believing the cursed people might find redemption or a cure through the earth.\n\nWarchief Thrall had misgivings, but the Horde accepted the Forsaken. In return, the Horde gained a vital foothold in the north of the Eastern Kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_forsaken-37fe8dc0f8135a9e384b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.24, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:frostmane-tribe"] = {
  hash = "b3a7e6",
  fullHash = "b3a7e6",
  text = "Frostmane tribe. Across Dun Morogh, the Frostmane tribe fights to reclaim the mountain home dwarves took from them. Driven into caves and remote ridges centuries ago, the ice trolls now wage war against the dwarves. Long before the Bronzebeard clan united under Ironforge, the Frostmane trolls ruled Dun Morogh’s snowy reaches. As the dwarven kingdom spread, it crushed their realm. The survivors fled into caves and across frozen ridges and crags.\n\nDefeat left the Frostmane with a hatred passed down through generations. They see every mountaineer, traveler, and homestead as an invader to drive out or slaughter. For decades, the Ironforge Senate treated the Frostmane as little more than a nuisance in the hills. Then the trogg menace emerged beneath Dun Morogh, and dwarven defenses fell into disarray. The mountaineers were overwhelmed while dwarven forces fought Alliance wars abroad.\n\nThe Frostmane seized their chance. From strongholds such as Frostmane Hold and Shimmer Ridge, they renewed their violent raids to reclaim their ancient lands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_frostmane-tribe-6ce24fd1ede84e34d7ff/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.2130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:gnoll"] = {
  hash = "20694a",
  fullHash = "20694a",
  text = "Gnoll. Across the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor, gnoll packs prey on settlements and travelers. Hyena-like scavengers, they are known for vicious infighting, brutal strength, and animal cunning. Their warlords lead raids, butcher wildlife, and threaten frontier hamlets and trade roads. Gnolls roam the wilds on two legs or all fours. Slightly shorter than an average human, these hyena-kin have thick pelts, spiky teeth, and a barking laugh. They lope swiftly through brush and track prey by scent.\n\nAmong gnolls, raw strength rules. Fractious packs follow the strongest and cruelest alpha. They yield only when a superior force batters them into submission. United gnolls once brought open war to the kingdom of Stormwind. Stormwind's armies defeated them, and the kingdom knew lasting peace until the orcs came through the Dark Portal.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_gnoll-20911fbafb637daced18/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.3273469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:gnome"] = {
  hash = "35cb93",
  fullHash = "35cb93",
  text = "Gnome. Gnomes in Ironforge fight to reclaim Gnomeregan, their poisoned home. Across Azeroth, they are renowned for engineering and arcane mastery, and remain staunch allies even in exile. Gnomes are small in stature and vast in intellect. Curiosity drives them to study machines and magic, and to invent what no one has built before. Clockwork devices, flying machines, and subterranean trains are among their works. Hardship has not dimmed their cheer; they still mean to rebuild and solve what seems impossible. The gnomes made their home in the frozen crags of Dun Morogh. There, they found kinship with the dwarves of Ironforge. Together, dwarven strength and gnomish ingenuity carved Gnomeregan, a remarkable city beneath western Dun Morogh. Visionary leaders called High Tinkers lead the gnomes. The surviving gnomes fled their poisoned home and found sanctuary with their dwarven cousins in Ironforge. High Tinker Mekkatorque and his people made their home in Ironforge's Tinker Town. There, they continue to support the Alliance and oversee the Deeprun Tram to Stormwind.\n\nFrom this adopted quarter, gnomish commandos and volunteers make repeated expeditions into Gnomeregan's irradiated depths. They seek to clear the troggs and mechanical abominations from their grand city and reclaim it someday.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_gnome-e0faba4b5dfd71d82b42/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.6032653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:goblin"] = {
  hash = "65c6f8",
  fullHash = "65c6f8",
  text = "Goblin. Across Azeroth, goblins bargain, build, and trade for profit. Their cartels began on the Isle of Kezan, and their craft and hunger for gain now reach far beyond it. Most keep neutral so they can trade with both Alliance and Horde, while the Venture Company and Steamwheedle Cartel leave their mark across the world. Goblins come from the Isle of Kezan in the South Seas. Long ago, rival economic cartels divided their society. Their leaders sought profit through hard bargaining, volatile engineering, and industry. They built neither kingdoms nor armies, and their ventures now reach across the world. During the Second War, Trade Prince Steamwheedle joined his cartel to Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde. Goblins supplied maps, siege craft, and knowledge of fleet-building in return for plunder taken from Stormwind. After the Horde fell, the cartels learned that open allegiance cost them profitable markets.\n\nToday, the major cartels claim neutrality and keep trade havens such as Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert. Alliance and Horde travelers meet there without drawn blades, while port bruisers keep the peace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_goblin-63f4f02440dab8a84006/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.6840816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:grimtotem-tribe"] = {
  hash = "4792be",
  fullHash = "4792be",
  text = "Grimtotem tribe. Across Kalimdor, the Grimtotem tribe raids settlements and keeps apart from Thrall's Horde. Dark hides and fierce warpaint mark them, and they seek to drive foreign races from Kalimdor and reclaim ancestral lands by any means. Their matriarch, Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem, resides atop Thunder Bluff while her kin raid across the continent. Grimtotem warriors train for ruthless combat rather than the patient harmony of the Great Hunt. They believe Kalimdor belongs only to the tauren. Their peaceful cousins honor the Earth Mother with reverence, but the Grimtotem disdain the other races settled on their ancient lands.\n\nCairne Bloodhoof welcomed them into Thunder Bluff, hoping to unite all tauren. Yet the Grimtotem remain the only major tribe never to formally join Thrall's Horde. They keep apart and pursue a violent cause. Beyond Thunder Bluff's watch, Grimtotem hostility turns to violence. In Stonetalon Mountains, they butcher local settlements and threaten Horde supply outposts. Across the mesas of Thousand Needles, they gather around Darkcloud Pinnacle, menace Freewind Post, and plot to seize the canyon.\n\nFeralas is no safer. They attack Horde travelers, Alliance caravans, and fellow tauren without hesitation. Horde garrisons at Sun Rock Retreat and Camp Mojache regularly post bounties and send warriors to cut down these violent outcasts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_grimtotem-tribe-54a0e93a7e51dd3cf3d6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.1412245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:grommash-hellscream"] = {
  hash = "f30a79",
  fullHash = "f30a79",
  text = "Grommash Hellscream. Grommash Hellscream's sacrifice has freed his people from the blood curse. The warsong chieftain once bound the orcs to the Burning Legion, but gave his life to slay mannoroth in ashenvale and free them. Before the Dark Portal opened, Grom Hellscream led the warsong clan with fierce resolve. His pitch-black tattooed jaw and the deadly sweep of Gorehowl, his two-handed axe, set him apart. On Draenor, Gul'dan and the Shadow Council offered the orcs demonic strength. Grom was the first chieftain to drink the blood of the pit lord mannoroth. The draught bound the orcs to demonic service and filled them with burning bloodlust.\n\nMany chieftains perished or lost themselves to the bloodlust, but Grom kept his fierce spirit through the old Horde's horrors. He carried a quiet ache for what his people had surrendered. Left behind on Draenor during the Second War, Grom later crossed the Dark Portal. The rift was shattered, and he was stranded. For nearly fifteen years, he and the surviving warsong warriors evaded the Alliance of Lordaeron. They endured lethargy and despair as the demon blood faded.\n\nThen a young fugitive named Thrall found Grom in the wilderness, and hope returned. Grom saw Thrall's strength and noble heart, and taught him the proud heritage of their people. With Orgrim Doomhammer, they rallied the scattered clans, broke open the human internment camps, and began the New Horde. During the Third War, Grom's aggression rose again in ashenvale's ancient forests. The night elves and the demigod Cenarius pressed his clan hard. To save them, Grom drank from a fountain corrupted with mannoroth's blood. Bound again to the demon lord, he turned against his own people.\n\nThrall and Jaina Proudmoore captured Grom and cleansed his spirit with ancient shamanic and arcane rites. Restored to his senses and burdened with guilt, he pursued mannoroth into Demon Fall Canyon. There Grom drove Gorehowl into the demon lord's chest, ending the demonic curse forever. Hellfire took Grom's life.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_grommash-hellscream-a5befec0a85c4f837e2f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 130.612245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:harpy"] = {
  hash = "a8c3b5",
  fullHash = "a8c3b5",
  text = "Harpy. Across Kalimdor and Alterac's high passes, Harpies foul their roosts and hunt travelers. Their shrieks and storm magic are feared, while night elf tales link them to Azshara's cursed betrayers. Their true origins remain uncertain. Harpies spread from the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains across Kalimdor. Their heads and upper bodies resemble night elf women, with broad feathered wings and wicked talons. Age makes their elven faces more striking, but their nature is savage. They terrorize Durotar's arid canyons, Teldrassil's sacred boughs, and the heights of Mulgore. Around their roosts, they despoil plant and animal life, leaving the picked-clean bones of their prey. Night elf tales say harpies began as handmaidens who betrayed Queen Azshara and were cursed before the Great Sundering. Their true origins remain uncertain. Today, ruthless matriarchies rule the harpies. Those who seek command betray, slaughter, and torment their rivals to hold it. Harpies tear flesh with razor-sharp claws and wield dangerous wind and lightning magic. From above, their shrieking gales batter their foes. Cunning hunters, they can speak and sometimes trade scavenged oddities with subterranean creatures such as kobolds for bright gems and shiny trinkets. Their elven forms can beguile unwary explorers, but their kiss is venomous and swiftly brings a violent, wasting fever.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_harpy-c24faaecaade2c73ed62/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.9061224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:highborne"] = {
  hash = "66d88e",
  fullHash = "66d88e",
  text = "Highborne. Across the ancient Kaldorei Empire, the Highborne wielded the Well of Eternity for Queen Azshara. Their sorcery drew the Burning Legion to Azeroth, unleashing the War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering. Before the Sundering, the Highborne rose around Queen Azshara as the favored upper class. They claimed superiority over common kaldorei through selective bloodlines and intelligence. Opulent privilege set them apart, and they kept tight control of the Well of Eternity.\n\nThere they learned to shape its primal arcane power. Across ancient Kalimdor, they raised magnificent cities. Their hunger for sorcery drove them to draw ever more deeply from the Well, until its calm waters grew turbulent and dark. The Highborne’s spellcraft drew the attention of the dark titan Sargeras and the Burning Legion. Azshara and her counselor Xavius persuaded the Highborne that demons would remake the world as an untainted paradise.\n\nThe Highborne opened a massive gateway inside the Well of Eternity, unleashing the War of the Ancients. Demonic hordes slaughtered the populace, while rebels rallied by Malfurion Stormrage fought to close the portal. Then the Well imploded and tore the supercontinent apart. After the Sundering, the Highborne met different fates. Those dragged beneath the waves with Azshara were twisted into serpentine naga. Others fell to demonic curses and became satyrs. Dath'Remar Sunstrider led a faction that aided the resistance but refused to give up arcane magic.\n\nExiled across the sea, they founded the high elven kingdom of Quel'Thalas. Today, isolated enclaves such as the Shen'dralar remain cloistered in Eldre'Thalas, shunned remnants of an empire that nearly destroyed Azeroth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_highborne-1596e5847699a408274f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.300408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:horde"] = {
  hash = "27d3a0",
  fullHash = "27d3a0",
  text = "Horde. Across Kalimdor and Lordaeron, the Horde seeks survival and freedom under warchief thrall. Orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren share shamanic bonds, while the Forsaken keep an uneasy place among them. Old grievances and hostile neighbors test a people who prize strength, action, and honor over political maneuvering. The Horde that stands now has left behind the demonic army that crossed the Dark Portal in the First and Second Wars. After freeing his people from the Burning Legion, warchief thrall led the orcs across the Great Sea to Kalimdor.\n\nThere they sought a spiritual homeland shaped by ancestral traditions and honor. Along the way, Thrall formed lasting ties with Vol'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls and Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren. Together, they fought centaur and demonic incursions during the Third War. In Kalimdor, the orcs founded Durotar and raised Orgrimmar as its fortress capital. They gave the Darkspear refuge on the nearby Echo Isles. Farther south, the nomadic tauren gathered across Mulgore's plains around Thunder Bluff under high chieftain cairne bloodhoof. These three peoples share shamanic reverence, a deep bond with nature, and the need to survive. Bloodshed and spiritual respect have bound them as family, not merely allies of convenience. Across the sea, in Lordaeron's ruined lands, lady sylvanas windrunner led the free-willed Forsaken out of the Scourge's grasp. Human factions and Scourge remnants still opposed them, so Sylvanas sought a place in the Horde. Tauren elders argued that the undead might yet be redeemed. Thrall accepted the Forsaken, giving the Horde a vital military outpost in the Eastern Kingdoms, though many in Kalimdor had misgivings. Thrall hopes to preserve peace with the Alliance, but the Old Horde's legacy still casts a shadow. To humans and dwarves, orcs remain bloodthirsty invaders, and the undead cannot be trusted.\n\nClashes over Ashenvale's vital lumber and skirmishes along territorial borders have left both factions in a tense, fragile cold war. Thrall directs campaigns against demon cults such as the Burning Blade to prove the Horde has broken with the Legion. Deep hatred still threatens to ignite all-out war at any spark.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_horde-93c394c2fd313b3e8962/1-0000.mp3", duration = 142.210612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:juggernaught"] = {
  hash = "6cb03a",
  fullHash = "6cb03a",
  text = "Juggernaught. During the Second War, ogres forged the Horde's answer to the Alliance fleet: the juggernaught. Its iron hull and heavy guns made it a floating fortress built to batter ships and coastal strongholds. During the Second War, the Horde needed ships to cross the Great Sea and meet the Alliance fleet. Orcs had little seafaring tradition, so they turned to ogre ingenuity. The ogre juggernaught became the backbone of the Horde armada. Slower and less nimble than troll destroyers, these great ships bore heavy plating and broadside guns. Their volleys broke Alliance hulls and reduced shore fortifications to rubble.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_juggernaught-e4e5b2d4d93575a55879/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.0865306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand"] = {
  hash = "34eec9",
  fullHash = "34eec9",
  text = "Knights of the Silver Hand. Across Stormwind, Ironforge, and the Plaguelands, the Knights of the Silver Hand still keep their vows. Founded after the First War, the order joined martial skill with the healing and smiting power of the Holy Light. Its northern chapter fell with Lordaeron, but knights still serve in the south and fight the undead in the north. After Stormwind fell in the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol saw that the Alliance needed holy warriors fit for battle. The clerics of Northshire could heal, but lacked the martial training to hold the front lines.\n\nSo Faol and his foremost student, Uther the Lightbringer, founded the Knights of the Silver Hand, training holy warriors to endure the Second War. They joined battle-hardened knights ready to learn devotion to the Light with devout clerics willing to wear plate armor and wield warhammers. Under Uther the Lightbringer, the first paladins became vital to the Alliance of Lordaeron. They rode into battle, lifted the spirits of weary soldiers, and faced the Horde's death knights and their sinister necromancy.\n\nDuring the war, the Silver Hand put down peasant rebellions, reclaimed Caer Darrow, and helped defend Capital City from the orc siege. In the peace that followed, paladins guarded Lordaeron's borders, settled civil disputes, and upheld honor, humility, and justice. The Third War fractured the order. Outside Stratholme, Prince Arthas Menethil, a decorated Silver Hand knight, clashed with his mentor Uther. Uther refused to butcher the infected citizens, so Arthas unlawfully suspended the paladins. Later, Arthas fell to corruption and led the Scourge against Lordaeron. Uther was slain, and the order lost its central leadership.\n\nThe surviving northern knights split over doctrine, giving rise to the zealous Scarlet Crusade and the vigilant Argent Dawn. In Stormwind and Ironforge, surviving chapters still train paladins under the Silver Hand's banner.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_knights-of-the-silver-hand-1fc43939e51606f0cbef/1-0000.mp3", duration = 124.734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kobold"] = {
  hash = "8cd394",
  fullHash = "8cd394",
  text = "Kobold. In Azeroth's mines and caverns, kobolds guard the candles fixed to their heads. Small and rat-like, they dig in the dark and defend their tunnels in numbers. Kobolds stand barely four feet tall. Filthy scavengers, they spend their lives delving underground. Their low-light vision fails in the deepest shafts, so each kobold fixes a crude candle to its head. Alone, one is cowardly when cornered. Yet kobolds breed in massive numbers and work with relentless energy. When agitated, their rat-like tails whip as they rush intruders in frantic swarms. They wield heavy iron mining picks and beat foes to death with them. Kobolds fear pitch-black tunnels with a deep, primal terror. To them, a candle is more than light. Its flame shields them from a darkness they believe will consume them. Across the Eastern Kingdoms, kobolds often overrun working mines. In Elwynn Forest, a clan displaced from the Redridge Mountains seized the Echo Ridge, Fargodeep, and Jasperlode mines. Riverpaw gnolls and the Defias Brotherhood helped drive them there. In Loch Modan, the Tunnel Rat clan seized the Silver Stream Mine from the dwarven Miners' League.\n\nKobolds also plague industrial digs in Kalimdor. They labor for the Venture Company in Stonetalon and uncover strange minerals in Thousand Needles.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kobold-2da8e251c80f5f382ffb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.1779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kolkar-clan"] = {
  hash = "7ec923",
  fullHash = "7ec923",
  text = "Kolkar clan. Across Kalimdor, the Kolkar clan raids settlements and turns on all who cross its path. The red-marked centaur claim descent from the first Khan, Kolk, and hold strongholds in Desolace and the Barrens. The Kolkar take their name from Khan Kolk, the first centaur khan. He was born from the union of Zaetar and Theradras. The clan hates other races and its fellow centaur clans, and its red markings set it apart. The Kolkar refuse all alliances. Their ancient village stood in eastern Desolace, where Khan Kolk's restless spirit still wanders beneath their ancestral lands. For generations, Kolkar raids harried the nomadic tauren crossing the arid Barrens. During the Third War, the orcs came to Kalimdor. Thrall joined Cairne Bloodhoof to drive the marauding centaur back and escort the tauren to safety in Mulgore. Defeat only deepened the feud. The Kolkar entrenched themselves around the Barrens' three vital water sources: the Forgotten Pools, the Lushwater Oasis, and the Stagnant Oasis. From fortified positions across the savanna, the Kolkar have pushed east past the Southfury River into Durotar. At Kolkar Crag in the south, raiding parties plot assaults on Sen'jin Village, the Valley of Trials, and the paths toward Orgrimmar. Senior Horde leaders face larger regional threats, so young recruits are sent to curb the centaur advance before its raiders strike the young orc capital.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kolkar-clan-a89baae4073233e6e85e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.7232653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:kul-tiras"] = {
  hash = "61da96",
  fullHash = "61da96",
  text = "Kul Tiras. Across the Great Sea, Kul Tiras keeps to its island waters west of Dun Morogh and Baradin Bay. Its navy once anchored the Alliance of Lordaeron. Since that alliance fractured and losses came in Kalimdor, the kingdom has drawn back. Kul Tiras rose to power on the work of its merchant marine and the wealth of its fishing grounds. Its naval armada had no equal in human history. When the Orcish Horde swept through the Eastern Kingdoms, Kul Tiras answered King Terenas Menethil II and joined the Alliance of Lordaeron. Lord Admiral Daelin Proudmoore commanded humanity's naval forces. They fought across the open ocean to cut the Horde's sea lanes and guard the coasts.\n\nAfter the war, the northern kingdoms eventually split. Kul Tiras drew back its fleets, strengthened its coastal defenses, and kept to itself.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_kul-tiras-883d66e4b2b66a9add32/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.5771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:lordaeron"] = {
  hash = "1b7329",
  fullHash = "1b7329",
  text = "Lordaeron. Across Lordaeron, the Scourge, Sylvanas Windrunner’s Forsaken, and desperate survivors contest the ruined kingdoms. Once the heart of human civilization and the Alliance, the northern subcontinent was laid waste in the Third War. Lordaeron takes its name from the human city-state that rose in the north after the ancient Empire of Arathor. In time, it became the most powerful of the human kingdoms. The region runs from the rugged Arathi Highlands in the south, across forests and waterways, toward Quel'Thalas.\n\nIt nurtured kingdoms such as Lordaeron, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. Tirisfal and Hillsbrad were known for fertile farmland, while mountains held strongholds. The land was also a sacred cradle of the Holy Light. During the Third War, the Lich King's invasion struck Lordaeron first. The Cult of the Damned used tainted grain to turn countless northern citizens into mindless undead. Prince Arthas Menethil fell to darkness, then returned from Northrend and killed his father, King Terenas Menethil II. He orchestrated the slaughter of the Capital City. The thriving heartland soon rotted into the blighted Plaguelands. Lordaeron is now a brutal battleground of fractured loyalties. Beneath the Capital City's ruins, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken have made the Undercity, a sovereign realm of free-willed undead aligned with the Horde. In the east, the Lich King's mindless Scourge hold corrupted keeps and strongholds. Human zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, surviving Alliance settlements in Hillsbrad, and wild forest trolls clash over what remains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_lordaeron-a81d03a56a7f093bf8d7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 103.026939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:medivh"] = {
  hash = "f08e1a",
  fullHash = "f08e1a",
  text = "Medivh. The Dark Portal stands open, and Medivh's hand has begun the First War. He was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order sworn to defend Azeroth from demonic corruption. Yet Sargeras's spirit had bound itself to Medivh before his birth. Born to Aegwynn, a powerful Guardian, and Nielas Aran, a court conjurer, Medivh inherited a cursed legacy. Centuries earlier, Aegwynn fought the fallen Titan Sargeras in Northrend, unaware that his dark essence had slipped into her body to await an heir. On Medivh's fourteenth birthday, the vast magic he inherited from his mother burst forth in a terrifying display. The boy fell into a coma that lasted a decade, watched over by clerics at Northshire Abbey. When Medivh awoke, he took up residence in the remote tower of Karazhan. He served as court magus to his childhood friend, King Llane Wrynn of Stormwind. The dark spirit within him slowly wore away his sanity. Seeking to unleash devastation, Medivh reached across the stars to Gul'dan, an orc warlock, and the Shadow Council.\n\nTogether they wielded tremendous magic to tear open the Dark Portal. The Horde poured into the Eastern Kingdoms, beginning the First War. Medivh died in his tower at the hands of his closest friend, Anduin Lothar, and his apprentice, Khadgar.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_medivh-465ee76a2c0cacc22fe5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.2081633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:mor-ladim"] = {
  hash = "9de4e1",
  fullHash = "9de4e1",
  text = "Mor'Ladim. At Raven Hill Cemetery, Mor'Ladim stalks the grounds as a vengeful skeletal warrior. Once Morgan Ladimore, he lost his family to Duskwood's ruin, then grief and madness drove him to rise in undeath and kill those who cross his path. Before Raven Hill feared his name, Morgan Ladimore defended the helpless from Grand Hamlet, now called Darkshire. He married Lys at eighteen, and they raised three children in peace. Then war called him north. During the Third War, Morgan marched with Uther the Lightbringer to Lordaeron. There he witnessed the fall of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the spread of Kel'Thuzad's plague, and Uther's death. Guilt over the innocent blood Morgan had shed and his suffocating grief stirred his corrupted remains from the earth. He rose as a hulking skeletal revenant, finding no peace in the grave. Travelers and the Night Watch soon learned to fear the name Mor'Ladim. With his ancestral blade Archeus, he patrols the misty graves, cutting down beasts and unwary travelers who stray near his resting place.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_mor-ladim-227dc4895b7409b453d9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.4669388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:murloc"] = {
  hash = "7053d5",
  fullHash = "7053d5",
  text = "Murloc. Along Azeroth's shores, lakes, and rivers, Murloc tribes gather and press inland from the deep ocean. Their cries and sudden swarms make them a threat, yet their deeds hint at minds sharper than their looks suggest. Murlocs gather along coasts, riverbanks, and freshwater lakes. They stand about four feet tall, with bulbous bodies coated in slime and wide mouths full of sharp fangs. Their keen sight pierces pitch-black water, and few stray far from their kin. Where one appears, a dozen more may wait below the surface. Together, they overwhelm foes with numbers and crude, sharp weapons. Ancient lineages say Murlocs lived beneath the waves before recorded history. Their spread onto Lordaeron and Kalimdor is recent, and began suspiciously alongside the naga's return from the ocean depths. Some tribes appear to worship monstrous beings in the deepest reaches. They may be fleeing darker terrors, or preparing the world for their masters' awakening. Their movement into freshwaters grows more coordinated, and more dangerous. Outsiders hear bubbling Murloc speech and mistake it for nonsense, dismissing them as feral pests. Yet their ambushes are coordinated, and they practice earth and water magic and live in communities. Explorer Brann Bronzebeard has suggested they hide their cunning and are far smarter than civilized races realize.\n\nMurlocs have shaped history, too. Their raid on the Darkspear tribe brought Thrall to intervene. That intervention forged the bond between orcs and trolls that built the modern Horde.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_murloc-308260b139b8188abab1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.5861224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:naga"] = {
  hash = "8cc2b3",
  fullHash = "8cc2b3",
  text = "Naga. Along shores across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, Naga press toward the surface. Once the Highborne elite of the Kaldorei Empire, they were changed in the Great Sundering and now serve their ancient empress, Queen Azshara, beneath the sea. Ten thousand years ago, the Highborne ruled the Kaldorei Empire from Zin-Azshari. Queen Azshara and her inner court drew on the Well of Eternity, consumed by arcane power, to summon the Dark Titan Sargeras. Their act brought the invasion called the War of the Ancients. Then the Well collapsed and the Great Sundering began. The palace sank into the crushing abyss.\n\nFacing annihilation, Azshara bargained with the Old Gods below. They spared her and her followers, changing them into cold, scaly serpentine beings bound to the deep. Beneath the Maelstrom, the survivors built the underwater empire of Nazjatar. For ten thousand years, the naga kept their Highborne arrogance and their grudge against the surface world. They became distinct casts: hulking warriors and sea witches with many arms, who weave spells. Under Azshara's gaze, they mastered sea creatures, storm magic, and frost sorcery. Their strength grew as they waited for a chance to strike at those who lived on land. The naga broke their silence during the Third War. Thrall's Horde first met the sea witch Zar'jira in the Great Sea. Lady Vashj answered Illidan Stormrage's summons and led naga forces through Dalaran's ruins.\n\nNow naga camps press against coasts in Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, Dustwallow Marsh, Hillsbrad, Arathi, and Stranglethorn. Ancient contempt and whispers of the Old Gods drive them to forge quiet alliances with the Twilight's Hammer and savage satyrs. They seek to claim what was once theirs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_naga-78a927ce95a5226e36f6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 115.356735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:new-horde"] = {
  hash = "7dc05b",
  fullHash = "7dc05b",
  text = "New Horde. Across Kalimdor and Lordaeron, the New Horde seeks a home and allies under warchief thrall. Orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren join in shamanic tradition, honor, and mutual survival, while a wary pact binds them to the Forsaken. The first Horde was forged by the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council as a weapon. Demonic bloodlust drove the orcs through the Dark Portal. After the Second War ended in ruin, the Alliance confined surviving orcs in internment camps, where they sank into soul-draining lethargy. Orgrim Doomhammer, Grommash Hellscream, and the young shaman Thrall helped the orcs break their demonic shackles. They reclaimed their ancestral shamanism and fought their way to freedom. Guided by visions from the mysterious Prophet, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor. Along the way, they welcomed the displaced Darkspear trolls of Sen'jin. On Kalimdor, Thrall joined Cairne Bloodhoof and the nomadic tauren to drive back hostile centaur tribes. Together with humans and night elves, they fought at Mount Hyjal and broke the Burning Legion's invasion. Then they made their home in Durotar. From their fortified capital of Orgrimmar, the New Horde works to build a lasting society. Orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren share strong bonds, while the need to survive has drawn Thrall into uneasy alliances. One binds him to Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken in the ruins of Lordaeron. Thrall seeks peace with the Alliance, yet old enmities, scarce resources, and hawkish voices on both sides keep the borders close to war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_new-horde-e111b775b2d6ff710790/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.571429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:night-elf"] = {
  hash = "ef73ac",
  fullHash = "ef73ac",
  text = "Night elf. Night elves now defend their ancestral forests beside the Alliance. The kaldorei are an ancient, reclusive people of northern Kalimdor. Once rulers of a vast magical empire, they turned from arcane magic after the War of the Ancients and embraced Elune and druidism. In the Third War, they gave up immortality to stop the Burning Legion, then joined the Alliance as the Horde pressed into their lands. Beside the Well of Eternity, the kaldorei began as a curious nocturnal tribe. Over generations, its raw arcane power changed them into tall, violet-skinned, long-lived people. They called themselves kaldorei, meaning “children of the stars.” Elune, the moon goddess, and Cenarius, the forest demigod, guided their early society and bound them to Kalimdor’s living wilds. Queen Azshara led the Highborne, the ruling caste, in pursuit of the Well’s endless arcane secrets. Their sorcery raised breathtaking wonders, but its reckless use drew the Burning Legion to Azeroth. The invasion ended in the Great Sundering. The Well imploded, breaking ancient Kalimdor into separate continents and destroying Azshara’s realm. The surviving kaldorei outlawed arcane magic, on pain of death or exile. For ten thousand years, the night elves kept to themselves. Druids slept in the Emerald Dream to heal the world, while the Priestesses of Elune and Sentinels guarded Kalimdor’s forests beneath Nordrassil. Their seclusion ended when the Legion returned in the Third War and attacked Mount Hyjal. The kaldorei gave up immortality to defeat Archimonde.\n\nHigh Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and Archdruid Fandral Staghelm now lead their people from their young World Tree, Teldrassil. The night elves fight beside the Alliance to protect Ashenvale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_night-elf-b950f844439ec8e003e3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 114.729796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:night-watch"] = {
  hash = "7ee15c",
  fullHash = "7ee15c",
  text = "Night Watch. In Duskwood, the Night Watch holds Darkshire and the forest roads against rising threats. Stormwind's House of Nobles has left the volunteers to face undead, feral worgen, and necromancy under Commander Althea Ebonlocke. They rely on travelers to defend their home. Stormwind withdrew its royal garrison after foul magic consumed the Grand Hamlet and changed the forest into Duskwood. King Varian Wrynn had disappeared, so the troops were recalled to the capital. Darkshire's townsfolk were left without royal soldiers or supplies. They formed the Night Watch and swore to defend the borders from horrors creeping out of the woods. From its headquarters in Darkshire, the Night Watch patrols roads buried in unnatural mist. Skeletal fiends and ghouls rise from Raven Hill and Tranquil Gardens cemeteries. Feral worgen prowl Roland's Doom and Brightwood Grove. The militia is badly outnumbered and short of resources, and many of its finest defenders have fallen to threats like Mor'Ladim, the wandering skeletal warrior. Commander Althea Ebonlocke, daughter of Darkshire's mayor, directs the defense with grim resolve. With no veteran reinforcements, she calls on capable travelers to thin skeletal legions, cull starving wolf packs, and investigate mysterious local murders. The Night Watch is the last barrier protecting human life in the region. If its lines break, Darkshire will fall into shadow.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_night-watch-35eb4c19381c3a83ffc9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.3240816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:nordrassil"] = {
  hash = "d84a36",
  fullHash = "d84a36",
  text = "Nordrassil. Above Mount Hyjal, Nordrassil bears its wounds, and the night elves know age and sickness. Planted over the second Well of Eternity, the World Tree once sheltered its power and gave the kaldorei immortality for ten thousand years. Its sacrifice broke Archimonde’s assault during the Third War. Nordrassil rose from an acorn of G'Hanir above the new Well of Eternity. Ten thousand years ago, after the Great Sundering, Illidan Stormrage poured vials of the original Well into a lake atop Mount Hyjal. There he made a new font of arcane power.\n\nTo keep its magic from corrupting the world or drawing the Burning Legion back, Alexstrasza planted the acorn. Nordrassil’s roots reached deep beneath Azeroth, sealing the Well’s volatile energies and spreading gentle, life-giving vigor across the continents. Three Great Dragon Aspects blessed Nordrassil, and the tree became the spiritual and cultural heart of night elf civilization. Alexstrasza filled it with vigor that sustained the kaldorei. Ysera bound its roots to the Emerald Dream, so night elf druids could enter her sleeping realm with effortless clarity.\n\nNozdormu the Timeless One laid his blessing over its boughs. While the tree stood, the night elves would not age or succumb to natural disease. For ten thousand years, the kaldorei knew immortality. During the Third War, Archimonde brought his war to Nordrassil. The demon lord marched on Mount Hyjal to tear apart the tree and seize the Well’s infinite power. As the mortal defenders neared defeat, Malfurion Stormrage sounded the Horn of Cenarius and called thousands of nature spirits. Then he unleashed the enchantments within Nordrassil. The blast destroyed Archimonde and shattered the Legion’s assault.\n\nNordrassil survived, but its enchantments broke. The night elves lost their immortality, and the wounded tree began a slow healing.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_nordrassil-87fad38ec5b42319cc01/1-0000.mp3", duration = 125.988571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:old-horde"] = {
  hash = "003827",
  fullHash = "003827",
  text = "Old Horde. Across Draenor and Azeroth, the Old Horde left conquest in its wake. Demonic power drove the orc clans to conquer Draenor and invade Azeroth. Blackhand and then Orgrim Doomhammer led them through the First and Second Wars, until the Alliance of Lordaeron defeated them. Thrall's New Horde now seeks to overcome that legacy, while remnants such as the Dark Horde cling to the bloody past. Kil'jaeden turned the orc clans toward war before they crossed into Azeroth. Through the warlock Gul'dan and the elder shaman Ner'zhul, the demon lord corrupted the proud, spiritual orcs. As the clans fought, the elemental spirits abandoned them. The orcs cast aside shamanism for warlock magic. Then they drank Mannoroth's blood and gave themselves to bloodlust. They slaughtered the draenei and drained life from their homeland. Draenor's withering lands could no longer provide what the Horde needed. Gul'dan and Medivh, a human guardian possessed by demonic power, opened the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The Horde poured through and began the First War against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Warchief Blackhand led them, while Gul'dan's Shadow Council secretly directed events.\n\nNear the war's end, Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand and purged the Shadow Council. He took command, then razed Stormwind to the ground. Orgrim Doomhammer led the Horde north across the seas to fight the Second War. There, the Horde joined forces with forest trolls and goblin mercenaries. At the height of the siege of Lordaeron, Gul'dan and his loyalists abandoned the war to seek the Tomb of Sargeras. Betrayed and divided, the Horde was driven back to Blackrock Mountain and crushed by the Alliance of Lordaeron.\n\nThe Alliance destroyed the portal and herded surviving orcs into internment camps. As the demonic blood haze faded, lethargy settled over them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_old-horde-66e571006c15228bd3a6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.04, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:orc"] = {
  hash = "4335b6",
  fullHash = "4335b6",
  text = "Orc. In Durotar, orcs lead the Horde and seek honor after a long fall into demonic corruption. Once shamanistic clans on Draenor, they were deceived by the Burning Legion, invaded Azeroth, and were defeated and interned. Warchief Thrall led them back to their spiritual heritage and into a new home. Across Draenor, orcs once roamed as nomadic hunter-gatherers in spiritual clans. They honored their ancestors and communed with elemental spirits, guided by revered shamans. Among their clans were the Frostwolves, Warsongs, and Shadowmoon. Orcs were physically formidable and proud, yet their society prized survival, honor, and harmony with the elements over conquest. Kil'jaeden and the warlock Gul'dan changed the orcs' fate through corruption. Deceived into drinking the blood of the demon Mannoroth, they fell to supernatural bloodlust. The original Horde nearly wiped out Draenor's draenei, then crossed the Dark Portal into Azeroth.\n\nIn the First and Second Wars, human kingdoms fell and orcish armies reached Lordaeron's gates. Treachery within their ranks and the united Alliance of Lordaeron defeated them, and survivors were driven into internment camps. As prisoners, the orcs lost their demonic frenzy and grew lethargic and broken. Thrall, raised in human captivity, escaped and returned to shamanism. He rallied the Frostwolf clan, freed his kin from the camps, and formed a renewed Horde. After receiving prophetic warnings, he led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor.\n\nGrom Hellscream's sacrifice finally broke Mannoroth's blood curse. The orcs now live in Durotar, where they built the fortress-city of Orgrimmar. They strive to keep their hard-won honor amid renewed tensions with the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_orc-8d01912ced0626d740d6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 112.613878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:pirate"] = {
  hash = "211c48",
  fullHash = "211c48",
  text = "Pirate. Across Azeroth's seas, pirates prey on ships from every shore. Their crews strike Alliance, Horde, and neutral merchant vessels across the Great Sea and coastal waters. They gather under loose flags to seize wealth, cargo, and coves, sparking naval fights from Kalimdor to the Eastern Kingdoms. Pirate crews roam Azeroth's seas, raiding ships and coastal settlements. They are called buccaneers, corsairs, or freebooters, and nearly every race joins their ranks. Their work ranges from smuggling and kidnappings to mercenary contracts for anyone with coin. After the Third War and the collapse of northern kingdoms, naval enforcement fractured. Pirates seized the chance to build fortified footholds on neglected islands and in coastal coves. Life aboard a buccaneer vessel is brutal and often short. Crews endure squalid, rat-infested holds and harsh discipline, while supplies of limes hold scurvy at bay. Betrayal is common, and captains keep command through strength and fear of punishment. A sailor might face execution, marooning, or be fed to sharks. After battle, buccaneers celebrate with dice, song, and heavy rum. Independent crews roam every sea lane, while larger organizations command enough ships to challenge sovereign navies. The Southsea Freebooters harass shipping in the waters around Kalimdor. The Bloodsail Buccaneers menace the southern seas and wage open war against the Steamwheedle Cartel in Booty Bay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_pirate-f9b020ffe1a8a31298a5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.9583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:plague-of-undeath"] = {
  hash = "9f7515",
  fullHash = "9f7515",
  text = "Plague of Undeath. Across the northern Eastern Kingdoms, the Plague of Undeath spreads at the Burning Legion's command. The Lich King fashioned it to scour the region, and tainted grain, airborne cauldrons, and poisoned waterways carry it to the living. The sickness kills mortals swiftly and raises them as thralls bound to the Scourge. After Kil'jaeden transformed the fallen shaman Ner'zhul into the Lich King, Ner'zhul devised the plague. From the Frozen Throne in Northrend, he perfected it as a weapon for the Burning Legion's invasion. He tested it on isolated human villages across Northrend, learning how to extinguish life and command the corpses that soon rose.\n\nHis summons crossed the Great Sea and reached Kel'Thuzad, a Kirin Tor mage who left Dalaran to serve him. Kel'Thuzad formed the secretive Cult of the Damned. Its mortal members infiltrated Lordaeron and prepared to poison the kingdom from within. The blight reaches beyond humanity, and the land bears its demonic and necromantic decay. Non-human wildlife usually suffers fever or rot instead of rising as undead thralls. Across the Plaguelands, massive plague cauldrons send alchemical and magical vapors into the air, corrupting the land around them. Carrion grubs and colossal mushrooms choked with spores thrive there.\n\nThe Argent Dawn sabotages the cauldrons, reversing their mixtures so they burn the Scourge instead of feeding it. In the Undercity, the Royal Apothecary Society keeps experimenting to refine a New Plague meant to kill both the living and the undead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_plague-of-undeath-70281efa76532362cd76/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.324898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:quilboar"] = {
  hash = "2c8ae8",
  fullHash = "2c8ae8",
  text = "Quilboar. Across central Kalimdor, Quilboar defend their lands with sharp quills and fierce resolve. They claim descent from Agamaggan and dwell in thorn mazes across Durotar, Mulgore, and the Barrens. Their struggles over water, soil, and sacred ground threaten travelers and Horde settlements. Quilboar honor Agamaggan, the colossal boar Ancient who died fighting the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. Their sacred traditions call them his mortal offspring.\n\nWhere Agamaggan bled and died across central Kalimdor, enormous thorny vines burst from scorched earth and formed sprawling root mazes. Quilboar hold these places sacred. Across the Barrens, his crystallized blood remains as crimson Blood Shards. Quilboar geomancers dig them up and honor them as powerful talismans. Quilboar build no towns of stone or timber. Instead, they grow colossal briars that choke nearby plants and leave the ground barren. Each tribe is usually guided by powerful geomancers. These practitioners follow a crude, earthbound shamanism, consulting stones and crystals to guide their clan. They call on Agamaggan's spirit to send spectral boars into battle.\n\nThe arid central plains have few oases, so quilboar prize water above all. When rare rains come, they hold lavish feasts. They defend their water holes fiercely. For generations, quilboar have clashed with nomadic tauren and roaming centaur packs. When Thrall led the Orcish Horde into Kalimdor, orcs quickly settled Durotar and opened trade routes through the Barrens. Displaced quilboar tribes rose in revolt.\n\nTheir war parties ambush supply wagons, are suspected of driving tallstriders into wagon trains, and raid outposts such as Crossroads and Camp Taurajo. Two tribes dominate the open plains: the Razormane and the Bristleback. Both fight the Horde and each other.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_quilboar-0cfb209720828eb369d1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 127.216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:ravenholdt"] = {
  hash = "9a5840",
  fullHash = "9a5840",
  text = "Ravenholdt. In the mountains above the Hillsbrad Foothills, Ravenholdt wages a shadow war against the Syndicate. Lord Jorach Ravenholdt leads the Assassin's League, a company of rogues, thieves, and killers that keeps clear of faction politics. It recruits the most cunning operatives across Azeroth, prized for discipline, stealth, and lethal efficiency. Ravenholdt Manor gathers Azeroth's foremost brotherhood of assassins in the mountains between Hillsbrad and Alterac. Lord Jorach Ravenholdt leads the League beyond the reach of kings and warchiefs.\n\nMaster Kang teaches close combat and assassination, while the Thunderwood brothers brew rare, deadly poisons. Gnome craftsmen build intricate locks to test new disciples. For anyone who is not a rogue, entering the estate courts death; one careless step can bring a poisoned dagger to the ribs. For years, Ravenholdt has waged an escalating feud against the Syndicate, an aristocratic criminal cartel that infests Alterac and Arathi. The League sees its rivals as clumsy, arrogant amateurs, yet their numbers make them impossible to wipe out through slaughter alone. So contracted rogues pickpocket the signet emblems worn by Syndicate ringleaders. Ravenholdt counts on the Syndicate's paranoia and ruthless hierarchy to execute its disgraced members from within.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_ravenholdt-a43428a0b56b99e6814b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.7559184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:razorfen"] = {
  hash = "70a45c",
  fullHash = "70a45c",
  text = "Razorfen. Across southern Kalimdor, Razorfen's quilboar defend their thorn-walled homes. Their strongholds span the southern Barrens and the edge of Thousand Needles, where their faith and power run deep. Razorfen's thorned brambles reach back to the War of the Ancients. Legend says they rose where Agamaggan, the giant boar Ancient, fell fighting the Burning Legion ten thousand years ago. His blood entered the earth, and vast vines of petrified bramble and iron-hard thorns grew there. The quilboar, his descendants, have lived among these defenses ever since. Razorfen's quilboar are among the most aggressive and firmly rooted of their kind. They build their communities among the vast briars instead of scattering into open warrens. From these strongholds, they defend their ancestral land against neighboring tauren tribes, Horde settlements in the Barrens, and any outsider who enters their holy grounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_razorfen-129da697a66fa5e75c19/1-0000.mp3", duration = 56.9730612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:refugee"] = {
  hash = "729392",
  fullHash = "729392",
  text = "Refugee. Across Azeroth, Refugees flee as invasion and betrayal bring war to their homes. From Stormwind's fall in the First War to Lordaeron's ruin in the Third, civilians lose their homes and face plague and worse. The Orcish Horde swept through Stormwind in the First War, burning Goldshire, Moonbrook, and Grand Hamlet. After King Llane Wrynn was assassinated, Sir Anduin Lothar took command of the kingdom's remnants. He gathered surviving soldiers and desperate civilians, then led them to the harbor as the city burned.\n\nLothar guided a massive fleet across the Great Sea to Lordaeron's northern shores. Near Southshore, King Terenas Menethil II granted sanctuary to the exiles, among them the young Prince Varian Wrynn. A generation after Stormwind's exiles found welcome, the Scourge plague and undead legions brought ruin to Lordaeron in the Third War. Prince Arthas Menethil slaughtered his own subjects as he swept across the realm, scattering survivors in terror. Lady Jaina Proudmoore led an expedition of refugees across the sea to Kalimdor, where they eventually established a settlement at Theramore.\n\nCountless others remained in the Eastern Kingdoms. The Scourge, the Forsaken, and monstrous horrors risen from the ruins hunted them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_refugee-5e21e4785d0ab6ebf711/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.117551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:riverpaw-pack"] = {
  hash = "915301",
  fullHash = "915301",
  text = "Riverpaw pack. In Elwynn and Westfall, the Riverpaw pack raids farms and roads and rules local kobolds. Since the Third War, its attacks have grown bolder and better organized as Stormwind's forces are stretched thin. For decades, Riverpaw gnolls stayed near the edges of human lands. In recent years, their raids have grown bolder. In Elwynn Forest, packs led by the infamous brute Hogger prowl the southern woods around Forest's Edge and the shores of Stone Cairn Lake. In Westfall, they cross the Dust Plains and coast, ambushing lone travelers and raiding homesteads.\n\nDeputy Rainer at Westbrook Garrison and Captain Danuvin at Sentinel Hill rely on mercenaries to thin their ranks. They offer standing bounties for painted armbands and severed paws. The Riverpaws seek more than easy prey. They drove a clan of kobolds from the Redridge Mountains and forced them to work in gold mines across Elwynn Forest and Westfall. There, the kobolds labor for ore until exhaustion.\n\nTheir hold over the kobolds and the mines shows a clan gathering wealth and pressing into strategic ground. Stormwind's attention is elsewhere.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_riverpaw-pack-b764fed4649d84cd815f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.5044898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:royal-apothecary-society"] = {
  hash = "184513",
  fullHash = "184513",
  text = "Royal Apothecary Society. Beneath the Undercity, the Royal Apothecary Society works under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. Its Forsaken researchers seek a New Plague to destroy the Scourge and avenge themselves on the living. They tell Horde allies they seek a cure for undeath, while agents gather venoms, slimes, and toxic reagents across Azeroth. After the Forsaken won their freedom, the Royal Apothecary Society became the scientific and alchemical arm of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom. In the Apothecarium's vats and underground laboratories, researchers try to turn the force that damned them into a weapon. Their chief work is a New Plague meant to eradicate the Scourge and destroy the Lich King.\n\nHuman alchemists often pursue healing draughts or transmutation. The Society experiments with pathogens, caustic acids, and necromantic mixtures. Its alchemists also raise fallen warhorses as skeletal steeds and brew ever deadlier strains of toxic blight. The Apothecary Society sends its work far beyond the Undercity's canals. Field apothecaries serve at remote outposts across Lordaeron and Kalimdor. In Silverpine and Hillsbrad, Apothecaries Renferrel and Lydon gather local plants and distill venoms. In the arid Barrens, Apothecary Helbrim catalogs strange desert plants and creatures.\n\nApothecary Zamah works beneath Thunder Bluff in the Pools of Vision. The Society sends acolytes wherever natural toxins, shifting oozes, or virulent organisms can be found. They collect samples for the Apothecarium's cauldrons.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_royal-apothecary-society-261be951494a6c304cb7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.2465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:ruins-of-gilneas"] = {
  hash = "6f4579",
  fullHash = "6f4579",
  text = "Gilneas. Beyond the Greymane Wall, Gilneas keeps the world at a distance. The human kingdom lies on a rugged peninsula south of Silverpine Forest, sealed away after the Second War by King Genn Greymane. Gilneas once ruled the waves among the human kingdoms and traced its line to Arathor, the ancient human empire. King Genn Greymane entered the Second War reluctantly. He believed his realm could defend itself without ties to foreign powers.\n\nBitter over Gilnean deaths and unwilling to help pay for internment camps holding captured orcs, he broke with the Alliance of Lordaeron. Then he ordered a great stone wall along Gilneas's northern frontier to shield the kingdom from calamity beyond its borders. The Greymane Wall left two villages exposed. Its builders anchored the ramparts to natural mountain passes, leaving Ambermill and Pyrewood Village beyond the gate on the northern side. When the Scourge crossed Lordaeron during the Third War, King Greymane refused pleas for aid. He barred human refugees from entering, and the villages beyond the wall faced plague and undead alone.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_ruins-of-gilneas-fca15f41f4f92c1a56db/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.2171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:sargeras"] = {
  hash = "83ccf7",
  fullHash = "83ccf7",
  text = "Sargeras. Once the Pantheon’s greatest warrior, Sargeras now leads the Burning Legion against all life. Despair turned him against his kind, and his designs brought the Sundering and later demonic catastrophes. Sargeras once guarded the Pantheon’s creations across the Great Dark Beyond. The Pantheon of Order had charged their greatest warrior with that duty. There he fought demons born in the Twisting Nether. Unable to imagine pure evil, he trapped countless fiends in a great prison. Yet eons of battle against cosmic horrors slowly wore down his altruistic spirit. Across eons of battle, Sargeras lost faith in the titans’ work. Despair at the evil he faced led him to believe creation was flawed and order could never prevail. He turned from the Pantheon and shattered the demons’ prison. Then he took up the chaotic fel fire he had once fought. Sargeras gathered the freed demons into the Burning Legion, an army bent on scouring life from the cosmos. Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne wielded reckless magic that reached across the stars. That magic revealed Azeroth to Sargeras. From the depths of the Well of Eternity, the Dark Titan whispered to Azshara and seduced her into opening a portal for his vanguard.\n\nThe invasion ended in the catastrophic Sundering. Yet Sargeras kept plotting to consume Azeroth. He orchestrated the opening of the Dark Portal and guided the demonic corruption of the orcish Horde.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_sargeras-41e9bb12501baf0b27fe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.9477551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:satyr"] = {
  hash = "322723",
  fullHash = "322723",
  text = "Satyr. Across Kalimdor, satyr sects foul woodlands and moonwells. They began as Highborne night elves, transformed by Sargeras during the War of the Ancients. Now they torment their kaldorei kin and spread fel corruption in the Burning Legion’s name. During the War of the Ancients, Xavius failed to hold the Burning Legion’s first gateway against Malfurion Stormrage. Sargeras tore apart the Highborne councilor’s spirit, then remade him as punishment and a tool. Xavius kept an elven torso but gained horns, taloned claws, a leonine tail, and a goat’s cloven lower body.\n\nSent back to Zin-Azshari, Xavius eagerly spread his fel affliction among other power-hungry Highborne. Xavius was struck down, but his demonic descendants survived. They still seek to make more of their kind by corrupting others. After the Great Sundering, surviving satyrs continued to threaten the shattered night elf civilization. Over the ages, they were broken and driven away. Their remnants split into scattered sects that linger in dark forests and ancient ruins, still hating their kaldorei cousins. Satyr clans now dwell in deep woodland enclaves across Kalimdor, from Darkshore and Ashenvale to Felwood, Azshara, and Feralas. They feed on the land’s pure magic, leeching and spoiling nature until their hooves swell with drained arcane energy.\n\nSatyrs turn peaceful furbolg tribes violent, taint ancient moonwells, and work as covert liaisons for the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. Some join Twilight’s Hammer cultists or hide in ancient ruins such as Dire Maul. All remain bound to the Burning Legion’s shadow.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_satyr-9ab847911fcf1fae43a9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.161633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:scourge"] = {
  hash = "182df3",
  fullHash = "182df3",
  text = "Scourge. Across Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas, the Scourge leaves kingdoms in ruin. The Burning Legion made it to weaken Azeroth before the Third War. Its undead, necromancers, and enslaved spirits broke free of demonic control. Now it wages war from Northrend and the corrupted Plaguelands. The Burning Legion made the Scourge to destroy Azeroth. The demon lords bound the spirit of fallen orc shaman Ner'zhul to the Frozen Throne in Northrend and made him the Lich King. His terrifying telepathic power was meant to prepare the world for conquest. He sent the Plague of Undeath south into Lordaeron, where mortal traitors in the Cult of the Damned poisoned the grain. Those who died rose again as twisted thralls bound to his will. Prince Arthas Menethil heeded the Lich King's whispers, and the Scourge brought ruin to the north. Seeking vengeance in Northrend, Arthas claimed Frostmourne and lost his soul. He became the Scourge's foremost death knight. Returning home, he slew his father, King Terenas, butchered Capital City, and sent the undead across Lordaeron.\n\nThey swept through Darrowshire and Andorhal, slaughtered the Silver Hand paladins, and destroyed Dalaran to summon Archimonde. Across Quel'Thalas, they cut a dead scar to raise Kel'Thuzad as a lich. Ner'zhul never meant to serve the Burning Legion forever. Through Arthas, he secretly brought about the Legion's defeat by urging Illidan Stormrage to kill the dreadlord Tichondrius in Felwood. Enraged, Kil'jaeden later ordered Illidan to destroy the Frozen Throne. Illidan's attack cracked the icy prison and weakened the Lich King's hold over his minions.\n\nLed by the resurrected banshee Sylvanas Windrunner, a faction of free-willed undead broke away and took the name Forsaken. They seized the ruins of Lordaeron. Even after the Frozen Throne was damaged and the Forsaken rebelled, the Scourge remains the deadliest force in the northern world. The lich Kel'Thuzad leads its forces in the field. Abominations, crypt fiends, and necromancers rule the corrupted Eastweald and Westweald, now called the Plaguelands.\n\nFrom grim strongholds and blighted fields, the Scourge wages war against the Forsaken of the Undercity, the fanatics of the Scarlet Crusade, and the Alliance's remaining enclaves.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_scourge-0bb87211a931af43edf1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 147.722449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:second-war"] = {
  hash = "bd45b3",
  fullHash = "bd45b3",
  text = "Second War. Across the Eastern Kingdoms, the Second War rages between the Orcish Horde and the newly forged Alliance of Lordaeron. After Stormwind falls, battle sweeps through Khaz Modan and the northern human realms, then reaches the gates of Capital City. The war ends when the Dark Portal is destroyed and the old Horde is scattered. After Stormwind fell in the First War, Regent Anduin Lothar led human refugees north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. King Terenas Menethil II called the Council of Seven Nations and forged the Alliance of Lordaeron. The high elves of Quel'Thalas, the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, and the gnomes of Gnomeregan joined it.\n\nAcross the sea, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer gathered the Horde. He allied with forest troll tribes and the Steamwheedle Cartel. The Dragonmaw clan brought red dragons under Horde control for the invasion of the northern continents. The Horde struck by land and sea, swiftly overrunning Khaz Modan. Dwarves and gnomes fell back behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. Horde forces crossed the Thandol Span and the seas, burning through Hillsbrad and the Hinterlands before laying siege to Capital City.\n\nAlterac betrayed the Alliance and guided orc divisions through mountain passes. Dragon attacks battered the defenders, but the Alliance rallied around the newly formed Knights of the Silver Hand. Their paladins wielded the Holy Light against orcish warlocks and death knights. The war turned when the warlock Gul'dan left the Horde with his followers to seek personal power. Doomhammer had to draw vital forces away from the front lines. The Alliance counterattacked, broke the siege of Lordaeron, and drove the Horde south across the Thandol Span, freeing Khaz Modan along the way.\n\nIn the final mountain passes of the Burning Steppes, Anduin Lothar fell in single combat against Doomhammer. Lothar's lieutenant Turalyon rallied the troops and routed the orcs. The Alliance pursued the survivors to the Black Morass, shattered the remnants, and demolished the Dark Portal.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_second-war-6fcf0dbca4b49cfb95a7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 131.265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:sentinels"] = {
  hash = "11e150",
  fullHash = "11e150",
  text = "Sentinels. Across Kalimdor, the Sentinels defend night elf lands and meet the Horde in battle. Formed after the War of the Ancients, this elite force is guided by the Sisterhood of Elune and led by General Shandris Feathermoon. Its mostly female ranks guard settlements and sacred ground from Ashenvale to Feralas, and watch over the night elves’ new home on Teldrassil. The Sentinels took shape after the War of the Ancients laid bare the failures of the old Kaldorei military. Before the Great Sundering, noble birth decided rank in the ancient Kaldorei Empire, while skill in battle counted for little. That order could not shield Azeroth from the war’s devastation.\n\nAfterward, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind remade night elf society. She looked to the Sisterhood of Elune, an order that had survived intact and knew tactics, combat, and healing. From its devoted ranks, Tyrande formed a standing army to guard the growing society, whatever a Sentinel’s heritage. Tyrande Whisperwind remains the Sentinels’ spiritual supreme commander. Her lieutenant, General Shandris Feathermoon, directs their active military from Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas. Sentinel discipline is known in the bow and the deadly moonglaive, and in their specialized forces.\n\nElite archers fight beside huntresses riding nightsabers. Scouts mounted on hippogryphs command the skies. Centuries before the Third War, the Sentinels fought under Archdruid Fandral Staghelm in the brutal War of the Shifting Sands in Silithus, where they halted the qiraji swarm. From Astranaar to Darnassus’s spreading branches, night elf settlements rely on Sentinel garrisons for protection. During the Third War, the Sentinels faced demons and early Horde incursions in desperate battles. They suffered immense losses when Cenarius fell in Ashenvale. They survived the Battle of Mount Hyjal and pursued Illidan across Lordaeron, then returned to a changed Kalimdor.\n\nNow danger presses from several fronts. On Teldrassil, garrisons face corrupted wildlife and enraged furbolgs. Farther south in Warsong Gulch, the Silverwing Sentinels fight the Warsong Outriders. They are determined to drive the Horde from Ashenvale and protect the ancient forests from further exploitation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_sentinels-ae82fdbe0c62a50a2a7a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 144.587755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:skyborne"] = {
  hash = "49d276",
  fullHash = "27b7d4",
  text = "Skyborne. In Skywall, the Skyborne face a failing refuge and the loss of those who once guarded it. Their ancestors, high elves descended from night elves, fled Eldre'Thalas after resisting Prince Tortheldrin's demonic pacts. A bargain with wind spirits carried them to Zephras Isle, but now the spirits are gone and the island is breaking apart. After the Sundering, the Shen'dralar of Eldre'Thalas struggled without the Well of Eternity. Prince Tortheldrin sought salvation by summoning the demon Immol'thar and draining its power. Nobles and citizens feared another Legion invasion, so they resisted. Tortheldrin answered with a purge that killed dozens of houses.\n\nSurvivors rallied behind a masked figure called the Shal'nan, or the Veiled One. A desperate raid on Tortheldrin's ritual sanctum unleashed chaotic magic. The Shal'nan gathered the survivors and led them west to the port of Eldre'narr. They took to the sea as the Prince's fleet pursued them. At sea, the exiles found themselves cornered, so they made a pact with primordial wind spirits. The spirits lifted Zephras Isle into the sky and anchored it in Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air. During the Shrine of the Four Winds' consecration, the Shal'nan gave up absolute rule and entrusted governance to an elected council.\n\nOver millennia in this strange realm, the night elf exiles became fair, slender high elves. In recent generations, more children have been born with pale blue skin through the Sky-Touched Blessing.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_skyborne-9d8a1acd693a496f1f37/1-0000.mp3", duration = 97.515102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:skywall"] = {
  hash = "3d15cc",
  fullHash = "3d15cc",
  text = "Skywall. Within the Elemental Plane, Al'Akir rules Skywall and its air elementals. The titans banished them there long ago, to a realm of wind, cloud, and lightning. The titans made the Elemental Plane a prison for Azeroth's unruly elemental spirits. After overthrowing the Old Gods, they sealed the spirits away from the surface world. The elemental lords fought among themselves and carved out separate dominions. Al'Akir the Windlord rules Skywall, where tempests still swirl in isolation. Old feuds followed the elemental lords into exile. Skywall has long fought over its borders with nearby realms, including the Firelands. During the Elemental Sundering, Garr and Baron Geddon, chief lieutenants of Ragnaros the Firelord, ambushed and betrayed Prince Thunderaan the Windseeker. Ragnaros devoured the prince's essence and bound its fragments in a shattered talisman. Beyond gales and djinn citadels, Zephras Isle drifts among Skywall's cloudbanks. The isle is a sanctuary inhabited by the Shen'dorei. Mortals on Azeroth rarely pierce the veil into the air realm. Across history, reckless spellcasters and weapon-crafters have risked disaster to siphon the Windlord's living storms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_skywall-7abaa4d1d66ff0f9cd96/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.684898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:steamwheedle-cartel"] = {
  hash = "96ba81",
  fullHash = "96ba81",
  text = "Steamwheedle Cartel. In its ports, the Steamwheedle Cartel welcomes Alliance and Horde alike, so long as their coin is genuine. This powerful goblin enterprise has hubs in Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan. Its trade reaches shipping, alchemy, salvaging, and mercenary contracts. The Steamwheedle Cartel began beneath the earth in Undermine, the goblin capital. During the Second War, it backed the Old Horde with war machines, sappers, and naval engineering. When the war ended, its leaders judged that choosing sides cost more than it earned.\n\nSo the cartel turned to neutrality and spread across both continents, opening trade to Alliance and Horde alike. As tensions rise, its ports remain common ground. Anyone with gold can buy passage, store goods, or hire local specialists. The Steamwheedle Cartel builds its influence around ports and frontier towns. In Stranglethorn Vale, Baron Revilgaz rules Booty Bay, a harbor fortified in the cliffs beside the Blackwater Raiders. Across the Great Sea, Chief Engineer Gazlowe directs Ratchet on the Barrens coast.\n\nA deep-water ferry runs straight from Ratchet to Booty Bay. Gadgetzan lies deep in Tanaris, while Steamwheedle Port and outposts in Desolace, Azshara, and Stonetalon Mountains secure resources across the region. Cutthroats threaten Steamwheedle trade at sea and on land. The Bloodsail Buccaneers menace Booty Bay's sea lanes, while Southsea Freebooters raid the coast near Ratchet. On land, the cartel fights its corporate rival, the Venture Company, over timber rights, oil fields, and shredder technology in Stonetalon and the Barrens. To keep order and enforce trade contracts, cartel barons hire passing mercenaries to settle disputes with extreme force.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_steamwheedle-cartel-dc49c372da1e5968ddaf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 114.102857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stonefield-family"] = {
  hash = "f2d9ea",
  fullHash = "f2d9ea",
  text = "Stonefield family. At Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn, the Stonefield family works its land while the Maclure feud endures. Young Tommy Joe is caught across the family divide. South of the road to Westfall, the Stonefields work their farm in western Elwynn. Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, and young Tommy Joe Stonefield make their home there. They tend crops and livestock, while pests and kobolds from nearby mines and borders trouble the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stonefield-family-f3e830aebb36e5b8a93b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 29.5967347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stormwind-army"] = {
  hash = "3eb961",
  fullHash = "3eb961",
  text = "Stormwind Army. Across the southern kingdom, the Stormwind Army struggles to guard its lands and the Grand Alliance. Its knights and marshals are famed for resilience, yet the king is missing, nobles quarrel, and too few soldiers hold the provinces. For centuries, Stormwind's army has fought gnolls on the frontier and Gurubashi trolls in desperate battles. In the First War, King Llane Wrynn I and Sir Anduin Lothar led the Knights of Stormwind in famed ambushes against the Orcish Horde. Yet fire took the capital. Survivors fled to Lordaeron, helped form the Alliance of Lordaeron, and fought through the Second War. After the Dark Portal fell, the restored kingdom raised its military anew. Stormwind Keep holds the army's command, and regional garrisons answer to the Stormwind Marshals. Within the capital, the Stormwind City Guard keeps civil order. The elite Royal Guard protects the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Beyond the gates, outposts patrol Elwynn Forest, watch the trade roads, and man ramparts as far as the Redridge Mountains. On the water, the Stormwind Fleet supports the army.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stormwind-army-5197325feff580a683d1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.8857143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:stormwind-guard"] = {
  hash = "1c1d08",
  fullHash = "1c1d08",
  text = "Stormwind Guard. Across Elwynn and Redridge, the Stormwind Guard struggles to hold its ground. Political gridlock and distant campaigns leave scattered marshals short of aid as gnolls, orcs, and bandits press the kingdom's borders. The Stormwind Guard patrols the roads and watchtowers that bind the kingdom together. Appointed marshals defend the outlying settlements: Marshal McBride in Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris in Lakeshire. They gather recruits and keep order. Between posts such as Westbrook Garrison, they ride brown horses prized for their stamina. Since the Third War, the Guard has kept Elwynn Forest safe and quiet. In recent years, though, crisis has come. King Varian Wrynn has vanished, and the kingdom's standing army is tied down abroad, leaving regional garrisons with little aid. At Westbrook Garrison, Deputy Rainer struggles to hold the western border against Riverpaw gnolls and Defias thieves. Local commanders now depend on visiting adventurers for crucial security work and bounties. The crown kept a tenuous military presence in the Redridge Mountains, even as Duskwood and Westfall were abandoned. The Guard's position there is desperate. Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs from the Burning Steppes overwhelmed the soldiers and drove them from Stonewatch Keep. In Lakeshire, blacksmith Verner Osgood uses iron to keep guards equipped. Exhausted sentries watch the road for the 2nd Battalion, which never arrives.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_stormwind-guard-32a0c9c458ce51455871/1-0000.mp3", duration = 93.7012245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:tauren"] = {
  hash = "1fa3c4",
  fullHash = "1fa3c4",
  text = "Tauren. Across central Kalimdor, the tauren are building a home in Mulgore while keeping their old ways. They call themselves shu'halo and honor the Earth Mother, shamanism, and the natural world. Centaur clans nearly destroyed them before they joined Thrall's New Horde. High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof now leads them from Mulgore and Thunder Bluff. For countless generations, the tauren roamed the arid Barrens with the seasons. They hunted the great kodo and listened for the Earth Mother's voice. Then the centaur tribes brought their peaceful wanderings to an end. Born from the tragic union of Zaetar and Princess Theradras, the fierce centaur waged a relentless war against them. Without a fortified homeland, and unwilling to fight without end, the tauren were driven near annihilation. An alliance from across the sea gave the tauren a chance to survive. During the Third War, Cairne Bloodhoof met Warchief Thrall and the orcs who had newly arrived. The leaders recognized a shared honor and longing for their shamanic heritage, and joined forces.\n\nWith the Horde's help, the tauren defeated the marauding centaur and claimed Mulgore's sheltered grasslands. Cairne founded Thunder Bluff on Mulgore's windswept mesas. In gratitude, the tauren marched with the Horde to Mount Hyjal and helped turn back the Burning Legion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_tauren-ae483aa61dd06aaf7c20/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.5469388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:terenas-menethil-ii"] = {
  hash = "ef71b9",
  fullHash = "ef71b9",
  text = "Terenas Menethil II. Across Lordaeron, Terenas Menethil II bears the burden of war and its aftermath. For more than five decades, he ruled as a wise and diplomatic king, forged the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War, and sheltered the young Prince Varian Wrynn. His corrupted son, Prince Arthas, murdered him, and Lordaeron began to fall to the Scourge. When Stormwind refugees reached the north with Sir Anduin Lothar, King Terenas Menethil II answered their call. He gathered the monarchs of the divided human kingdoms and envoys from Ironforge and Quel'Thalas. Together, they formed the Alliance of Lordaeron. Terenas gave command of its armies to Lothar, its supreme commander, rather than claiming it for himself. For the first time in millennia, humanity’s kingdoms stood united. Together, they defeated the Old Horde. After the Second War, Terenas insisted that surviving orcs be held in internment camps rather than executed. He believed peace might endure once their demonic fury had faded. Yet the camps placed a heavy tax burden on Lordaeron’s allies. Gilneas shut itself behind the Greymane Wall, Stromgarde left the Alliance over political disputes, and the high elves withdrew entirely. As the coalition fractured, Terenas bore its cost.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_terenas-menethil-ii-e21a2047921c928d76f4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.2146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:third-war"] = {
  hash = "47d901",
  fullHash = "47d901",
  text = "Third War. Across Lordaeron and Kalimdor, the Third War ended only after mortals stood against the Burning Legion. The war began with the Lich King's Plague of Undeath and brought down Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas before the defense of Mount Hyjal. The Third War began with plague in northern Lordaeron. Infected grain from Andorhal carried a sickness that killed its victims and raised them as ghouls bound to the Lich King's will. Prince Arthas Menethil and Jaina Proudmoore, a sorceress of the Kirin Tor, tried to stop it.\n\nDreadlords turned Arthas's grief and fear toward Stratholme. He purged its infected people, then pursued his foe to Northrend. There Arthas took Frostmourne, the cursed runeblade, and cast aside his soul. He returned south and murdered his father, King Terenas. Lordaeron then fell into the Scourge's hands. Arthas used the dead to crush Quel'Thalas and raise the necromancer Kel'Thuzad at the Sunwell. The Scourge then besieged Dalaran and seized the spellbooks needed to summon Archimonde, the demon lord, into Azeroth. With Archimonde on mortal soil, Dalaran was leveled by arcane devastation. The Scourge had served as the Legion's advance guard. Now the Burning Legion turned toward the eternal power across the Great Sea. The mysterious Prophet sent cryptic warnings to Warchief Thrall and Lady Jaina Proudmoore. Thrall led the New Horde across the Great Sea, while Jaina brought surviving Alliance refugees to safety. In ancient Kalimdor, the newcomers clashed with the reclusive night elves.\n\nThen the three factions recognized the threat before them. Atop Mount Hyjal, mortals joined forces beneath the World Tree Nordrassil. When Archimonde reached the tree, ancestral wisps destroyed him. The Legion's vanguard was broken, and the war ended. Archimonde's fall brought no lasting peace. Lordaeron's Alliance is gone. Stormwind and Ironforge now anchor the southern Alliance, while the old kingdom rots as the Plaguelands. In Kalimdor, Thrall's Horde has carved out Durotar, though tensions with the newly allied night elves remain volatile.\n\nThe Scourge still holds the northern ruins. The freed undead, called the Forsaken, hold the catacombs of Capital City. They fight to survive against their former masters and vengeful crusaders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_third-war-de59f113d3da50f89774/1-0000.mp3", duration = 150.151837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:titan"] = {
  hash = "3d2319",
  fullHash = "3d2319",
  text = "Titan. Across the Great Dark Beyond, the titans travel from world to world, seeking places to shape and order. Long ago, they came to Azeroth, defeated the Old Gods, raised great works, and prepared the world for life before departing among the stars. Across the Great Dark Beyond, the titans journey as cosmic giants of primordial matter and arcane magic. They carry the forces of order and creation, seeking sleeping worlds to cultivate. There they raise continents, calm the elements, and shape life. Stone, iron, and sea constructs guard their works and keep the balance they sought. When the Pantheon found Azeroth long ago, chaotic elemental lords and the horrific Old Gods held the world in their grip. The titans drove the elementals into pocket planes and sealed the Old Gods beneath the earth. They shaped great biomes and built vaults such as Uldaman. Then they entrusted five great dragons, the Dragon Aspects, with the land, magic, time, and life, and journeyed back among the stars. Sargeras once stood among the titans as their greatest warrior and defender. Yet the deep cosmic darkness and demonic malevolence he fought filled him with fear and despair. He rejected his kin's work and embraced nihilism. To extinguish all creation rather than let it fall to corruption, he founded the Burning Legion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_titan-6925e8e8cc77b30e6a7c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.7902041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:trogg"] = {
  hash = "3258ae",
  fullHash = "3258ae",
  text = "Trogg. Deep beneath the earth, Troggs have clawed their way into the world above. Dwarven excavations disturbed their ancient vaults, and now they overrun quarries and hold Gnomeregan after driving its gnomes out. The first stone servants were a flawed design. Long before mortal races kept records, titan-forged keepers tried to shape servants from stone. They made brutish creatures with stone skin, little sense, and savage tempers. The keepers refined their design into the earthen, ancestors of the dwarves. Ironaya, a giantess, spared the flawed troggs and sealed them deep within Uldaman, hoping they would sleep without end. Dwarven excavations woke the troggs after countless millennia. The Explorers' League of Ironforge broke into ancient underground complexes in Khaz Modan, and furious swarms poured out to hunt their distant dwarven kin. The bright surface world drove them to dig tunnels beneath Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and the Badlands. From below, they ambushed quarries, archaeological dig sites, and trade outposts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_trogg-0243c0d77e8a56ae22b5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.1795918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:troll"] = {
  hash = "facd8c",
  fullHash = "facd8c",
  text = "Troll. Across Azeroth, the troll empires lie divided, while the Darkspear now live within Thrall's Horde. Trolls are among the world's oldest peoples, born near the Well of Eternity before the Sundering. They heal swiftly and honor primal spirits called loa through voodoo. Trolls first arose near the Well of Eternity, long before the Great Sundering broke ancient Kalimdor. They lived by hunting and gathering, swift and agile, and their bodies healed grievous wounds with astonishing speed. Given time, they could regrow even severed limbs. Around the sacred mountains of Zandalar, they turned to primal spirits called loa. Shadow hunters and witch doctors guided these rites, which grew into voodoo. In ancient times, troll empires fought a long war against the insectoid aqir, whose swarms threatened to overrun the continent. The Amani and Gurubashi led the struggle and drove the aqir to the land's far reaches. Some fled north to what became Azjol-Nerub. Others went south to the deserts where Ahn'Qiraj would rise. With the aqir broken and contained, the trolls lost their common cause. Their great factions withdrew into separate empires, shaping distinct cultures, physical traits, and tribal orders for forests, jungles, and frozen wastes. Millennia of separation and rivalry followed, along with territorial wars against younger races.\n\nThese struggles wore down troll dominion. Most surviving troll kingdoms now meet other races with fierce hostility, clinging to the ruins of their former greatness. The Darkspear, jungle trolls driven into exile, chose another path and joined the Horde with orcs and tauren.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_troll-dee5ff6b6cfa12d7e79a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.329796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:twilight-s-hammer"] = {
  hash = "e08954",
  fullHash = "e08954",
  text = "Twilight's Hammer. Across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, Twilight's Hammer works in secret to bring Azeroth to its end. Its creed calls for the Old Gods to awaken and all things to end. On Draenor, Twilight's Hammer began as an orc clan of the Old Horde. Two-headed ogre-mage Cho'gall led the clan and served as one of Gul'dan's chief lieutenants in the Shadow Council. Under Cho'gall, its members embraced a grim creed that looked toward the end of all things. They came to see the Horde as a force of destruction for that purpose. Twilight's Hammer crossed the Dark Portal during the First War, and its apocalyptic zeal deepened on Azeroth. Its cultists heard the true whispers of the Old Gods, ancient horrors chained beneath the earth. Blackhand tolerated their erratic rituals because Cho'gall kept their bloodlust in check. He sent them with Kilrogg Deadeye's Bleeding Hollow clan in early assaults on Stormwind Keep.\n\nDuring the Second War, the clan worked deep inside Grim Batol, overseeing critical refineries until Gul'dan's betrayal fractured the Horde. Orgrim Doomhammer ordered the traitorous clan slaughtered near the Tomb of Sargeras. A core of zealots survived. On Kalimdor's forgotten shores, Twilight's Hammer grew from a broken orc clan into a secret, multiracial conspiracy. It draws disillusioned members from both the Alliance and the Horde, recruiting any soul broken enough to welcome oblivion. In northern Kalimdor, Twilight Lord Kelris leads cultists entrenched in the submerged halls of Blackfathom Deeps. There they feed sacrifices to Aku'mai, a monstrous servant of an Old God.\n\nFarther north in Darkshore, their excavators disturb the colossal fossilized remains at the Master's Glaive. In Silithus's southern deserts, robed fanatics tend abyssal windstones and listen for ancient horrors stirring beneath the sands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_twilight-s-hammer-1b9418aab916ec506c0c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 117.028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:venture-company"] = {
  hash = "80e617",
  fullHash = "80e617",
  text = "Venture Company. Across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, Venture Company crews strip-mine and clear-cut land, heedless of its people and nature. Mogul Razdunk directs the ruthless goblin cartel from afar. Its crews seek raw materials with heavy machinery, away from major goblin ports like Booty Bay and Ratchet. Venture Company crews tear into the land for raw materials. Mogul Razdunk directs the cartel from afar, and workers wield heavy machinery across Azeroth. Shredders harvest timber, drilling rigs bore into the earth, and refineries process what they find. The cartel takes resources by force, ignoring treaties, borders, and local rights.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_venture-company-4ed88557e62bc0e05cec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.8408163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:westfall-brigade"] = {
  hash = "a828f2",
  fullHash = "a828f2",
  text = "People's Militia. In Westfall, the People's Militia holds its ground against the Defias Brotherhood. Gryan Stoutmantle raised the volunteers at Sentinel Hill after Stormwind withdrew its troops. With local farmers and visiting adventurers, they defend the region from banditry. Westfall once fed the Kingdom of Stormwind. Then the kingdom withdrew its troops, leaving the people defenseless. The Defias Brotherhood spread across the plains, seizing family homesteads, taking over iron mines, and extorting local farmers. With no royal reinforcements in sight, veteran warrior Gryan Stoutmantle rallied citizens and retired soldiers to form the People's Militia. Sentinel Tower, atop Sentinel Hill, is the Militia's base. From there, volunteers patrol the region's main roads with little military gear or steady supplies. Gryan Stoutmantle directs them against the growing ranks of Defias highwaymen, thieves, and pillagers. When Redridge asks for mutual aid, the Militia must refuse. Sending even a handful of men could leave Westfall lost.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_westfall-brigade-173b5dbce9a0770df03a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.5077551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["topic:worgen"] = {
  hash = "783eea",
  fullHash = "783eea",
  text = "Worgen. Across Silverpine Forest, Ashenvale, and Duskwood, Worgen now hunt with bestial swiftness and a fury they cannot master. Once feared as nightmares from another realm, their beginnings remain uncertain. Long before Worgen appeared in Lordaeron and Kalimdor, people told folktales and cautionary tales of wolf-men. Their beginnings remain a mystery. Scholars who have studied them believe they came to Azeroth from another realm. Archmage Arugal and the night elves' Scythe of Elune have both been shown to summon them. During the Third War, the Scourge overran Lordaeron, and slain Kirin Tor wizards rose again as undead. Archmage Arugal sought a weapon to strengthen Dalaran's dwindling ranks. He used forbidden research by the Dalaran wizard Ur to pierce the veil and summon Worgen into Silverpine Forest. They savaged the undead invaders, but Arugal could not master their fury. The beasts turned on the wizards and spread their curse with fang and claw.\n\nMad with guilt and fascination, Arugal claimed the monsters as his children. He seized Baron Silverlaine's ancestral seat and renamed it Shadowfang Keep. There, he cursed nearby villagers into monstrous nocturnal servitude.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_topic_worgen-cd85e9229a86382e28c2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.8783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:alterac"] = {
  hash = "b79aea",
  fullHash = "20b624",
  text = "Alterac Mountains. Across the Alterac Mountains, ruined peaks shelter ogres, outlaws, and the remnants of arcane power. Once home to the human kingdom of Alterac and Dalaran’s scholarly magocracy, these highlands draw travelers from nearby foothill settlements and the rebuilding city. During the Second War, Alterac stood among humanity’s Seven Kingdoms. King Aiden Perenolde betrayed the Alliance of Lordaeron and joined the Old Horde, hoping to save his realm. His treachery was uncovered and crushed, and the kingdom broke into disputed borderlands. After Alterac dissolved, the Third War brought fresh horrors. Its grand stone capital now lies in ruins.\n\nThe Crushridge ogres occupy the Ruins of Alterac under Mug'thol’s leadership. Alterac’s surviving human nobles formed the Syndicate, a vicious band of criminals. They fight by force to reclaim their lost estates. On the mountains’ western edge, Dalaran’s surviving magi rebuild beside lordamere lake. The demon lord Archimonde devastated the great city-state during the Third War. Its magi raised a massive, opaque violet dome over the ruins, but the shield has since been removed. The kirin tor now rebuild their sanctum in the open. Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver can be found inside the Violet Citadel. Southshore and Tarren Mill are staging grounds for journeys into the peaks. Yeti, mountain lions, and Syndicate footpads roam here. Beneath the snow line, a greater conflict churns.\n\nDeep in the mountain passes, the dwarven Stormpike Guard and the reclusive Frostwolf clan of orcs contest Alterac Valley. They fight over ancient territorial and mining rights.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_alterac-7b77b742fc1c161e4435/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.486531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:arathi"] = {
  hash = "064ca3",
  fullHash = "706e85",
  text = "Arathi Highlands. In Arathi, ruined Stromgarde leaves the highlands at war on several fronts. The kingdom's remnants, the Horde, Syndicate nobles, and invading ogres fight for the ancient birthplace of the Empire of Arathor in southeastern Lordaeron. The dry plains of Arathi once formed the heart of a united human realm. Millennia ago, King Thoradin gathered the divided tribes beneath the Arathi banner and built their capital, Strom, on ground that was easy to defend. During the Troll Wars, humanity made its compact with the high elves here and learned the arcane arts to drive back the Amani.\n\nNoble bloodlines later spread north into Lordaeron and south toward Stormwind. The imperial guard remained and formed Stromgarde, a martial kingdom that guarded the border against forest trolls for ages. Under King Thoras Trollbane, Stromgarde helped defend the Alliance during the Second War. The Third War and its aftermath broke the kingdom. It escaped the worst of the Scourge plague, but King Thoras's mysterious death plunged the realm into turmoil. Boulderfist ogres came down from the hills, while nobles of Alterac in the Syndicate seized wide districts of Stromgarde Keep.\n\nPrince Galen Trollbane and his loyalists now hold a small part of the city and their refuge at Refuge Pointe. Against heavy odds, they fight a bitter guerrilla war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_arathi-ef512277bf3d991cc05c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.9428571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ashenvale"] = {
  hash = "30d864",
  fullHash = "f18edd",
  text = "Ashenvale. Ashenvale's sacred woods have become a battleground as the Horde cuts timber for Orgrimmar. Between the Barrens and Darkshore, the ancient forest remains under the care of the Sentinels and Cenarius. Its canopy shelters moonwells and old ruins, while demonic blights, hostile tribes, and a contested frontier threaten the woods. Beneath Mount Hyjal, Ashenvale has belonged to the kaldorei and the children of Cenarius for millennia. Immense trees knit a close canopy overhead. Daily rain gathers as mist beneath them, and twilight settles on the forest floor.\n\nMoss covers the ruins of elven settlements, where walking ancients and dryads keep watch. For thousands of years, the night elves built no true cities here. They hunted lightly and kept the wild domain in balance. The Third War shattered Ashenvale's peace. Grommash Hellscream and the Warsong clan entered the forest for lumber, and their demonic bloodlust led them to slay Cenarius. Now the struggle is a full territorial war.\n\nFrom Splintertree Post and the southern Mor'shan Rampart, the Warsong Outriders send axes and shredders into the eastern woods to supply Orgrimmar. The Silverwing Sentinels hold Astranaar, a sanctuary town. They have sworn to drive every orcish logger from the sacred grove. The lumber war is only one danger in Ashenvale. The Burning Legion left blighted scars at Felfire Hill and Demon Fall Canyon, where Mannoroth died. Satyrs make their bases in ruins such as Satyrnaar and Xavian, corrupting waterways and wildlife.\n\nFurbolg tribes, once friends of the elves, have largely fallen to dark madness. Mysterious worgen stalk the northern shrine near Felwood. Cursed dragonkin prowl Bough Shadow and guard a portal into the troubled Emerald Dream.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ashenvale-180db5a18914680ceda2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 115.800816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:azshara"] = {
  hash = "d0a0f1",
  fullHash = "f314e6",
  text = "Azshara. Along northeastern Kalimdor, Azshara’s broken coast runs from autumn woods to drowned ruins and perilous waters. Horde and Alliance outposts stand near its borders, while naga, ghosts, and other dangers haunt the land. Zin-Azshari’s coast once lay at the heart of kaldorei civilization. Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne court drew the Burning Legion into Azeroth. So began the War of the Ancients. The Well of Eternity collapsed and the sea swallowed most of the magnificent city. Fractured cliffs, perilous coral reefs, and waterlogged spires remain. Survivors named the ruined land for their fallen monarch, whose pride and betrayal changed it forever. Crimson, orange, and gold leaves cover Azshara’s woods through an autumn that never ends. Yet the quiet canopy hides a harsh wilderness. Satyrs perform dark rituals in forest hollows, Highborne apparitions haunt drowned foundations, and Spitelash naga prowl the Coral Sea’s shallow reefs. Ferocious beasts, giant hydras, and mountain giants roam the highlands and ivory beaches. Azshara’s isolation and harsh terrain draw those who pursue forgotten crafts or watch dangerous powers. Archmage Xylem lives in a secluded tower high above the cliffs. There, he tests promising spellcasters and cunning rogues. Farther along the coast, the demon hunter Loramus Thalipedes watches the waves from a barren isle. At a temple above the churning waters, the immense sea giant Lord Arkkoroc demands blood sacrifices.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_azshara-a4526d7ba8d0619b7f70/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.623673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:badlands"] = {
  hash = "9c579d",
  fullHash = "2ca59e",
  text = "Badlands. Dust storms sweep across the Badlands, where rival forces hold ground in dwarven Khaz Modan. Dark Iron dwarves, troggs, Dustbelcher ogres, and black dragonkin crowd this frontier between Loch Modan and Searing Gorge. Uldaman's titan ruins and the Horde stronghold of Kargath make it a crossroads. South of Loch Modan's green hills, you enter a hot basin of barren flats and rugged ravines. Coyotes, cougars, and vultures roam the land, and fierce sandstorms can hide the horizon. Dustbelcher ogres keep crude camps across the plateaus. At Angor Fortress, the Dark Iron clan fortifies its hold and clashes with anyone who trespasses. Among the northern bluffs lies Uldaman, an underground complex carved by the titan-forged earthen. Ironforge prospectors recently uncovered it, revealing monumental secrets about the ancient ancestry of the dwarven race. Their digging awoke hordes of vicious Stonevault troggs and animated earthen guardians. The creatures slaughtered the archaeological teams and turned the digsites outside into bloody skirmish grounds. The Horde holds one of its most precarious footholds in the southern Eastern Kingdoms here. Kargath, in the northwest, gives Warchief Thrall's forces a base to watch the rogue Dark Horde in nearby Blackrock Mountain. Far to the east, Lethlor Ravine cuts a treacherous chasm through the land. Beneath the mountains' shadow, black whelps and dangerous black dragonkin guard their brooding grounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_badlands-113eb94e3f32d5406edf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.1118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:barrens"] = {
  hash = "883834",
  fullHash = "c4a71c",
  text = "The Barrens. Across The Barrens, Horde outposts guard the roads as rival forces press in from every side. Once forest before the Great Sundering, this central Kalimdor plain is now dry country of red dust, scrub, and wandering wildlife. The Crossroads anchors Horde traffic, but quilboar, centaur, and creeping corruption threaten its hold. For generations, tauren nomads hunted kodo and zhevra across these plains. They also fought a recurring defensive war against the Kolkar centaur. During the Third War, Thrall led the orcs to Kalimdor. He helped Cairne Bloodhoof drive back the centaur, then crossed the savanna into Mulgore with him. The Barrens now links Durotar, Mulgore, and Ashenvale.\n\nThe Crossroads and Camp Taurajo guard trade along the Gold Road. Orcish homesteads endure the harsh climate as the Horde builds a lasting home. Horde control of the savanna remains contested. Razormane quilboar raid farms and supply lines from thorn-choked strongholds, their bristles like porcupine quills. To the south, the dens of Razorfen Kraul loom over the land. On the eastern coast, Theramore forces hold Northwatch Hold, while dwarven prospectors dig into the titan complex of Bael Modan. Ratchet's goblins keep uneasy neutrality in trade and welcome travelers who bring coin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_barrens-93f7e1915f1727e1757a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.677551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:blackfathom"] = {
  hash = "241f6a",
  fullHash = "4f750a",
  text = "Blackfathom Deeps. In the flooded halls of Blackfathom Deeps, Twilight's Hammer cultists gather along Ashenvale's northwestern shore. The temple once honored Elune, the night elven moon goddess. Naga and satyrs now venerate Aku'mai, a primordial hydra and servant of an Old God. Blackfathom Deeps lies sunken beneath the coastal waters of the Veiled Sea. Before the Great Sundering, Highborne masons raised gem-encrusted bridges through its halls, and Queen Azshara guarded the waters around its sacred moonwell. She kept salt seas and mountain streams apart. When the Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, the temple sank. It lay undisturbed for millennia, until foul whispers rose from the darkness below. Twilight's Hammer cultists came to the sunken ruins, drawn by mad dreams and dark portents. Inside, they forged an unholy truce with coastal naga led by Lady Sarevess and prowling satyrs. Together, they desecrated the moonwell and enslaved nearby creatures.\n\nYears of torment drove the giant sea turtle Ghamoo-ra mad. The brutal murloc Gelihast raised a blood shrine, and the caverns filled with fresh sacrifices. Under Twilight Lord Kelris, the cultists made the ruins a staging ground for the Old Gods' impending return.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_blackfathom-77e3432b9c2d341ddf35/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.48, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:blastedlands"] = {
  hash = "7fb091",
  fullHash = "d4b471",
  text = "Blasted Lands. In the Blasted Lands, Nethergarde Keep watches a wasteland scarred by the Dark Portal. Red clay and barren rock cover the former Black Morass, while demons and Dreadmaul ogres still threaten the region. Once, the Blasted Lands belonged to the Black Morass, a stretch of dense fenland. During the First War, the warlock Gul'dan and the possessed Medivh tore open the Dark Portal and unleashed the orcish Horde. Fel energies poured through the breach. They drained the land of moisture and baked it into cracked red clay.\n\nThe Portal was destroyed at the end of the Second War. Its shockwaves scorched the ground again and turned the native stone crimson. Nethergarde Keep watches the only road into the valley from the northern bluffs. Archmage Khadgar and the Kirin Tor commissioned the stone fortress after the Second War. Its mages and soldiers are sworn to stop renewed demonic or orcish incursions.\n\nThe posting is strict. Garrison members who break its rules are banished to the Stockade in Stormwind. Dreadmaul ogres remained after Draenor's fall and keep the defenders under pressure. They raid the roads and attack the gates under fell guidance. The Tainted Scar cuts through the southwestern Blasted Lands, a cratered canyon filled with demons. After the Legion's defeat at Mount Hyjal, doomguard commander Lord Kazzak gathered wandering demons, doomguard, and felhounds under his banner. His dreadlord lieutenant, Razelikh the Defiler, directs the Shadowsworn cultists at the Altar of Storms and commands the local Dreadmaul ogres. Scholars and wanderers still brave the Blasted Lands in search of discoveries. Just inside the northern pass, Bloodmage Drazial and Bloodmage Lynnore experiment with organs from scorpids, basilisks, and boars. They use them to brew potent physical enhancers.\n\nFarther into the wastes, the draenei hermit Kum'isha seeks scattered draenethyst fragments, remnants of Draenor's shattered past. Travelers who recover them receive a reward from him.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_blastedlands-8e5079f8c16f608271b4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 130.847347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:burningsteppes"] = {
  hash = "a115b0",
  fullHash = "05d370",
  text = "Burning Steppes. At Blackrock Mountain’s southern base, fire and lava have scorched the Burning Steppes. The land holds the main overland passage between Stormwind and Khaz Modan, where hostile clans and dragons threaten travelers. The Burning Steppes were once the northern reaches of the Redridge Mountains. Centuries ago, during the War of the Three Hammers, the exiled Dark Iron clan built Thaurissan as its capital here. Facing defeat by the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan tried to summon elemental forces.\n\nHis spell shattered the surrounding mountains and brought Ragnaros the Firelord into Azeroth. Rivers of lava opened, and Blackrock Mountain rose from the earth. The blast left the steppes charred and barren. During the Second War, Blackrock Spire became the Old Horde’s foremost seat of power. Near the war’s end, the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron marched into the Burning Steppes and laid siege to the mountain. At its foot, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer struck down Lord Anduin Lothar.\n\nLothar’s death spurred the Allied armies to rout the orcs and win the war. Ash and rusted steel still mark the valley where the battle was fought. Blackrock orcs remain in the steppes, keeping the Old Horde’s cruelty despite Thrall’s New Horde in distant Kalimdor. From Blackrock Stronghold, they ally with the black dragonflight and Firegut ogres. They honor no truce.\n\nDark Iron dwarves and elemental servitors haunt the fiery ruins. Black drakes and wyrmkin prey on travelers crossing between Stormwind and the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_burningsteppes-a1d6557c6bd3c3801c49/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.597551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:darkshore"] = {
  hash = "3b8af3",
  fullHash = "7a1e6a",
  text = "Darkshore. Auberdine links Darkshore to night elf lands and the wider Alliance. Travelers reach this cold, misty coast in northwestern Kalimdor from Teldrassil or the Eastern Kingdoms. Ancient ruins and troubled wildlife mark the land, while cultists, naga, and a creeping rot threaten it. Darkshore lies beneath the western shadows of Mount Hyjal and Felwood. Pine groves give way to fog and cold, rocky shores. Millennia ago, the Kaldorei cities of Ameth'Aran and Bashal'Aran flourished here. The Great Sundering shattered them, leaving pillars and arches half-drowned or tangled in roots. Highborne spirits haunt the ruins, while scavenging murlocs and invading naga roam among them. After the Third War and the planting of Teldrassil, the night elves made Auberdine a busy haven on this wild coast. Ships arrive from Rut'theran Village, Stormwind Harbor, and Menethil Harbor in the distant Wetlands by way of Southshore. Auberdine links night elf lands to the rest of the Alliance. Sentinels patrol the trade road south to Ashenvale, but their small garrison cannot watch the deep wilds beyond it. A slow, malignant rot has reached Darkshore beneath the eastern mountains. Corrupted runoff from Felwood flows to sources such as Cliffspring Falls, poisoning the water and driving bears, stags, and the furbolgs of Blackwood Den into violent madness.\n\nThe Dark Strand cultists perform foul rites at the Tower of Althalaxx. Twilight's Hammer gathers around the ancient and terrifying monument called the Master's Glaive.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_darkshore-9f2001043a08d74d08d4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.6832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:darnassus"] = {
  hash = "64ec56",
  fullHash = "8964d9",
  text = "Darnassus. High among the branches of Teldrassil lies Darnassus, the city of the night elves. Tyrande Whisperwind and the druids guide it. Its people are ancient, but they are mortal now. They must learn to live without their undying life, and they seek new ties with the Alliance. Darnassus spreads across the upper canopy of the World Tree, off the coast of northern Kalimdor. Its wooden halls and winding paths circle a quiet lake. Bridges join the terraces of tradesmen and craftsmen, of Sentinels and druids. The wild is never far from the city's daily life. Beside the long history of the kaldorei, Darnassus is young. In the Third War, Nordrassil was sacrificed, and the night elves came to know age and sickness. So Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and his followers planted Teldrassil, hoping to restore their lost vitality. The city grew up among its boughs, a refuge and a monument to their survival. Yet already a malaise stirs among the roots. Two powers hold sway in Darnassus. From the Temple of the Moon, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind commands the Sentinels and guides her people's faith. Across the waterways, Fandral leads the druids, for Malfurion Stormrage is lost in the Emerald Dream. To their new Alliance partners, the two leaders show a united face. But between priestesses and druids, the friction lingers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_darnassus-e5c1db936d3ba77e1f71/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.3722449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:deadmines"] = {
  hash = "9ef30a",
  fullHash = "5a8666",
  text = "The Deadmines. Beneath Moonbrook, the Deadmines hide the Defias Brotherhood's staging ground. Once a source of the kingdom’s gold, the tunnels were abandoned in the First War and long feared as haunted. Edwin VanCleef and goblin engineers now work there beyond the realm’s eyes, building an engine of vengeance against Stormwind. Before the First War, the Deadmines were called the greatest gold-producing center in human lands. Their gold supplied a third of Stormwind’s reserves. When the Orcish Horde swept through the kingdom and razed the capital, the mines were hastily abandoned to darkness.\n\nSir Anduin Lothar spent twenty months imprisoned by subterranean ogres while recovering the sacred Tome of Divinity. Warchief Blackhand’s rebellious daughter Griselda fled into the lower passages with the ogre Turok, and both met their ends there. Believed cursed and home only to shambling undead and lurking subterranean vermin, the depths lay untouched for decades. After the Stonemasons’ Guild was cast out of Stormwind without payment for rebuilding the city, Edwin VanCleef gathered the disgruntled artisans into the Defias Brotherhood. Seeking a secure headquarters beyond the reach of Gryan Stoutmantle’s People’s Militia, the Brotherhood chose its central redoubt in the abandoned tunnels.\n\nThey drove out the legitimate Miners’ League, leaving Foreman Thistlenettle’s doomed workers to perish and rise as undead ghouls. Lumbering ogres, seasoned brigands, and mechanical constructs guard the shafts, now an industrial war camp. Picks, fires, and illicit industry echo through the lower mines once more.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_deadmines-400cc85cb853fc6705c3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.5265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:deadwind"] = {
  hash = "7990db",
  fullHash = "7a4708",
  text = "Deadwind Pass. A foul wind sweeps Deadwind Pass, where the road runs between Duskwood and the Swamp of Sorrows. Karazhan rises at the heart of the pass, its ruined tower still tainted by Medivh's death. Ogres and ghosts haunt the canyon, and no town offers shelter. Deadwind Pass lies between Duskwood and the Swamp of Sorrows. Its ravines cut through pale stone and petrified wood. Once counted as part of the Redridge Mountains, the pass is now a hollow scar. A foul wind tears through the gorge, and sudden lightning under a gray sky makes metal arms dangerous to carry.\n\nTotemic effigies hang from dead branches beside the main road, warning travelers to keep moving. No fertile soil or mineable veins offer reason to stay. No town or flight master can give aid. Karazhan dominates Deadwind Pass, built above a powerful meeting of ley lines. The tower was Guardian Medivh's stronghold, where he plotted to open the Dark Portal and bring ruin to Stormwind. During the First War, Lord Anduin Lothar and the young Khadgar led an expedition into the tower after Medivh's madness and possession came to light. They killed him there.\n\nHis death released fel taint across the pass and Duskwood. Karazhan remains an ominous ruin, and its unstable magic still leaks into the land. South of the main road, Deadwind ogres hold the territory around the Vice. Clad in foul rags and armed with jagged iron, they ambush anyone who ventures near the southern road. Reports say they serve a hidden master nearby.\n\nFarther south, Morgan's Plot holds the skeletal remains of a hamlet that once served the tower. Ruins above ground and twisting cellars echo with wailing phantoms and restless shades, the dead left by Karazhan's unleashed sorcery.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_deadwind-1b66905324960d49a88f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 113.893878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:desolace"] = {
  hash = "0855ef",
  fullHash = "140cea",
  text = "Desolace. Across Desolace, centaur tribes fight for the plains as Horde, Alliance, and demonic cults press into the wastes. Gray earth, storm-dark skies, ancient ruins, and fallen beasts mark Kalimdor’s western coast. Storm-dark skies hang over Desolace, where gray earth lies broken and pools have turned yellow. Legend says Princess Theradras, an earth elemental, drained the land’s life to restore her form. Zaetar, son of Cenarius, came to subdue her, but became her mate instead. Their union brought the first centaur. Ashamed and enraged, the newborn centaur killed their father and spread across the plains, leaving the scarred waste travelers see today. Thousands of hooves thunder across Desolace as four centaur tribes fight outsiders and one another. The Kolkar roam the eastern cliffs, while the Magram keep crude yurts around brackish sinkholes. Farther south, the Gelkis live beyond rope bridges in deep ravines. The fanatical Maraudine guard the approach to Maraudon. Travelers crossing the wastes are often drawn into this old blood feud. Safe passage may mean fighting one tribe to win a wary parley with another. Beyond centaur lands, dangers gather along Desolace’s borders. The Burning Blade operates from Thunder Axe Fortress and Shadowbreak Ravine, summoning demons and satyrs whose fel fire threatens the region.\n\nNaga creep onto the storm-lashed beaches of Ethel Rethor, while Darkspear trolls spear fish from the stilts of Shadowprey Village. High in the northern crags, the Alliance keeps watch from Nijel's Point. They fear Horde expansion and the demonic taint spreading from Mannoroc Coven.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_desolace-d0c88ad0687541c641a8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 108.251429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:dunmorogh"] = {
  hash = "ee26fa",
  fullHash = "80aba6",
  text = "Dun Morogh. Dun Morogh is high snow country, the mountain home of the dwarves. The gnomes shelter here too, for they have lost their own home. But the snows are not safe. Troggs and Frostmane trolls prowl them, and so do the leper gnomes. The mountains rise between Searing Gorge in the south and the Wetlands in the north. Their snowmelt feeds the rivers of Khaz Modan, and copper and iron run in veins through the rock. Dwarven Mountaineers watch the narrow roads at the north and south gates. And beneath the tallest mountain lies Ironforge, the seat of King Magni Bronzebeard. Centuries ago, the dwarves drove the native Frostmane ice trolls up into the crags. So they made room for themselves, beside their gnome allies. The two peoples thrived in neighboring mountain halls, until the Third War. Then Gnomeregan fell.\n\nTroggs invaded the gnomes' city, and radioactive gas ruined it. Its own defenders were twisted into leper gnomes. High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque led the survivors into exile, and now they live as guests in Ironforge. But the leper gnomes and the troggs spill out into Chill Breeze Valley. The Frostmane have grown bolder, with armed camps from Shimmer Ridge to Coldridge Valley. Troggs have clawed up from old fissures into Gol'Bolar Quarry, and the mines are in danger. Wendigos and wild boars roam the drifts, and the Dark Iron dwarves send in their spies. Toxic fallout spreads from the ruins of Gnomeregan. The Mountaineers struggle to keep order, from Kharanos to the border gates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_dunmorogh-79c759880104f656c8c0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 97.0187755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:durotar"] = {
  hash = "660cb8",
  fullHash = "76951e",
  text = "Durotar. Durotar is red canyon country, where young orcs and Darkspear trolls prove themselves to the New Horde. Warchief Thrall founded this dry homeland after the Third War, and named it for his father, Durotan. The Southfury River and the Great Sea hem it in. Razor Hill and the warrior city of Orgrimmar rise from its rock. But quilboar tribes still hold out here, and so do stranded Alliance troops. Thrall chose this hard frontier on purpose. Here the orcs would earn an honest living by labor and grit, and not by conquest. The cracked red soil and the towering spires remind them of Draenor, before its ruin. Water is scarce and little of the land can be farmed, yet the Horde has embraced its stark grandeur. Durotar is proof that the orcs have forged their own destiny. The Horde holds Durotar, but keeping it is still a war. The Razormane quilboar, pushed out when the nation was founded, raid its farms from the western crags. In the south, what is left of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet still holds Tiragarde Keep, and refuses to surrender. Worse still, the demon cult of the Burning Blade lurks in hidden ravines and deep sea caves. It tempts young orcs back to fel power. On the southern coast lies Sen'jin Village, where the exiled Darkspear trolls gather under Master Gadrin. Their home was on the nearby Echo Isles. But the witch doctor Zalazane went mad, and with dark voodoo he enslaved his own kin. The Darkspear cannot take the islands back alone. They are counting on new warriors from the Valley of Trials, to break Zalazane's hold.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_durotar-f11a450521da8f6b8a75/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.545306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:duskwood"] = {
  hash = "246803",
  fullHash = "30e952",
  text = "Duskwood. In Duskwood, unending gloom hides the forest's sunlit past beneath a curse. Stormwind has abandoned its human inhabitants, who cling to survival in Darkshire under the Night Watch's protection. Ghouls, giant venomous spiders, raiding ogres, and savage, mysterious worgen roam the groves and haunted barrows. Duskwood once fed the Kingdom of Stormwind with rich farmland and prosperous settlements such as Grand Hamlet and Raven Hill. Its ruin began when Guardian Medivh fell at Karazhan during the First War. Waves of corrupted fel magic twisted the woodland into a gnarled, shadowy morass.\n\nSpiders became terrible predators, and fleeing wolves spilled north into Elwynn Forest. Grand Hamlet became Darkshire, where townsfolk were left largely to fend for themselves after the royal garrison withdrew to defend the capital. Commander Althea Ebonlocke and her volunteer militia, the Night Watch, keep Darkshire alive. Lord Ello Ebonlocke tries to govern the crumbling settlement. Beyond the town's lantern-lit perimeter, danger fills the land. The restless dead raised by necromancer Morbent Fel stir in massive Raven Hill Cemetery. Vul'Gol ogres came from Deadwind Pass to claim the south, while Dark Riders stalk the crossroads in relentless search of the lost Scythe. Near Duskwood's center lies a deep, ancient crater untouched by the surrounding corruption: the Twilight Grove. An ancient Great Tree there holds a dormant portal into the Emerald Dream. Soft, ethereal twilight bathes this unearthly sanctuary in the dying land. Sleeping druidic energies watch over it.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_duskwood-dbbfdbdcc2616718cfeb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.34449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:dustwallow"] = {
  hash = "da49f5",
  fullHash = "65e3f7",
  text = "Dustwallow Marsh. Across central Kalimdor, Dustwallow Marsh lies between the Barrens and the Great Sea. Underground springs feed its treacherous fens, where dangerous predators, covert faction skirmishes, and the black dragonflight all lurk. To the east, theramore isle is an Alliance fortress; to the northwest, Horde-allied Stonemaul ogres hold Brackenwall Village. Before the Great Sundering, Dustwallow was a high, grassy plain where herds roamed. The cataclysm shattered the valley and raised the water table, drowning the land in endless stagnant pools, mossy shallows, and choking mud.\n\nUnderground springs keep the marsh humid and submerged beneath thick fog. Crocolisks, raptors, and spiders haunt the fens. Crumbling causeways cross them, but jagged shallows and wandering bogs make every step beyond those roads perilous. Dustwallow stayed largely wild until after the Third War, when Lady Jaina Proudmoore led human refugees across the sea and founded the citadel of Theramore Isle on the eastern coast. Soon, Kul Tiran forces under Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore fought Thrall's fledgling Horde in bloody battles across the wetlands. The Admiral was defeated, and Theramore and Orgrimmar now keep an official armistice.\n\nTension still smolders. Theramore faces desertions and sabotage, while spies wage quiet, deadly conflicts across the fens.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_dustwallow-d655bce241cb32fcb342/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.9583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:easternplaguelands"] = {
  hash = "0cae87",
  fullHash = "1236aa",
  text = "Eastern Plaguelands. Scourge blight darkens the Eastern Plaguelands. Stratholme remains an undead stronghold, while the Scarlet Crusade fortifies the east. Light's Hope Chapel offers neutral refuge to those who fight back. Once called the Eastweald, the Eastern Plaguelands were Lordaeron's fertile heartland. Stratholme anchored the region, with prosperous towns like Darrowshire and Corin's Crossing nearby. During the Third War, Prince Arthas purged Stratholme after learning its grain had been laced with the Plague of Undeath.\n\nAfter Lordaeron fell, Scourge magic spread unchecked. The soil turned brown and rotten. Ordinary plants warped into colossal mushrooms, and orange mist settled over the valleys. Stratholme is the Scourge's southern seat of power in Lordaeron. Baron Rivendare commands it under the direction of the lich Kel'Thuzad. In the southeast, the fanatical Scarlet Crusade has fortified Tyr's Hand and assaults Stratholme's scarlet strongholds.\n\nThe Crusade's hatred reaches beyond the undead to any living traveler who crosses its borders. Farther north, the Mossflayer forest trolls of Zul'Mashar wage isolated, desperate campaigns amid the wider conflict. Watch your footing in the Eastern Plaguelands. The neutral Argent Dawn keeps Light's Hope Chapel in the far east, where Alliance and Horde champions can find shelter, supplies, and flight routes. Beyond the chapel, ruined watchtowers mark Lordaeron's lost defenses: Crown Guard, Eastwall, Northpass, and Plaguewood.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_easternplaguelands-0ad4973f74b4be964bbc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:elwynn"] = {
  hash = "9881e6",
  fullHash = "c188aa",
  text = "Elwynn Forest. Elwynn Forest is good green country, and Stormwind lives off its timber and gold, its fish and grain. It ought to be a peaceful place. But vermin, stray beasts and brigands roam the woods. So Northshire Abbey sends its recruits out to deal with them. The forest runs south from Stormwind's gates, all the way down to the Nazferiti River. It rains a good deal here, and the fields are the richer for it. Behind the fields grow thick hardwoods, and their timber goes to the capital. Carts roll up from Goldshire and the Eastvale Logging Camp, loaded with grain and livestock, lumber and raw copper. Elwynn breeds horses too, and knights and travelers all over the kingdom are glad to own a sturdy Evendale horse. But these are uneasy days. The Stormwind Army is busy far away, so the marshals and deputies must make do with recruits who have never been tested. Kobolds have overrun the Fargodeep and Jasperlode mines. Riverpaw gnolls prowl the southwestern borders, and murlocs have taken the shores of Stone Cairn Lake and Crystal Lake.\n\nEven the wolves are newcomers. They fled the corruption across the river in Duskwood, and came over into Elwynn.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_elwynn-9b7c3a33d6352eb1bc72/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.4636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:felwood"] = {
  hash = "95909a",
  fullHash = "7cb509",
  text = "Felwood. Felwood remains in the grip of corruption between Ashenvale and Mount Hyjal. Once a paradise tended by night elves, it was poisoned by demonic magic during the Third War. Satyrs, twisted beasts, and ancients roam its depths as the Cenarion Circle and Alliance and Horde outposts work to cleanse it. Before the Third War, clear streams ran beneath singing birds, and ancient protectors guarded the peaceful groves. Then the dreadlord Tichondrius and the Burning Legion brought that peace to an end. Before their assault on Mount Hyjal, they used the Skull of Gul'dan to poison northern Kalimdor. Its sorcery blackened the forest floor and turned it to mire.\n\nIllidan Stormrage later slew Tichondrius and consumed the artifact's essence. Yet the fel corruption remained. Treants and ancients rotted within, rivers boiled with bile, and the forest's beasts fell into cannibalistic madness. The work of healing Felwood is slow. On its southern border, the Cenarion Circle fights the blight from the Emerald Sanctuary. Its druids tend poisoned songflowers and night dragon bulbs, and give wanderers Cenarion beacons and cleansing salves.\n\nThe night elves hold Talonbranch Glade in the north, while the Horde keeps Bloodvenom Post. Both footholds stand amid a deadly wilderness where nature has turned against mortal flesh.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_felwood-d2d2ee00e7e239dc199e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.0546939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:feralas"] = {
  hash = "f6fa49",
  fullHash = "2250de",
  text = "Feralas. In Feralas, rainforest endures between Desolace, Thousand Needles, and the Veiled Sea. Mountain walls shelter an ancient wilderness of overgrown Kaldorei ruins and primordial beasts. Highborne remnants, Gordunni ogres, and rival Alliance and Horde outposts contend for its reaches. You enter a rainforest still green while Desolace and Thousand Needles lie dry nearby. Mountain ranges keep the wastes at bay, sheltering ancient growth beneath warm, misty air and towering waterfalls. Much of Kalimdor looked this way before the Great Sundering. Over the ages, jungle growth swallowed the monuments of the Kaldorei who once ruled here, hiding forgotten temples and pavilions beneath moss and ferns. At Feralas's heart, Eldre'Thalas lies in ruins, now called Dire Maul. The Kaldorei built the city at their empire's height, but the Gordunni ogres have defiled its vast stone courtyards. The clan spreads through the valley, pillaging sacred sites, fouling pristine groves, and driving wildlife to defend its territory. Whispers speak of Highborne remnants and powers trapped for ten thousand years in Dire Maul's sealed chambers. Alliance and Horde expeditions have pushed into the jungle, where every crossing brings danger. At Feathermoon Stronghold on Sardor Isle, General Shandris Feathermoon leads Sentinels against the ogres and advancing naga. Farther east, Camp Mojache anchors the Horde against aggressive Woodpaw gnolls, hostile Grimtotem tauren, and relentless ogres. Neither faction controls Feralas's wild interior.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_feralas-484dc093d850f755dcda/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.545306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:gnomeregan"] = {
  hash = "7af386",
  fullHash = "045cae",
  text = "Gnomeregan. In Dun Morogh, Gnomeregan lies ruined and quarantined after the gnomes' desperate defense against troggs. Once their underground capital and greatest technological wonder, it now holds irradiated halls, mutated troggs, deranged leper gnomes, and rogue mechanical sentries. Its surviving people live as refugees in nearby Ironforge. Beneath Dun Morogh, Gnomeregan gathered gnomish invention, competitive craft, and politics under one roof. With help from the nearby dwarves of Ironforge, its people built a vast mechanical complex, powered by grinding engines and whirring gears.\n\nIts intricate ventilation network took fifteen technicians working around the clock to keep fresh air moving. At Tinkers' Court, gnomes debated and competed as their capital grew into a marvel of industry. During the Second War, they sent the Alliance of Lordaeron siege engines, flying machines, and armaments. During the Third War, as the Scourge tore through Lordaeron, dwarven excavations at the ancient titan vault of Uldaman awakened primitive troggs beneath Dun Morogh. The creatures burrowed into Gnomeregan's lower foundations, and High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque led the defense.\n\nAs the city's defenses began to fail, Mekkatorque followed the counsel of his chief advisor, Mekgineer Thermaplugg. He ordered radioactive waste tanks vented into the lower levels to eradicate the invaders. The toxic gas spread beyond control. Nearly eighty percent of the gnomish population died within days.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_gnomeregan-4a679e7c7556bbffe384/1-0000.mp3", duration = 98.6383673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hallofthanes"] = {
  hash = "d9aa4b",
  fullHash = "270aff",
  text = "Hall of Thanes. Alliance defenders now seek a way into the Hall of Thanes, deep beneath Ironforge. Its old vaults hold dwarven relics, and danger has reached those depths. Hammers ring in Ironforge's Great Forge, but forgotten halls run deeper beneath the stone. The Hall of Thanes lies in those ancient reaches, tied to dwarven bloodlines and relics of stonecraft. Now Alliance champions must enter its shadowed vaults and guard the depths. The route into the Hall of Thanes is hidden from Ironforge's everyday travelers. Its corridors have been sealed or concealed. To find a way through, recover an Underground Map and use forgotten charts to navigate the deeper tunnels to the entrance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hallofthanes-6f7698ab9fa884e362da/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.8783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hillsbrad"] = {
  hash = "49a273",
  fullHash = "6d5da3",
  text = "Hillsbrad Foothills. In Hillsbrad Foothills, green fields still divide two forces at war. Southshore's humans and the Forsaken at Tarren Mill clash across the pastures, making the region a battleground for Alliance and Horde armies. Hillsbrad Foothills still holds green fields while blight consumes much of northern Lordaeron. Its pastures, pine groves, and working farms recall the kingdom before the Third War. Armed patrols, rogue mercenaries, and scavenger beasts cross the fields and roads. Once Lordaeron's breadbasket, Hillsbrad is now one of its most contested borderlands. Southshore stands on the southern coast, held by human refugees and soldiers loyal to the Alliance. North of it, Tarren Mill was once a quiet human farming hamlet. The Scourge destroyed it, and the Forsaken fortified it as their southernmost garrison. Between them, open ground has become a relentless killing ground, where Alliance and Horde champions skirmish for control of Hillsbrad. Hillsbrad's violence reaches back to the Second War. Its coast and hills saw the first great clashes between the Grand Alliance and the Old Horde. Later, Durnholde Keep held the internment camp where Thrall was raised before leading the orcish rebellion. Now the ruined keep belongs to the Syndicate, displaced human nobles turned bandits.\n\nCoastal murlocs, hostile gnolls, and the shadowed dead on Purgation Isle leave no corner of the hills secure.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hillsbrad-a6fff06f2e037c61cb51/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.0889796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:hinterlands"] = {
  hash = "ecf2d1",
  fullHash = "eb4a81",
  text = "The Hinterlands. The Hinterlands still bear the Amani's old claims, while Wildhammer dwarves hold Aerie Peak. Forest trolls keep their ruined temples, and the Revantusk have made a village on the eastern coast. The Amani forest trolls ruled the Hinterlands long before humans or dwarves came to northern Lordaeron. Their defeat in the ancient Troll Wars broke the empire into separate tribes. Centuries later, the War of the Three Hammers drove the Wildhammer dwarves from Grim Batol.\n\nThey migrated north and settled above ground at Aerie Peak. There, they tamed native gryphons and kept their shamanic traditions as they built a great stronghold in the heights. The forest trolls saw them as squatters on ancestral land. Skirmishes and raids have kept the feud alive ever since. Three forest troll tribes occupy the dense woods and ruined temples. The strongest are the brutal Vilebranch, who hold the terraced temple city of Jintha'Alor and the towering Altar of Zul. Farther south, the weaker Witherbark cling to Shadra'Alor and Hiri'Watha.\n\nThe nomadic Revantusk were long scorned by their cousins and had no true home. After the Third War, they made a treaty with Thrall's New Horde. With aid from orcs and Forsaken, they founded Revantusk Village on the eastern sea cliffs and claimed a coastal stronghold of their own.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_hinterlands-31a9581f0f0f7edd18b3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.6367347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ironforge"] = {
  hash = "48033d",
  fullHash = "e70a4a",
  text = "Ironforge. Ironforge is carved into the heart of a mountain, in the snows of Dun Morogh. It is the ancient capital of the Bronzebeard dwarves, and one of the Alliance's strongest bulwarks. At its center burns the Great Forge. Here King Magni Bronzebeard rules, and now his halls also shelter the gnomes who lost Gnomeregan. Twenty-five centuries ago, the first dwarves awoke in Uldaman. Westward they journeyed, into the jagged peaks of Dun Morogh, and delved into the tallest mountain. There they raised a shrine to their maker, the titan Khaz'goroth, and beside it the Great Forge. And ever since, the city has grown outward in rings around that fiery hearth.\n\nNo city above the ground is like it. Ironforge lies wholly beneath stone, warmed by roaring hearths and by rivers of molten rock. Its fortified gates and deep halls have held through centuries of war. For generations, three dwarven clans shared the mountain. The Bronzebeards lived beside the Wildhammers and the Dark Irons. When High King Modimus Anvilmar died, a bitter civil war broke out for rule of Ironforge. The Bronzebeards won it, and drove their rivals out.\n\nThen Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan led the Dark Irons back for vengeance. His siege reached the very heart of Ironforge, before the Bronzebeards threw it back. In the south, by mischance, Thaurissan then summoned Ragnaros the Firelord. The Firelord's coming shattered the Redridge Mountains, and doomed Thaurissan's own host. Ever since, the Bronzebeards have held Ironforge unchallenged. In the Second War, the Bleeding Hollow clan overran much of Khaz Modan. But Ironforge held firm behind its sealed gates, until the Alliance of Lordaeron broke the siege. For that debt of honor, King Magni Bronzebeard pledged Ironforge to the Alliance forever. He stood by it even when Gilneas and Quel'Thalas walked away. In the Third War, Magni mourned his brother Muradin, lost in Northrend. When Gnomeregan fell, the king opened his gates to thousands of gnome refugees. Under High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, they carved out Tinker Town within the stone city. And the Deeprun Tram now runs beneath the earth, straight to Stormwind. So Ironforge has become a crossroads of dwarven steel and gnomish invention.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ironforge-7bbf83151a7bb5a23707/1-0000.mp3", duration = 149.629388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:lochmodan"] = {
  hash = "338a02",
  fullHash = "2aebae",
  text = "Loch Modan. Loch Modan's great lake stands above the Wetlands, held back by the Stonewrought Dam. Around Thelsamar, troggs threaten the southern valleys, kobolds work the mines, and ogres hold the northeast highlands. Loch Modan's lake fills a green valley east of Dun Morogh. Pines and birches climb toward rugged peaks, and the Stonewrought Dam spans the northern rim. The dwarven town of Thelsamar draws mountaineers, hunters, and miners. Copper and tin run through the surrounding hills. Broken catapults and ballistae on the lake bed recall the Second War. The orcish Horde occupied Loch Modan then, until the dwarves drove them out. More recently, dwarven excavations opened underground chambers and released savage troggs. Their tribes, led by chieftains, overran the southern canyons of Stonesplinter Valley and the Valley of Kings. The Ironforge Mountaineers fight to defend the trade routes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_lochmodan-f5bd0eb3560243f64c70/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.3240816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:moonglade"] = {
  hash = "def14d",
  fullHash = "59b97c",
  text = "Moonglade. In Moonglade, druids of the Cenarion Circle gather beneath an endless twilight. The valley in northern Kalimdor shelters night elf and tauren druids, while travelers on foot brave Timbermaw Hold to reach it. Moonglade lies among the mountains north of Felwood and Winterspring. Beneath Elune's moon, twilight lingers over Lake Elune'ara, at the heart of a valley sacred since kaldorei civilization began. The Burning Legion left the glade untouched in both its ancient invasions and the recent Third War, held back by Cenarius and his offspring. Keeper Remulos watches over Moonglade now, guarding nature's balance in a refuge where factional conflict has no place. Malfurion Stormrage founded the Cenarion Circle millennia ago, and Moonglade remains its headquarters. In lakeside Nighthaven, druids learn from ancient traditions. After the Third War, the Circle welcomed tauren into its ranks, following Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem's teachings. Here, Alliance and Horde loyalties are set aside. Armed sentries keep the peace, and members who turn to violence face swift justice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_moonglade-741dc068e9e061b0309b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.2620408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:mulgore"] = {
  hash = "437a28",
  fullHash = "c99c0f",
  text = "Mulgore. Below the Stonetalon Mountains lies Mulgore, the green and sheltered homeland of the tauren. Grasslands and rolling plains spread between its tall plateaus, and high mountain walls ring it round. It is the heart of the united tribes, and they watch over it from Thunder Bluff. For generations, the tauren lived as nomads, hunted across the plains by centaur warbands. Their fortune turned when High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof met Thrall and his orcs. They shared ideals of honor, shamanism and strength, so the tauren joined the young Horde. With the orcs' help, Cairne broke the centaur's grip and led his people into Mulgore. Here they built lasting homes for the first time in centuries, and crowned the northern mesas with their capital. To come of age in Mulgore, young tauren must answer the call of the Great Hunt and honor the Earthmother. Their training begins at Camp Narache, under Chief Hawkwind. From there they go down to Bloodhoof Village, and take up the Rites of the Earthmother. They learn to speak with the ancestral spirits and to forage the native herbs. And they learn to respect the herds of kodos and tallstriders that sustain their people. For all its calm, Mulgore is threatened on every side. Displaced quilboar have dug in at Brambleblade Ravine, and they have defiled the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks. In the mountains, Windfury harpies prey on the wild game, and Palemane gnolls strike from hidden caves.\n\nAnd outsiders have come to profit from the land. The goblins of the Venture Company are fouling the sacred wells, and quarrying the eastern hills. Dwarven explorers from Bael Modan have opened the Bael'dun Digsite. There they dig for ancient titan relics, over the tauren's protests.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_mulgore-2da23053471833414342/1-0000.mp3", duration = 102.60898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:orgrimmar"] = {
  hash = "bbb1c4",
  fullHash = "71992d",
  text = "Orgrimmar. Out of the red canyons of northern Durotar rises Orgrimmar, stronghold of the orcs. It is the heart of the New Horde. Trolls, tauren and Forsaken allies have found shelter within its walls. The orcs came here looking for a home. After the Third War, Warchief Thrall led them to the dry coasts of Kalimdor. There they would be free at last, of demon taint and of human chains. He named the land Durotar, after his father. And he named the city for Orgrim Doomhammer, the mentor he had lost.\n\nThe stronghold was raised with blood and sweat. Tauren stood with the orcs, and Darkspear trolls, and goblin engineers. The Champion Rexxar stood with them too. Together they drove back the centaurs, the harpies and the fierce thunder lizards of the canyons. Rugged mountains ring the city. They keep out both foreign foes and the razor-winds of Durotar. Within, the rock opens into hollows, and each valley has its own part in the life of the Horde.\n\nIn the Valley of Strength stands the gatehouse. From it a road runs deeper in, to the market of the Drag. Tauren druids and shaman gather by the pools in the Valley of Wisdom. And in the Valley of Spirits, the shadow hunters and witch doctors of the Darkspear dwell among their pavilions on stilts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_orgrimmar-7ead5cb99668c9c23e0b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.5730612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ragefire"] = {
  hash = "7abebc",
  fullHash = "a6daa9",
  text = "Ragefire Chasm. Beneath Orgrimmar, troggs and a demonic cult threaten the young Horde. The Searing Blade plots in volcanic caverns below the Cleft of Shadow, with the capital above. Ragefire troggs burst from deep beneath Orgrimmar into winding volcanic corridors and lava pits. As orcish builders raise their new home above, the troggs grow more aggressive. Magatha Grimtotem sends Maur Grimtotem to speak for Thunder Bluff, but the troggs slaughter him. They yield only to force. The Searing Blade, a demonic cult, poses a greater danger than the troggs. Loyal to the Shadow Council, its fanatics plot the young Horde's ruin in caverns beside streams of magma. Jergosh the Invoker, a cunning warlock, leads with Bazzalan, a satyr, and Taragaman the Hungerer, a vicious felguard. Taragaman presides over an altar in the lava lake.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ragefire-c4fe3cc223a1db712eb8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.8236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:razorfenkraul"] = {
  hash = "a1cb57",
  fullHash = "a1cb57",
  text = "Razorfen Kraul. In the far south of the Barrens, quilboar defend the sacred brambles of Razorfen Kraul. They honor the thickets as the resting place of their ancestor, Agamaggan, while Charlga Razorflank’s rule threatens the region. Ten thousand years ago, Agamaggan fought the Burning Legion in the War of the Ancients. The colossal wild god fell, but his sacrifice preserved life on Azeroth. Legends tell that thorn-covered vines rose wherever his blood entered the earth, forming thick defenses across the southern plains. The quilboar honor Agamaggan as their divine ancestor and claim the thickets as holy ground. The greatest of them became the Razorfen.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_razorfenkraul-0305178efae0a0c5b634/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.2587755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:redridge"] = {
  hash = "1add3c",
  fullHash = "0d9c28",
  text = "Redridge Mountains. Redridge Mountains now face attacks from Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs of the Dark Horde. Rushing rivers run through wooded slopes around Lake Everstill, while Lakeshire holds the line with wandering mercenaries as Stormwind’s forces contend with strife at home. Redridge once stood among the kingdom’s most peaceful regions. Its forests supplied Stormwind with timber, while its waters and fields gave the kingdom fish and crops. Now Lakeshire is fortified against repeated attacks. Stonewatch Keep, once a proud stronghold of the kingdom, has fallen to renegade Blackrock orcs. Their hold cuts off roads and threatens the eastern valleys. These peaks form part of the spine that runs down the subcontinent of Azeroth. Centuries ago, the range stretched much farther north. Then the Dark Iron clan accidentally summoned the Firelord Ragnaros, and the earth split open to form the fiery crater of Blackrock Mountain. The cataclysm severed the northern peaks into the ash-choked Burning Steppes. Only the southern Redridge valleys remain lush and green, exposed to attacks from the slopes above. Magistrate Solomon and Marshal Marris plead for reinforcements, but Stormwind’s regency council leaves Lakeshire to fend for itself. Appeals to Sentinel Hill and Darkshire bring no aid; both settlements are crippled by crises of their own. With no reinforcements in sight, local magistrates hire any capable sellsword willing to brave the Lakeridge Highway, clear the gnoll camps, and push back the danger across Lake Everstill.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_redridge-bf3931a8c34e36aee287/1-0000.mp3", duration = 96.2089796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:riverglades"] = {
  hash = "202c0d",
  fullHash = "c6b144",
  text = "Riverglades. In Riverglades, human loyalists and Horde outposts still contest a valley east of the Badlands and Burning Steppes. Stormwind once claimed the region, but the Old Horde overran it in the First War. The Redridge Mountains lie southwest, the Swamp of Sorrows south, and Tanaris across the sea. Before the First War, Riverglades belonged to the Kingdom of Stormwind. Then the Old Horde swept through. Orcs sacked Sunnyglade but spared its mage tower to study human sorcery. They enslaved the townsfolk who survived. Stormwind soldiers tried to free the captives and rebuild, but the kingdom judged the valley lost and sent its strength to defend the core homelands. For years, the crown gave Riverglades little formal support. Stormwind's leaders left Riverglades behind, yet its human defenders never fully surrendered their homeland. Knight Commander Demar Brant leads the remaining loyalists of the legendary brotherhood of the horse. They hold farholde keep against the wilderness and hostile invaders. Nearby, the modern Horde keeps a strong presence at outposts such as rog'mar and bolder'ok. The old struggle over the glades continues. Riverglades follows the coast of the Forbidding Sea, among mountains and winding waterways. You can cross west through Blackrock Pass to the Burning Steppes, or travel southwest through Redridge Pass to the Redridge Mountains. South lies the Swamp of Sorrows. To the northwest, trails pass Twilight's Shroud into the Badlands. At powderfuse port, you can take a boat across the sea to steamwheedle port in Tanaris.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_riverglades-7126558423b98b3ef6c7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.276735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ruinsoflordaeron"] = {
  hash = "f03631",
  fullHash = "b51fa6",
  text = "Ruins of Lordaeron. Above Undercity, the Ruins of Lordaeron still shelter horrors from the fallen capital. Prince Arthas and the Scourge brought the human kingdom to ruin during the Third War. Now the Alliance and Horde venture across the surface to reclaim relics of the past or purge its threats. Capital City once held the crown of the Eastern Kingdoms. King Terenas Menethil II sat on its throne, and human civilization stood at its heart. When Prince Arthas returned from Northrend, he killed his father in the throne room and unleashed the undead upon the people.\n\nThe Forsaken now dwell in the catacombs below, in Undercity. Above them, the surface remains a forsaken twilight zone. Broken arches and weed-choked cobbles shelter feral beasts such as Witherfang and deranged remnants of the past. Restless dead are not the only danger in the ruined grounds. Apothecaries and sinister figures such as viktor the vile test new plagues in the shadows, while twisted experiments such as the abandoned roam freely.\n\nWrathful spirits such as rath'mael and cursed retainers such as The Baron haunt the collapsed halls, trapped in endless torment. They strike at any living or undead trespasser who crosses their path.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ruinsoflordaeron-6971ae7f6c7b6a1b5441/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.1322449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:scarlet"] = {
  hash = "f7370c",
  fullHash = "f31517",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery. In the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, Scarlet Monastery shelters the Scarlet Crusade behind fortified walls. Once a peaceful sanctuary and a center of learning for the Church of the Holy Light, it now holds four wings where zealots train, torture captives, and wage war on outsiders. Before the Third War, the Scarlet Monastery was a proud seat of Lordaeron's priesthood, devoted to learning and enlightenment. Then the Scourge devastated the land. The surviving crusaders cleared the halls of undead and spiders, and made the archives and courtyards a fortified garrison.\n\nGrief and fear hardened into suspicion. The Scarlet Crusade came to see every outsider as a carrier of the plague, living or undead, human or otherwise. In their eyes, such foes must be cleansed with fire. Beyond the Grand Vestibule lie four wings. In the Graveyard, fallen crusaders rise as undead abominations, while torturers force confessions from prisoners. The Library holds theological and historical works, guarded by renegade mage Arcanist Doan and his hounds.\n\nIn the Armory, weaponmasters such as Herod train recruits to fight as disciplined warriors. Deep within the stronghold lies the Cathedral, the Crusade's heart. There, its leaders direct their campaign across the blighted north.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_scarlet-100d576327259fa5717e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.1330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:searinggorge"] = {
  hash = "0dce1e",
  fullHash = "3d719e",
  text = "Searing Gorge. In Searing Gorge, the Dark Iron clan works a land once joined to the green Redridge Mountains. Ragnaros the Firelord scorched it when he was summoned over two centuries ago. Now the Thorium Brotherhood watches the clan's slave mines in the Cauldron. Before the War of the Three Hammers, Searing Gorge was a green, rolling part of the Redridge chain. That land was lost over two hundred and thirty years ago. Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan summoned Ragnaros from deep beneath the world, hoping to turn defeat into victory. The Firelord tore the earth apart, raised Blackrock Mountain and reduced the great Dark Iron city of Thaurissan to smoking ruins. Ash, sulfur and volcanic glass covered the northern valley. After their old capital fell, the Dark Iron dwarves seized the gorge for the materials their war machine needed. At the valley's heart, the Cauldron cuts deep into the stone as a vast open-pit excavation. In its lower reaches, the Slag Pit forms a harsh underground mining complex.\n\nDark Iron overseers and lumbering war golems force captured enemies into slave labor, hauling iron, mithril and rare Dark Iron ore from burning rock. Lava surges and roaming beasts threaten the miners at every turn. Thorium Point crowns the northern crags above the excavation. The Thorium Brotherhood holds this fortified outpost, after splitting from its kin in Blackrock Mountain. Its master craftsmen offer travelers neutral shelter in return for undermining Dark Iron operations or bringing rare smelting components.\n\nAccess to the gorge remains tightly controlled. The Alliance bars Stonewrought Pass toward Loch Modan with heavy iron gates, while Horde expeditions from nearby Kargath have cut a narrow trail through the eastern badlands to scout the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_searinggorge-4d4c62da8d7bef16f135/1-0000.mp3", duration = 113.345306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:shadowfang"] = {
  hash = "f27519",
  fullHash = "014683",
  text = "Shadowfang Keep. In southern Silverpine, Shadowfang Keep shelters Archmage Arugal and the worgen he summoned. The old Silverlaine estate is overrun by feral beasts and haunted by its slaughtered defenders. Nearby Pyrewood Village remains bound to a horrifying curse. Shadowfang Keep once belonged to the Silverlaine family, with pious Commander Springvale charged to defend it. During the Third War, the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, and Dalaran's wizards fell in droves. Desperate to stem the dead, Archmage Arugal defied the Kirin Tor leadership and used the research of the wizard Ur.\n\nHe summoned worgen from beyond this world to turn the tide. The beasts savaged the undead, then slipped beyond mortal control and stormed southern Silverpine. Baron Silverlaine and his court were massacred, doomed to haunt the stone halls beside the monsters that overran their home. Guilt and madness drove Arugal behind the blood-stained battlements. He called the ravenous wolf-men his beloved children and gave the castle the name Shadowfang Keep. Lupine packs and shadowy hounds thrive in its stone roosts under trainers such as Wolf Master Nandos. Arugal's massive lupine beast, Fenrus the Devourer, watches over them.\n\nThe curse reached beyond the walls. Nearby Pyrewood Village remains trapped in a horrifying cycle: its people walk as men by day, then hunt as ravenous worgen beneath the moon. They are bound to the mad archmage on the heights above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_shadowfang-0c4be3246d613bcf97ec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 94.6938776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:shendralas"] = {
  hash = "9a7f5d",
  fullHash = "f231f2",
  text = "Shen'dralas. Centaur, outcasts, and quilboar contest Shen'dralas, south east of desolace. Ancient ruins rise among its crags, between Feralas and Mulgore. Shen'dralas lies in the heights south east of desolace, between Mulgore and Feralas. Its craggy passes and hidden enclaves shelter groups struggling to survive far from the great faction capitals. At Evenshade Overlook, some watch from secluded heights. At Outcast Hideaway, others seek refuge where they can be forgotten. Ancient ruins recall the highborne legacy behind Shen'dralas's name, yet the land is far from peaceful. Centaur feuds spill across the Magram Front, keeping old bloodshed alive along the borders. Bristleback quilboar hold fortified ground at Bristleback Retreat. Travelers find contested territory in the ravines. The valley of bones leads north-west toward Desolace and offers the main overland route into Shen'dralas. Sun and wind have bleached the passage. Its bones recall the violent struggles that still sweep through these crags.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_shendralas-9d609807efd321ff4253/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.2987755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:silithus"] = {
  hash = "e23931",
  fullHash = "f369fd",
  text = "Silithus. Silithus lies beneath spreading silithid swarms, watched by the Cenarion Circle. The desert holds the ancient ruins of Ahn'Qiraj, sealed behind the Scarab Wall a thousand years ago. Druids guard against the swarms and Twilight's Hammer cultists. You enter Silithus from Un'Goro Crater through a broad, rocky pass in its northwest corner. Jungle gives way to dunes, jagged stone, and towering hives. Water is scarce, and the wind carries the hum of insect wings across the sand. Near the desert's center, Cenarion Hold offers shelter to travelers, researchers, and warriors. The Cenarion Circle keeps this outpost as the only refuge in the hostile wastes. A thousand years ago, night elves and the great dragonflights fought desperately to drive back the Qiraji in the War of the Shifting Sands. The Qiraji were an insectoid empire born from the ancient Aqiri civilization.\n\nWhen victory proved beyond reach, the defenders sealed the swarms inside their stronghold with the Scarab Wall and enchanted Scarab Dais. The gates held, but silithids burrowed through tunnels below and formed vast colonies across the desert, including Hive'Ashi, Hive'Zora, and Hive'Regal. Twilight's Hammer has fortified several outposts in Silithus. Its cultists summon elemental terrors and worship the dark power beneath Ahn'Qiraj. Cenarion Circle observers watch the silithid colonies spread and keep guard against the ancient threat behind the Scarab Wall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_silithus-e6837ad1cbac7aa816c2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 93.8318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:silverpine"] = {
  hash = "0fe2eb",
  fullHash = "0fe2eb",
  text = "Silverpine Forest. Forsaken patrols hold Silverpine Forest, where rival forces threaten the woods along Lordaeron’s western coast. The Scourge’s plague scars the forest, and worgen, diseased gnolls, and hostile human enclaves endanger travelers. Silverpine Forest stretches between Tirisfal Glades and the sealed Greymane Wall. Once a lush wonder of Lordaeron, it has withered since the Third War. Ghostly fog drifts among dying, silver-barked pines, while ruined farmsteads lie abandoned to the wilderness.\n\nTirisfal Glades borders the forest to the north, and Lordamere Lake runs along its eastern shore. To the south, the Greymane Wall shuts Gilneas away from the chaos beyond. The Forsaken hold their central outpost at the Sepulcher, struggling to secure the woods from enemies on every side.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_silverpine-b26428f31080ba3ce40c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.3755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stockade"] = {
  hash = "187713",
  fullHash = "187713",
  text = "The Stockade. Violent rebellion has seized The Stockade beneath Stormwind's canal district near the Mage Quarter. Built as a high-security prison for cutthroats, political insurgents, and captured enemies of state, it now holds dangerous prisoners in anarchy. Warden Thelwater is driven back to the entrance and seeks help before the riot reaches the capital. Beneath Stormwind's canals, the Stockade has become a war zone. When the regular army marched to distant fronts, understaffed prison guards relied on inexperienced youths, including fourteen-year-old recruits. They could not keep order. The inmates launched a bloody, coordinated rebellion, tore their wool blankets into gang scarves, and cut down the remaining guards. Warden Thelwater barely holds the entrance while convicts control the cells, hallways, and holding blocks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stockade-311338565e959a3d2b0c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.5469388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stonetalon"] = {
  hash = "4a5f9d",
  fullHash = "9157dd",
  text = "Stonetalon Mountains. In Stonetalon Mountains, sacred heights face smoke, felled forests, and ruined earth. Night elves and tauren revere the range, while tensions simmer along its borders. Stonetalon Mountains rise from central Kalimdor beneath the Ill Winds. Moisture from the western seas brings sudden downpours, slicking the sandstone trails. Fierce gusts scour the lower valleys, blinding travelers with grit and sometimes hurling the careless from cliffs.\n\nHigher ridges bear evergreen forests around Stonetalon Peak. Night elves tend ancestral barrows and glades there. They also watch sacred wyvern and hippogryph roosts along Mirkfallon Lake. The Venture Company has broken the range's quiet with logging camps and strip mines in Windshear Crag. Aided by Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle, the deranged leper gnome, the goblins have felled old-growth timber and filled the skies with furnace smoke. Their industrial sludge fouls the mountain runoff.\n\nLocal shamans from Orgrimmar answered the mountain spirits' cries. Small Alliance camps nearby also resist the defilers and try to protect the highlands that remain. South lies the Charred Vale, once a blossoming wilderness and now an ash-covered wasteland. Rogue fire elementals, black dragonspawn, and harpies fill its ruins. During the Third War, the Horde and Alliance forged an uneasy truce in these peaks under the mysterious Oracle's guidance.\n\nRelations have since frayed. Renegade Grimtotem tauren hold seized encampments, while Darkspear trolls in Malaka'jin clash with Alliance forces pressing out from the Barrens and Ashenvale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stonetalon-fe22d11ca38c4847a44f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.124082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stormwind"] = {
  hash = "2527da",
  fullHash = "1fc246",
  text = "Stormwind City. White-walled Stormwind is the greatest haven of men, and the heart of the Alliance. Yet its king is lost. The crown now rests on a boy of ten. Stormwind is old. More than a thousand years ago, the descendants of the Arathi founded it. For long generations it flourished, a realm that needed no one's help. Then the Horde came through the Dark Portal. The southern continent was laid waste, and in the First War the city was razed.\n\nIts survivors followed Sir Anduin Lothar north, across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. When the Horde fell in the Second War, King Terenas Menethil II and the Alliance paid for Stormwind to rise again. The Stonemasons Guild raised it anew in polished white stone, with canals running through its boroughs. But the white stone hides a troubled house. The House of Nobles quarreled with the Stonemasons Guild over wages, and the quarrel left the kingdom crippled by debt. The guild's workers rose in riot. They were driven from the very city they had built.\n\nThen King Varian Wrynn vanished on his way to a summit. Lest panic spread, the crown passed to his son Prince Anduin Wrynn. He is ten years old. Highlord Bolvar Fordragon rules now as Regent and commander of the army. And at his side stands the royal advisor, Lady Katrana Prestor. With Lordaeron overrun by the Scourge, men look to Stormwind above every other city. The faithful gather at the Cathedral of Light. And in the Valley of Heroes, tall statues honor the Sons of Lothar. They passed beyond the Dark Portal, and were seen no more.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stormwind-71cbf75eb329b21b755c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 102.817959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:stranglethorn"] = {
  hash = "c999e7",
  fullHash = "91a09d",
  text = "Stranglethorn Vale. Trolls, goblins, pirates, and soldiers contest Stranglethorn Vale. South of Duskwood, the old Gurubashi heartland has become a maze of ruins, beasts, and rival camps. Zul'Gurub once ruled the vale as the Gurubashi Empire's capital. That rule ended when radical priests summoned Hakkar the Soulflayer, and civil war tore through the region. The Zandalari crushed the blood god's uprising, but the empire never recovered. Now successor factions fight across the jungle. The Bloodscalp hold the northwest, while the Skullsplitter occupy the east. Both tribes slaughter outsiders as they fight to reclaim their ancestral home. Booty Bay fills the vale's southern tip with trade and sea traffic. The Steamwheedle Cartel carved out the port, and the Blackwater Raiders run it under Baron Revilgaz. Bloodsail Buccaneers anchored along the coast keep the port under constant threat. Farther inland, the Venture Company cuts timber and mines the hills for rare singing crystals. Rival powers meet across the vale's dangerous borderland. The Horde holds Grom'gol Base Camp on the western coast, and goblin zeppelins link it to Durotar and Tirisfal Glades. Alliance loyalists at the Rebel Camp hunt for the rogue Colonel Kurzen. In the thick jungle, famed hunter Hemet Nesingwary leads big-game expeditions. Wanderers drawn to his hunts face prowling panthers, enormous apes, and enemy soldiers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_stranglethorn-99d884b507bbdc1ebbb7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.3126531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:swampofsorrows"] = {
  hash = "283d7e",
  fullHash = "e59d43",
  text = "Swamp of Sorrows. Green dragonspawn guard the drowned Temple of Atal'Hakkar beneath the Pool of Tears. Between Deadwind Pass and the Blasted Lands, this salt-crusted mire holds dangerous wildlife and the Horde garrison at Stonard. The Alliance has no safe haven here. The Swamp of Sorrows was once the northern half of the vast Black Morass. After the Gurubashi Empire shattered, fanatical Atal'ai trolls fled into the mire and built the Temple of Atal'Hakkar. They meant to summon Hakkar the Soulflayer, their bloodthirsty loa. The Aspect Ysera sensed the danger and drowned the cursed pyramid beneath the Pool of Tears. Green dragonflight guardians led by Eranikus were set to watch over it.\n\nLater, the Dark Portal's cataclysm scorched the southern morass into the Blasted Lands. The northern fen took its sorrowful name. Stonard is the swamp's main foothold for settled folk. First established during the First War alongside Rockard and Kyross, both now ruined, it was rebuilt under Thrall as the Horde's southernmost staging ground in the Eastern Kingdoms.\n\nStrange wanderers cross the fen beyond its palisades. Draenei stranded on Azeroth grew homesick and mad, becoming feral Lost Ones. At the Harborage, a small enclave of lucid Broken exiles remains wary, yet willing to trade peacefully with travelers. Predators make every crossing of the Swamp of Sorrows dangerous. Deep bogs conceal lunging crocolisks, venomous spiders, prowling swamp jaguars, and bog beasts coated in marsh debris. Vicious murlocs crawl through coastal caverns and along the Misty Reed Strand.\n\nSalt-laden tides creep inland and crust rotten logs with brine. Farther east, aggressive elite dragonspawn guard the approaches to the Sunken Temple in Sorrowmurk. They attack anyone who strays near their vigil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_swampofsorrows-0e0809b6d5a64ec6be84/1-0000.mp3", duration = 121.182041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:tanaris"] = {
  hash = "f09fd8",
  fullHash = "9bf3c2",
  text = "Tanaris. In Tanaris, goblins trade beneath a fierce sun as dangers stir in the sands. Gadgetzan welcomes both the Alliance and the Horde, while Sandfury trolls, silithid, and ancient powers hold the dunes beyond. Tanaris lies beneath a fierce sun, and sand covers much of the land. Within Zul'Farrak's walls, the Sandfury trolls guard their isolated home. Hyenas, basilisks, and scorpids roam the desert floor. Tunnels wind beneath the hot earth. Close to the goblins' island homeland of Kezan, Tanaris has become a trading ground for the Steamwheedle Cartel. From walled Gadgetzan to the docks of steamwheedle port, the goblins welcome coin from the Alliance and the Horde alike. Bruisers keep the peace, yet Southsea Freebooters still harass ships and dig sites along the coast. Tanaris holds powers older than mortal empires. In the east, the Caverns of Time belong to Nozdormu and the bronze dragonflight. Their drakes sometimes ride the desert thermals.\n\nFarther south, massive stone gates mark the titan complex of Uldum. Silithid swarms burrow outward from nests such as the Gaping Chasm and the Noxious Lair.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_tanaris-e3b6646e6c5666b6c78f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.2065306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:teldrassil"] = {
  hash = "9db73b",
  fullHash = "86df2e",
  text = "Teldrassil. Off Kalimdor's northwest shore rises Teldrassil, the World Tree, and in its crown lies Darnassus. The druids raised it after the Third War. Yet its growing is troubled. Vast are the limbs of Teldrassil, and far beneath them lies the Veiled Sea. Upon its boughs are hills and ancient glades, and misty lakes. Its rivers are fed by the morning dew. At the western edge stands Darnassus, city of the kaldorei.\n\nFar below, at the foot of the trunk, lies Rut'theran Village. It is the island's harbor. And a gateway of enchantment joins it to the crown. In the Third War, Nordrassil was sacrificed. Shan'do Malfurion Stormrage passed into the Emerald Dream. In his absence, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm went before the Circle of Ancients. He prevailed upon them to grow another tree in its place. For he hoped to win back the undying life the kaldorei had lost.\n\nBut Nozdormu and the Dragon Aspects judged it a work of pride. They withheld their blessing. And so Teldrassil grew unwarded. With it grew flaws not of nature.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_teldrassil-3869584e5336ae67981a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.2473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:thousandneedles"] = {
  hash = "595df6",
  fullHash = "59626c",
  text = "Thousand Needles. In southern Kalimdor, Thousand Needles stands over a dry canyon of sandstone pillars. Tauren hold the mesas, while gnomes and goblins race rocket cars across the Shimmering Flats. Before the Great Sundering, inland waterways fed a lush river canyon that ran toward the sea. The cataclysm shattered ancient Kalimdor, raised the riverbed above the water table, and changed the river's course. Dry stone and sandstone spires remain. Dozens of slender, cylindrical columns rise from the canyon floor like a forest turned to stone.\n\nTauren built villages atop the columns and joined the peaks with precarious rope bridges. They sought escape from hostile centaur marauders patrolling the shadow-draped floor below. Life atop the needle-like spires is far from peaceful. At Freewind Post, tauren loyal to the Horde face predatory wind serpents and wyverns from Highperch, and treacherous kin of the militant Grimtotem clan, entrenched at Darkcloud Pinnacle. Cliffwatcher Longhorn directs the plateau's defense. He seeks bold fighters to strike Grimtotem agitators and secure the fragile bridges between peaks.\n\nTo the north, enormous mechanical winches at the Great Lift carry travelers down from the southern Barrens. Crossing the canyon floor remains a deadly gamble. To the southeast, the canyon walls open onto the Shimmering Flats, an enormous dried-up salt pan below sea level. Gnomes and goblins keep an uneasy neutrality around the Mirage Raceway in this harsh, blinding expanse. Rival engineers Pozzik and Fizzle Brassbolts race experimental rocket cars across the hard salt, each seeking to prove their race's technological superiority.\n\nThe pit stop draws scoundrels, swindlers like Kravel Koalbeard, and hunters pursuing the hardy scorpions, turtles, and basilisks that roam the searing basin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_thousandneedles-b6a7f6905b2bb73f119f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 119.457959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:thunderbluff"] = {
  hash = "d0a075",
  fullHash = "f4693b",
  text = "Thunder Bluff. High above the grasslands of Mulgore, Thunder Bluff rises on windswept red mesas. It is the capital of the tauren, and the heart of their spirit, their culture and their trade. Swaying rope bridges and wooden lifts join its heights. Safe from the centaur marauders, it is a place of peace for travelers from all over Kalimdor. For countless generations, the tauren wandered Kalimdor, fighting the centaur for their very survival. That age ended in the Third War, when High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof joined forces with Warchief Thrall. Together the orcs and tauren broke the centaur's assaults, and won the pastures of Mulgore. Then Cairne led the allied tribes up onto these bastions of stone, and founded Thunder Bluff. It was the first lasting home the united tribes had known in centuries. The central bluffs hold the markets, the bank and the inn, and a towering roost for the wind riders. Three more mesas branch out across the abyss, each with its own calling. Scouts and trackers live on Hunter Rise. The lorekeepers live on Elder Rise, with the Cenarion Circle under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem.\n\nAnd Spirit Rise is given to the spirits. There dwell the shaman and the priests. Deep beneath them lie the caverns called the Pools of Vision.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_thunderbluff-f3a9eccfe6dc05e266f9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.964898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:tirisfal"] = {
  hash = "2f6067",
  fullHash = "8bc57b",
  text = "Tirisfal Glades. Tirisfal Glades, in the north of Lordaeron, is home to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and her Forsaken. Under blighted skies, fallen manors and dormant crypts surround their vast capital beneath the earth, the Undercity. The mindless Scourge hem them in, and so do the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade. Here the free-willed dead fight to survive. Tirisfal was once the fertile cradle of humanity, before the Lich King's plague withered its people. Now the sun rarely breaks through the gloom, over dying woods and cold coastal hills. Old farmsteads like the Agamand Mills still stand, but most of their masters lie in mass graves or walk as restless dead. Yet a dark resilience holds on in towns like Brill. And in Deathknell, the newly awakened Forsaken learn to endure their second life. Now Lady Sylvanas rules the region from beneath the Ruins of Lordaeron, yet her people are beset on every side. Scourge thralls still infest the hollows, the fields and the crumbling crypts, bound wholly to the Frozen Throne in Northrend. To the east, the Scarlet Crusade holds northern Tirisfal, from its stronghold at the Scarlet Monastery. Sworn to destroy every undead creature, its patrols wage constant guerrilla war on Forsaken scouts and travelers. Older than any human crown is the story of the Glades. Of old, high elven exiles sought to settle in these woods. But shadowy powers deep beneath the earth began to drive them mad, and they passed on into the north. Centuries later, the secret Council of Tirisfal chose these shadowed groves. Here it empowered its solitary Guardians, who shielded Azeroth from the demons.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_tirisfal-dbf123eec75c211520ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 103.993469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:undercity"] = {
  hash = "57ce7b",
  fullHash = "a26602",
  text = "Undercity. Beneath the shattered ruins of Lordaeron's capital lies the Undercity, the dark stronghold of the Forsaken. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner took it from the Scourge, and made it a sanctuary no enemy can breach. Today it is the seat of Forsaken power. And it is the Horde's great bastion, in the north of the Eastern Kingdoms. Long before the plague, the catacombs and sewers beneath the palace were already called the Undercity. Nobles lay entombed there, beside the poorest people of the realm. And there lay the kingdom's deepest prisons. Even Orgrim Doomhammer, the former Warchief, was held in them before he escaped.\n\nIn the Third War, Arthas Menethil murdered King Terenas, and laid waste to the capital. Then the prince set his undead servants to dig. They widened the old passages into a fortified seat of Scourge rule. But the Scourge never saw their dark capital finished. When the Lich King's power waned, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner broke free of his will, and took thousands of undead with her. She ambushed Arthas, and he fled back to Northrend. A vicious civil war followed, against the Scourge remnants, the dreadlords and the human resistance. Out of it, Sylvanas claimed the half-finished catacombs. She named her people the Forsaken, and oversaw years of labor on the sprawling city. The city spreads in rings around the Trade Quarter, and great lifts join it to the palace ruins above. Around it lie the other quarters, and among them is the toxic Apothecarium. There the Royal Apothecary Society brews virulent plagues, beside canals of green slime. Deeper still, the Banshee Queen holds court in the Royal Quarter, with her dreadlord commander Varimathras. Legions of towering abominations stand guard.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_undercity-b5f544d52fd60d5e8f28/1-0000.mp3", duration = 114.181224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:ungoro"] = {
  hash = "aead63",
  fullHash = "e6fef4",
  text = "Un'Goro Crater. Un'Goro Crater draws travelers into a lush basin between the deserts of Tanaris and Silithus. Dinosaurs roam beneath a central volcano, among living elementals and ancient Titan relics. Colored power crystals lie across the basin, though their origins remain a mystery. Ancient night elf records of the War of the Shifting Sands say the Qiraji were turned back at Un'Goro Crater's edges. In their tongue, they called this place the 'God Lands.' Scholars believe the Titans may have used the caldera as a testing ground or dwelling while shaping Kalimdor. Stone pylons rise along the ridges and hum with energy. Blue, red, green, and yellow crystals emerge from the primeval earth. Un'Goro has no cities, towns, or native humanoid societies. Life thrives here in a wild, primeval state. Devilsaurs stalk the flats of Terror Run, bloodpetals lash out in the marshlands, and oozes creep across the tar pits of Lakkari. At the crater's heart, Fire Plume Ridge is an active volcano filled with hostile fire elementals led by Blazerunner. Explorers and skinners seek its natural wealth, including devilsaur hides, if they can survive the food chain. Marshal's Refuge, an elevated camp in the northern cliffs, holds nearly all of Un'Goro's settled presence. Williden Marshal led scientists, naturalists, and fortune seekers here to catalogue the basin's treasures.\n\nMonstrous wildlife battered the expedition and drove its members to high ground. The refuge remains an embattled staging ground for travelers studying the mysterious power crystals, mapping volcanic activity, and watching silithid swarms encroach through the southern ridges.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_ungoro-52f395144703d206cfe2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 106.631837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:wailing"] = {
  hash = "6c8555",
  fullHash = "d24af8",
  text = "Wailing Caverns. Beneath the Barrens' Lushwater Oasis, trapped steam whistles through the Wailing Caverns. Springs and fissures once promised to revive Kalimdor's sun-bleached savanna. Now the caverns hold poisoned waters, twisted wildlife, and fallen disciples caught in an unnatural nightmare. The night elf druid Naralex found a vast system of underground waters and sought to restore the Barrens. Through a conduit into the Emerald Dream, he meant to draw pure primal life through the springs and turn the parched savanna into a thriving paradise.\n\nDeep in the caverns, Naralex entered communion with the Dream, anchored by his loyal acolytes. But his sleeping mind touched the Emerald Nightmare. Corruption seized him, and his conduit became a valve pouring venom and madness into the waters. As the Nightmare's dark energy spread from Naralex's helpless form, his students' minds fell to madness. They forsook the tranquil wisdom of the Cenarion Circle and became the fanatical Druids of the Fang.\n\nTheir leaders, Lady Anacondra, Lord Cobrahn, Lord Pythas, and Lord Serpentis, take reptilian forms. They embrace the caverns' venomous mutation instead of fighting it, and shield their sleeping master from outside interference. They believe this twisted growth is nature's true evolution.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_wailing-bd10881b501b0c0ac0f3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.48, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:westernplaguelands"] = {
  hash = "4bb9e5",
  fullHash = "ebdb75",
  text = "Western Plaguelands. Toxic mist and undead legions choke the Western Plaguelands, where rival factions fight for ground. Once northern Lordaeron’s breadbasket, its farms and towns fell to Kel'Thuzad’s cauldrons, though life still clings to the soil. The Argent Dawn, Alliance, Forsaken, and Scarlet Crusade wage desperate campaigns across its blighted fields. Before the Third War, farms spread east from Tirisfal Glades toward Darrowmere Lake. Hearthglen governed the north from Mardenholde Keep, while Andorhal stored and shipped grain across the kingdom. So Kel'Thuzad made the region his primary target. At Andorhal, the Cult of the Damned put the Plague of Undeath into grain shipments, turning Lordaeron's food supply into its doom. The Scourge raised four great plague cauldrons to poison the soil. They stand at Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering. Monstrous Cauldron Lords tend the vats and fill the air with toxic fog. Trees twist with swollen growths, and blighted wildlife rampages through the brush. Beneath Caer Darrow on Darrowmere Lake, Darkmaster Gandling trains necromancers at Scholomance, adding to the undead threat. The Scourge holds much of the region, yet other factions keep footholds. In the north, the Scarlet Crusade controls Hearthglen and purges whatever it deems tainted. Alliance forces gather at Chillwind Camp in the south, while the Forsaken defend their borders from the Bulwark. The neutral Argent Dawn directs the fight against the cauldrons, aids champions of both factions, and guards Uther's Tomb, the region's last truly sanctified ground.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_westernplaguelands-03ae2a13eaed917e345d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.459592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:westfall"] = {
  hash = "343283",
  fullHash = "a1b2c9",
  text = "Westfall. Westfall's farms lie exposed, and the Defias Brotherhood holds much of the countryside. Once Stormwind's breadbasket, the territory now struggles without crown support or military garrisons. Westfall's pastures and wheat fields once fed the Kingdom of Stormwind. After the Second War, the kingdom's finances ran thin, and its leaders neglected the territory. Stormwind sent resources elsewhere and let its garrisons go. The land fell fallow.\n\nNow barren pastures, harsh coastal winds, rogue harvest golems, and Riverpaw gnolls make survival hard for the few homesteaders who remain. The Defias Brotherhood's arrival hastened Westfall's collapse. The bandits wear red masks and follow a leader whose name no one in the countryside knows. They made Westfall their stronghold, burning farmers' fields, salting the soil, and taking abandoned mines.\n\nFrom Moonbrook, a ghost town, the Brotherhood rules most of the countryside in all but name. The House of Nobles has ignored Westfall's plight, so local people have taken up their own defense. At Sentinel Hill, Gryan Stoutmantle formed the People's Militia, a volunteer force holding the line against gnolls and bandits. Stormwind sends no formal soldiers or supplies.\n\nThe Militia depends on wandering adventurers to push back the Defias and protect the farms that remain.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_westfall-8e446702ddbe6d0db5c1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.3461224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:wetlands"] = {
  hash = "5a7384",
  fullHash = "4385a6",
  text = "Wetlands. Fog and fighting shape life in Wetlands, between Khaz Modan and Baradin Bay. Dwarven wars and orc invasions scarred this marshland between Ironforge and Lordaeron. Menethil Harbor keeps the Alliance connected to distant shores as Dragonmaw orcs, murlocs, and gnolls threaten the land. Below the Stonewrought Dam, cold marshland lies under gray skies and heavy fog. Once, thriving dwarven settlements stood here. Now sunken stone ruins, sodden bogs, and treacherous mires fill the land. You can come north from Loch Modan through the narrow pass at Dun Algaz, or cross the arches of the Thandol Span from the Arathi Highlands. Grim Batol looms over the eastern swamp. The Wildhammer dwarves once held its halls, but the War of the Three Hammers left the stronghold defiled. During the Second War, the Dragonmaw clan made it their redoubt. Then the red dragonflight broke free of orcish captivity and brought fiery vengeance upon the orcs, laying waste to the slopes.\n\nRed wyrmkin guard the Dragonmaw Gates. Feral Dragonmaw survivors live in scattered hill camps and harass travelers across the swamp. Menethil Harbor rims Baradin Bay, carrying Alliance trade and supplies through Khaz Modan. The port honors King Terenas Menethil II and links the Eastern Kingdoms with Kalimdor. Ships sail to Theramore Isle and, by way of Southshore, to Auberdine. Dwarven-built defenses protect the harbor, but coastal murlocs, swamp creatures, and raiders threaten vessels in the rocky channels. War reaches the Wetlands from several directions. In the north, Dark Iron dwarves led by the warlock Balgaras the Foul sabotaged the Thandol Span and seized Dun Modr. Overland supply trains were cut off. Mosshide gnolls roam the central fens and threaten even Rethiel the Greenwarden, an ancient protector.\n\nRogue orc warbands, Dark Iron infiltrators, and cursed shipwrecks keep the swamp dangerous. Its defenders must stay alert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_wetlands-42d8c8ed9e1bfab05606/1-0000.mp3", duration = 126.328163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:winterspring"] = {
  hash = "356040",
  fullHash = "35d713",
  text = "Winterspring. Snowstorms sweep Winterspring, where old dangers still shape life. High on Mount Hyjal's northeastern slopes, the valley lies above Moonglade and Felwood. Ancient Highborne ruins and fierce predators mark the land, while Everlook stands at its heart. Blue dragons shelter here, and demonic corruption grips the southern heights. Snowstorms cross Winterspring's high forests, bluffs, and frozen lakes. Far below the summit, the valley rises above the surrounding wilds under cold mountain air. Vast roots of Nordrassil, the World Tree, arch through the drifts, twisted in the struggles of the Third War. Northern Kalimdor is mostly warm and thick with trees, but these alpine woods shelter frostsabers, chimaeras, yetis, and winter bears. Ancient passages through Timbermaw Hold link Winterspring with Felwood and Moonglade. Uncorrupted furbolgs guard the way and scrutinize travelers who pass through. At the valley's center, goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel run Everlook, a trading town open to both Alliance and Horde. Its trade, engineering, and warm fires offer shelter from the mountain cold. Kel'Theril's ruined pillars rise beside a frozen lake, haunted by the Highborne who once lived there. Reckless sorcerers shattered the cursed Crystal of Zin-Malor, and madness claimed them. Farther south, Mazthoril's caves hold the blue dragonflight's Kalimdor stronghold. Haleh, its matron protector, watches over Malygos's brood as they guard arcane currents and face nearby threats. Darkwhisper Gorge cuts through the calm at Winterspring's southern edge. Demonic forces left from the Third War crawl through its jagged canyons, blocking the high approaches to Mount Hyjal's summit. Winterfall furbolgs have also fallen to foul corruption. Independent researchers such as Donova Snowden investigate the spreading taint as it threatens the valley's wildlife.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_winterspring-cab66f59664ca887efd3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 121.338776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["zone:zephras"] = {
  hash = "665598",
  fullHash = "2bd193",
  text = "Zephras Isle. On Zephras Isle, the Skyborne face a homeland slowly coming apart. Air elementals once shared the island with them, but those guardians vanished and the magic that holds the land aloft is fading. Refugees arrive from Anvilas and Siroccas as those islands crumble. In skywall, the elemental plane of air, the Skyborne have lived on Zephras Isle for generations. Native air elementals once helped preserve the magical currents that keep the island aloft. Several centuries ago, those elementals vanished. Without their presence, the enchantments beneath the land have slowly weakened, and Zephras has begun to fracture. Anvilas and Siroccas once drifted through skywall alongside Zephras, and the Skyborne made homes on all three islands. When the elementals vanished, communication between the islands ended. Now refugees from the crumbling neighbors have reached Zephras seeking safety. They report that the land beneath them on Anvilas and Siroccas is breaking apart. As Zephras decays, the Skyborne look beyond skywall toward Azeroth. Airships now set out from the skydocks of valanaar to make rare links with the mortal realm. Their voyages reach the outskirts of Dalaran in the alterac mountains and skywatcher plateau in Mulgore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_zone_zephras-0f344ffa1b85d6a47a4e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.684898, start = 0},
  },
}
